text_mt:
You are the purifying strainer of the Vasu, having a hundred streams; you are the purifying strainer of the Vasus, having a thousand streams.
Free from disease, I unite you with offspring, you who multiply through the increase of wealth.
Swelling with sweetness, rich in ghee, living, may we sit near you living!
You are the cauldron of Mātariśvan;
you are the sky;
you are the earth;
all-sustaining by your highest abode.
You are unbent;
do not stumble!
She is all life;
she is all-embracing;
she is all-sustaining.
The drop is offered;
the droplet is offered;
to Agni, to the high firmament, svāhā! to Heaven and Earth!
Unite, O righteous ones, with the wave, most sweet,
cheering for the winning of wealth!
I curdle you as the portion of Indra with soma.
You are not cast down for Viṣṇu.
O Viṣṇu, protect the oblation!
O Waters, keep watch!

Shaken off is the demon;
shaken off is the enemy.
You are the skin of Aditi;
may the earth recognise you!
You are the mortar, born of the mountain;
may the skin of Aditi recognise you!
Make the earth firm!
You are the mortar, daughter of the mountain;
may the mountain-born recognise you!
Make heaven firm!
You are the prop of heaven.
You are grain;
delight the gods!
Delight the sacrifice!
Delight the lord of the sacrifice!
Delight me, the leader of the sacrifice!
For out-breath [I take] you;
for diffused breath [I take] you;
for in-breath [I take] you;
for a long course, for life [I take] you.
May the divine Savitṛ of golden hands receive you!
With an unblinking eye I look upon you, for the increase of wealth, for good offspring.

Burnt up is the demon;
burnt up is the enemy.
Drive away, O Agni, the flesh-consuming fire;
banish the consumer of raw flesh!
Convey hither the place of sacrifice to the gods!
You are firm;
make the earth firm!
Give life;
give breath;
gather his kinsmen around this sacrificer!
You are the maintainer;
make the intermediate space firm!
Give sight;
give hearing;
gather his kinsmen around this sacrificer!
You are the supporter;
make heaven firm!
Give vigor;
give strength;
gather his kinsmen around this sacrificer!
You are order;
make the directions firm!
Give wealth;
give prosperity;
gather his kinsmen around this sacrificer!
You are the restrainer;
make the quarters firm!
Give form;
give color;
gather his kinsmen around this sacrificer!
Piled up are you, piled around, the kinsmen of the sacrificer;
of the Bhṛgus […]
The potsherds that the wise arrange at the hot cauldron,
under the rule of Pūṣan, may Indra and Vāyu release them.

You are the milk of Pṛśni;
from that of yours which is unexhausted and swelling, I pour out what is swelling.
Praying for goodwill, offspring, fortune, and wealth, / obedient to Agni, having girded myself for righteous action—
O Agni, lord of the house, invite me!
O wives of the gods, invite me!
May I sit near you like Aditi with good sons, like Indrāṇī not a widow!
For food [I take] you!
With an unbeguiled eye I gaze upon you for the increase of wealth, for good offspring!
For strength [I take] you!
You are the tongue of Agni, a good fortress for the gods.
For every abode, for every sacrificial formula [I sprinkle you].
You are the Vaiśvānara oblation, with heightened energy, true in might;
might by name are you;
overcome the enemy, overcome those who fight [us]!
You are of thousandfold power; having wiped it, [I make you ready].
You are the clarified butter of the clarified butter, the oblation of the oblation;
you are besprinkled with truth;
with truth I sprinkle you.
You are brilliance;
you are brightness;
you are light.
You are the dear abode of the gods, the unassailable place of sacrifice to the gods.
For the divine beings worthy of sacrifice I take you.
Singed is the demon;
singed is the enemy.
You are unblunted, destroying rivals;
may I not sever life and breath!
Speedy one, I wipe you, the speedy [spoon], for gaining speed;
may I not sever sight and hearing!
Speedy one, I wipe you, the speedy [spoon], for gaining speed;
may I not sever offspring and womb!
Speedy one, [I wipe] you for gaining speed;
may I not sever the cattle-stall of the sacrificer and the increase of wealth!
Speedy one, I wipe you, the speedy [spoon], for gaining speed.

O divine waters moving in front, lead this sacrifice forward;
promote the lord of the sacrifice!
Indra chose you in the contest with Vṛtra;
you chose Indra in the contest with Vṛtra.
You are sprinkled.
You are the black [antelope], dwelling in the burrow;
acceptable to Agni I sprinkle you.
You are the altar;
acceptable to the sacrificial grass I sprinkle you.
You are the sacrificial grass;
acceptable to the offering spoons I sprinkle you.
You are the portions of the ancestors;
with strengthening food go to the earth!
You are the topknot of Viṣṇu;
soft as wool, spread abroad, comfortable to sit upon for the gods!
You are the Gandharva Viśvāvasu;
[protect] from every danger!
You are the enclosure of the sacrificer, implored with refreshment;
you are the right arm of Indra;
you are the enclosure of the sacrificer, implored with refreshment;
may Mitra and Varuṇa enclose you with firm ordinance, implored with refreshment!
As an invoker of the banquet, O seer, we, radiant, would kindle you, / O Agni, great in the rite!
May Sūrya protect you from the east from any curse / on the highest vault of heaven!
You two are the arms of Savitṛ, the support of the divine races;
you are the seat of the Vasus, of the Rudras, of the Ādityas.
Come, Juhū, turned toward the ghee, heaven by origin;
Aditi with unbroken wings, beloved of the gods, sit in the dear seat with your dear abode!
Come, Upabhṛt, turned toward the ghee, intermediate space by origin;
Aditi with unbroken wings, beloved of the gods, sit in the dear seat with your dear abode!
Come, Dhruvā, turned toward the ghee, earth by origin;
Aditi with unbroken wings, beloved of the gods, sit in the dear seat with your dear abode!
You are the bull of the Śakvarī [verses], Vāyu by origin;
Aditi with unbroken wings, beloved of the gods, sit in the dear seat with your dear abode!
Firm they have sat in the lap of cosmic order, in the world of righteous action;
guard them, O Viṣṇu;
guard the sacrifice;
guard the lord of the sacrifice;
guard me, the leader of the sacrifice!

You are givers of moisture;
you are givers of life.
Moistening [waters], help me here!
May the waters moisten [my head] for life,
for long life, for splendor!
O plant, protect him!
O axe, do not harm him!
Seeking the gods, I shave these [hairs];
may I attain subsequent well-being.
May the waters of the desert be auspicious to us, may those of the marshy lands be auspicious to us; / may the waters of the ocean be auspicious to us, and may those of the wells be auspicious to us!
May the waters, our mothers, purify us; may those that purify with ghee cleanse me with ghee; / for the goddesses carry away all defilement; I emerge from them bright and purified.
You are the gift of Soma;
you are the body;
protect my body!
You are the milk of the great ones;
the sap of the waters and plants.
You are the bestower of splendor;
bestow splendor upon me!
You are the pupil of Mitra's eye;
through you I place hatred within, enmity within.
You are the bestower of sight;
bestow sight upon me!
May the lord of thought purify you;
may the lord of speech purify you;
may the divine Savitṛ purify you with an unslit sieve, with the rays of the sun!
With your sieve, O lord of the sieve, for whatever I purify [myself],
may I be capable of that!

You two are the two artistic forms of Ṛg and Sāman;
I lay hold of you two;
protect me until the conclusion!
You are shelter;
give me shelter!
Homage be to you;
do not harm me!
Sharpen this thought, insight, and skill of the one striving, O divine Varuṇa, / whereby we may cross over all misfortunes; may I mount a well-crossing boat.
I have mounted this good boat, unharmed, carrying across, / with a hundred oars, for well-being.
Heaven and Earth providing good protection, blameless, providing good shelter, Aditi who leads well— / may we mount the divine boat with good oars, guiltless, unswerving, for well-being.
You are strength, belonging to the Aṅgirases, soft as wool;
bestow strength in me;
do not harm me!
You are the knot of Soma's garment;
do not harm me!
You are the womb of Indra;
do not harm me!
For plowing with good grain [I take] you;
make the plowing yield good grain!
You are the shelter of Viṣṇu,
the shelter of the sacrificer;
give me shelter!
Homage be to you;
do not harm me!
Protect me from the flashing light of the stars!
O tree providing a good lap, upright, protect me until the conclusion!
Svāhā, the sacrifice from the mind,
svāhā, from heaven,
svāhā, from earth,
svāhā, from the broad atmosphere,
svāhā, from the wind I receive the sacrifice!

Prepare the vow [food]!
May I be capable of that!
We meditate on the divine, kindly thought for assistance,
granting splendor, all-nourishing, with good crossings, that it may be in our control.
Whatever gods are born of Manu, yoked by Manu, very skillful, whose father is Dakṣa,
may they protect us;
may they help us;
to them svāhā!
O Agni, wake well; we will rejoice well;
guard us heedfully; place us in front for awakening!
You, O Agni, are the vow-protector among gods and among mortals;
you are to be praised at sacrifices.
Pūṣan of acquisitions!
Soma of riches!
Give so much, O Soma;
bring more!
Divine Savitṛ, bestower of wealth from the good,
may the bestower not be deprived of fulfillment;
may I not be deprived of life, of splendor!
O divine waters, O child of the waters, whatever wave of yours is fit for oblation and full of vigor, may I not trample upon that!
Come with power!
With whatever body of yours is worthy of sacrifice, mount with that!
This is your seasonal womb from which, born, you shone;
knowing it, O Agni, mount it; then increase wealth for us!
We have come to this place of divine worship of the earth, where all the gods found pleasure;
crossing over with Ṛg and Sāman, with Yajus verses, may we rejoice in abundance of wealth with food!

This is your bright body, this your splendor; unite with it;
go to radiance!
You are swift, held by mind, pleasing to Viṣṇu;
at the instigation of you of true instigation, may I attain restraint of body, svāhā!
You are bright;
you are shining;
you are immortal;
you are the oblation for all the gods;
you are thought;
you are mind;
you are prayer;
you are the sacrificial gift;
you are worthy of sacrifice;
you are of the ruling class;
you are Aditi, double-headed;
as such, be well-facing-forward, well-facing-backward to me!
May Mitra bind you at the foot;
may Pūṣan protect [your] paths;
for Indra the overseer!
May your mother approve you, your father, your brother of the same womb;
[your] companion belonging to the same herd!
As such, O goddess, go to the god,
Soma for Indra!
May Rudra turn you back;
with well-being, as a friend of Soma, come back!
You are Vasvī;
you are Rudrā;
you are Aditi;
you are Ādityā;
you are Candrā;
you are Rudrā;
may Bṛhaspati delight you in benevolence;
Rudra with the Vasus has desired [you]!
On the head of the earth I besprinkle you, on the place of divine worship, on the footprint of Iḍā, full of ghee, svāhā!
Here I cut off the neck of the demon!
Here I cut off the neck of whoever, equal to us or unequal, acts as our adversary!
Rest with us;
with us be your riches;
[may] my riches [increase];
yours, yours are riches;
may I not be separated from abundance of wealth!
The divine goddess Urvaśī has looked [upon you];
with good seed, placing seed,
possessing Tvaṣṭṛ, may we attend you!
Do not steal away my life, nor I yours;
may I find a hero in your presence!

O divine Sūrya, we will buy Soma;
that we proclaim to you;
for all the gods arrange the seasons,
arrange the sacrificial gifts according to the Ṛṣis, according to the deities;
you belong to us;
your cup is the bright one!
Towards that divine Savitṛ within the two halves [of the world], possessing the insight of a sage,
I sing, of true instigation, bestower of jewels, towards [his] beloved thought.
Whose radiant form, his light, shone high at his instigation;
golden-handed, of good insight, he measured out the heaven with [his] fair shape.
For offspring [I buy] you;
may offspring breathe after you;
do you breathe after offspring!
I buy Soma from you, vigorous, milky, full of virility, of great price, resplendent.
I buy your bright [substance] with bright [substance], shining with shining, immortal with immortal,
the power which is of your cow;
with us are your shining [treasures]!
You are the body of ascetic toil, the form of Prajāpati,
thriving with thousandfold nourishment;
you are bought with the supreme beast;
with us is your kin!
Come to us as Mitra, establishing good friends;
enter Indra’s right thigh, willing him who is willing, gentle to him who is gentle!
Svāna, Bhrāj, Aṅghāri, Bambhāri, Hasta, Suhasta, Kṛśānu—
these are your Soma-purchasers;
guard them;
let them not harm you!
Up with life, with good life, up with the sap of plants,
up with the rain of Parjanya, up have I arisen following the immortals!
Traverse the broad atmosphere!
We have reached the path that goes to well-being, free from harm,
by which one avoids all enemies and finds wealth.
You are the skin of Aditi;
sit on the seat of Aditi!
The bull of all knowledge propped up the sky, he measured out the expanse of the earth;
he sat down over all worlds as sovereign ruler: all these are the ordinances of Varuṇa.
He stretched out the atmosphere among the trees, speed in racers, milk in the inviolable [cows],
Varuṇa set insight in hearts, Agni in clans, the sun in the sky, Soma on the rock.
I have mounted the eye of Sūrya, the pupil of Agni’s eye,
when you go with dappled steeds, shining, wise!

Come, you two reddish oxen, pressing against the yoke, not slaying heroes, tearless, impelled by the sacred word,
prolonging the life of the sacrifice;
go to the houses with well-being!
You are the yoke-pin;
injure him who injures!
Him who injures us, injure him;
and him whom we injure, injure him too!
You belong to Varuṇa;
you are the prop of Varuṇa!
Move forward, O lord of the world, to all dwellings;
may marauders not find you, may highwaymen not find [you];
may malicious wolves not find you!
Becoming a falcon, fly away
to the house of our sacrificer, prepared!
Homage to the eye of Mitra and Varuṇa! Serve that truth for the great god!
Praise the far-seeing, god-born banner, the son of heaven, Sūrya!
You belong to Varuṇa;
may Varuṇa prop you up!
You are the unfastener of Varuṇa’s prop;
released is the noose of Varuṇa!
You are Varuṇa, whose ordinances are steadfast, whose leaf is unbroken;
descend to the offspring!
You are Varuṇa’s seat of truth;
you are the skin of Aditi;
sit on the seat of Aditi!
Varuṇa, whose ordinances are steadfast, has sat down among the homesteads,
of good insight, for universal sovereignty.
You belong to Varuṇa;
for Varuṇa, you!

At the impulse of divine Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I take you;
you are a woman.
Here I cut off the neck of the Rakṣas.
Here I cut off the neck of him who, whether our equal or unequal, acts as our enemy.
You are Taptāyanī;
you are Vittāyanī.
May she protect me in distress;
may she protect [me] in trembling.
O Agni, O Aṅgiras, who are on this earth, come with the name of Āyu!
With that name of yours which is unassailed, unassailable, with that I place you.
O Agni, O Aṅgiras, who are on the second, who are on the third earth, come with the name of Āyu!
With that name of yours which is unassailed, unassailable, with that I place you.
Agni found the name 'Nabhas'.
You are a lioness;
you are a buffalo cow;
spread out for the gods;
be fit for the gods;
purify yourself for the gods;
adorn yourself for the gods!
May great Vibhrāj drink the Soma mead, bestowing unswerving life upon the lord of the sacrifice;
he who, impelled by the wind, protects by himself, fosters the creatures, shines manifold.
May the Vasus with Indra’s shout protect you from the east;
may the Fathers, swift as thought, protect you from the south;
may Pracetas with the Rudras protect you from the west;
may Viśvakarman with the Ādityas protect you from the north;
may Tvaṣṭṛ with the forms protect you from above!
You are a lioness overcoming rivals, svāhā!
You are a lioness winning the increase of wealth, svāhā!
You are a lioness winning good offspring, svāhā!
You are a lioness winning the Ādityas, svāhā!
You are a lioness; bring hither the gods for the sacrificer who serves the gods, svāhā!
For living beings, you!
You are all-lived;
prop the earth!
You dwell unshakably;
prop the atmosphere!
You dwell firmly;
prop the sky!
You are Agni's nest;
you are Agni's rubbish-filling;
the sacrifice has stood firm.
Like Manu we would set you down, like Manu we would kindle [you];
O Agni, like Manu, O Aṅgiras, sacrifice to the gods for him who serves the gods!

They harness the mind, and they harness the thoughts, the inspired singers of the inspired, great, wise one;
he alone, knowing the ordinances, arranges the priestly duties; great is the praise of divine Savitṛ.
Heard by the gods, make yourselves proclaimed among the gods!
This Viṣṇu strode out; thrice he planted his foot;
it is gathered up in its dust.
Rich in refreshment, rich in milk be, rich in good pasture, bringing glory to mankind;
you propped apart these two world-halves, O Viṣṇu; you supported the earth all around with pegs.
You belong to Viṣṇu;
may Viṣṇu prop you up!
Either from the sky, O Viṣṇu, or from the earth, or from the great, the wide atmosphere, O Viṣṇu,
fill your hands with many treasures, bestow [them] from the right and from the left!
Now indeed I proclaim the heroic deeds of Viṣṇu, who measured out the earthly spaces,
who propped the upper dwelling place, striding out thrice, the wide-striding one.
You are Viṣṇu's back;
you are Viṣṇu's forehead;
you two are Viṣṇu's corners of the mouth;
you are Viṣṇu's seam;
you are Viṣṇu's firm [point];
you belong to Viṣṇu;
for Viṣṇu, you!
For this is Viṣṇu praised for heroic power, like a dread beast, roaming, dwelling in the mountains,
in whose three wide strides all worlds abide.

You are Vibhū, the conveyer;
you are Vahni, the bearer of oblations;
you are Śvātra, the mindful;
you are Tutha, the all-knowing;
you are Uśij, the sage;
you are Aṅghāri, Bambhāri;
you are Avasyu, the attentive;
you are Śundhyu, the Mārjālīya;
you are the sovereign, Kṛśānu;
you are the one of the assembly, the Āstavya;
you are Nabha, the fast runner;
you are the unpolished, the conveyer of oblations;
you are Ṛtadhāman, the light of heaven;
you are the ocean, all-embracing;
you are Aja Ekapāt;
you are Ahi Budhnya;
you are Kavya, the bearer of the Kavya oblations.
With your terrible troop, protect me, O Agni!
Rescue me, O Agni!
Homage be to you!
Do not harm me!

Possessed of oblation are these waters; possessed of oblation is the divine sacrifice;
possessed of oblation, he seeks to win [them]; possessed of oblation let Sūrya be!
I seat you in the seat of Agni whose home is not fallen;
desirous of favor, for the benevolent one, establish favor!
You are the portions of Indra and Agni;
you are the portions of Mitra and Varuṇa;
you are the portions of all the gods;
the Vasatīvarī [waters] of Soma!
For the heart, you;
for the mind, you;
for heaven, you;
for Sūrya, you!
Raise this sacrifice aloft to heaven, among the gods; hold fast the priestly services!
O King Soma, descend to all creatures;
may all creatures descend to you!
May Agni hear my call along with the kindling stick; may the waters and the divine vessels hear!
Hear, O pressing stones, now the sacrifice of the wise one; may the god Savitṛ hear my call!
O divine waters, son of the waters, the wave that is fit for oblation, powerful, most intoxicating—that [wave] give you to the gods, among the gods, to the drinkers of pure [Soma], whose portion you are, svāhā!
You are furrow-drawn.
For the non-exhaustion of the ocean, I draw you up.
May Varuṇa, Mitra, Agni unite you; Indra, Bṛhaspati unite [you];
Tvaṣṭṛ, Viṣṇu, rejoicing together with offspring, may he bestow wealth upon the sacrificer!
Yoke-pin by yoke-pin, may they fit according to the yokes!
Whom in battles, O Agni, you protect, the mortal whom in contests you impel,
he obtains perpetual refreshments. Svāhā.
You are the foot-washing [waters] of the Vasus, the Rudras, the Ādityas.
O Vasus, Rudras, Ādityas, these are your foot-washing [waters];
you follow the ocean standing in the Gandharva, praised on the course of the wind.
Fair one, in your sight may I obtain all seed; in your sight, fair one!

You are the downward grasps, heard by the gods, bright, bringing brightness!
Satisfy my life;
satisfy my breath;
satisfy my inter-breath;
satisfy my down-breath;
satisfy my eye;
satisfy my ear;
satisfy my speech;
satisfy my self;
satisfy my offspring;
satisfy my houses;
satisfy my cattle;
satisfy my troops!
Satisfied, satisfy me;
pleased, please me;
may they not thirst for me!
At the impulse of the god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I take you.
You are a pressing stone, performing the sacrifice for the gods.
Make this sacrifice aloft with the highest rim;
most well pressed for Indra;
sweet, full of milk;
for Indra, slayer of enemies, [I take] you;
for Indra, bestower of strength, [I take] you;
for Indra, accompanied by the Vasus, [I take] you;
for Indra, accompanied by the Rudras, [I take] you;
for Indra, accompanied by the Ādityas, [I take] you;
for Agni, bestower of the increase of wealth, [I take] you;
for Viṣṇu, you;
for the falcon, bearer of Soma, you;
for Viṣṇu, you!
What light of yours, O Soma, is in heaven,
what on earth, what in the wide intermediate space,
with that make wide space for this lord of sacrifice, for wealth!
Speak above for the sustainer!
You are invigorating, overcoming Vṛtra, approved by generosity, wives of the immortal;
as divine ones, having made this sacrifice among the gods, invited, drink of the Soma!
Do not fear,
do not tremble!
O two firm vessels, be firm;
bestow vigor;
bestow vigor in us!
May I not harm you two;
may you two not harm us!
East, west, north, downward—may those quarters flow to you!
Mother, sound forth;
may you find the enemy!
Thus the third sthānaka called Jyotirika in the Iṭhimikā of the Caraka recension of the glorious Yajurveda in the Kāṭhaka is complete.

Come, O Vāyu, drinker of the pure [Soma], to us; a thousand are your teams, all-bounteous one!
To you is offered the exhilarating draught, of which, O god, you possess the first drinking!
You are taken with a support;
for Vāyu, you!
O Indra and Vāyu, these [drops] are pressed; come toward them with delights!
For the drops desire you both!
You are taken with a support;
for Vāyu, for Indra and Vāyu, you!
This is your womb;
for the harmonious pair, you!
This Soma is pressed for you two, O Mitra and Varuṇa, who increase truth;
hear this invocation of mine here!
You are taken with a support;
for Mitra and Varuṇa, you!
This is your womb;
for the seekers of truth, you!
Your whip, O Aśvins, sweet and full of pleasantness,
with that sprinkle the sacrifice!
You are taken with a support;
for the Aśvins, you!
This is your womb;
for the sweet pair, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Vāyu, for Indra and Vāyu, you!
This is your womb;
in the wide stride of Viṣṇu!
You are taken with a support;
for Mitra and Varuṇa, you!
This is your womb;
in the wide stride of Viṣṇu!
You are taken with a support;
for the Aśvins, you!
This is your womb;
in the wide stride of Viṣṇu!
Guard that!
May they not harm you!
May the evil-eyed one not gaze down upon you!

This Vena, born of Pṛśni, drove forth the shining afterbirths in the measure of space;
at the confluence of the waters, of the sun, the inspired poets lick him with songs like a calf.
You are taken with a support;
for Indra, beloved of Śaṇḍa, I take you!
This is your womb;
for heroism, you!
Him in the ancient way, in the former way, in every way, now, the highest state, sitting on the sacrificial grass, winning the sun,
by song you milk the turned-toward enclosure, [singing] which you conquer, [and] among which you grow!
You are taken with a support;
for Sūrya, beloved of Marka, I take you!
This is your womb;
for offspring, you!

You gods who are eleven in heaven, who are eleven upon earth,
who are eleven dwelling in the waters with majesty, O gods, enjoy this sacrifice!
You are taken with a support, belonging to the Āgrāyaṇa;
enliven the sacrifice; enliven the lord of the sacrifice!
Protect the pressings;
may Viṣṇu protect you;
protect the people with your power!
This is your womb;
for all the gods, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Indra possessed of greatness, possessed of vigor, worthy of praise;
for that great vigor of yours, O Indra, [I take] you;
for Viṣṇu, you!
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
The head of heaven, the messenger of earth, Vaiśvānara, Agni born in truth,
the sage, the sovereign king, guest of men—let the gods generate him as a vessel in the mouth!
You are taken with a support, belonging to Vaiśvānara;
you are firm, a firm resting place of the firm, the firmest of the unshakeable, the most unshakeable resting place!
This is your womb;
for Vaiśvānara, you!

You are taken with a support;
for Mitra and Varuṇa, you!
For the recitations I take you, worthy of recitation!
This is your womb;
for Mitra and Varuṇa, you!
You are a renewed oblation;
for the gods, I mix you, pleasing the gods, for the life of the sacrifice!
You are taken with a support;
for Indra, you!
For the recitations I take you, worthy of recitation!
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
You are a renewed oblation;
for the gods, I mix you, pleasing the gods, for the life of the sacrifice!
You are taken with a support;
for Indra and Agni, you!
For the recitations I take you, worthy of recitation!
This is your womb;
for Indra and Agni, you!
For the gods, I mix you, pleasing the gods, for the life of the sacrifice!
You are taken with a support;
for Indra, you!
For Indra, you!
For the recitations I take you, worthy of recitation!
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
For the gods, I mix you, pleasing the gods, for the life of the sacrifice!
You are taken with a support;
for Indra and Varuṇa, you!
For the recitations I take you, worthy of recitation!
This is your womb;
for Indra and Varuṇa, you!
You are a renewed oblation;
for the gods, I mix you, pleasing the gods, for the life of the sacrifice!
You are taken with a support;
for Indra and Bṛhaspati, you!
For the recitations I take you, worthy of recitation!
This is your womb;
for Indra and Bṛhaspati, you!
You are a renewed oblation;
for the gods, I mix you, pleasing the gods, for the life of the sacrifice!
You are taken with a support;
for Indra and Viṣṇu, you!
For the recitations I take you, worthy of recitation!
This is your womb;
for Indra and Viṣṇu, you!
You are a renewed oblation;
for the gods, I mix you, pleasing the gods, for the life of the sacrifice!

You are taken with a support;
for Madhu, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Mādhava, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Śukra, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Śuci, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Nabhas, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Nabhasya, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Iṣa, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Ūrja, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Sahas, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Sahasya, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Tapas, you!
You are taken with a support;
for Tapasya, you!
O Indra and Agni, come to the pressed [Soma], desirable like the misty sky, with our songs,
impelled by our thought, drink of this!
You are taken with a support;
for Indra and Agni, you!
This is your womb;
for Indra and Agni, you!
O protectors, supporters of men, all you gods, come,
generous ones, to the pressed [Soma] of the worshiper!
You are taken with a support;
for all the gods, you!
This is your womb;
for all the gods, you!

You were born fierce for swift strength, charming, most mighty, of boundless pride;
the Maruts magnified Indra even then, when the mother, most wealthy, bore the hero.
You are taken with a support;
for Indra accompanied by the Maruts!
This is your womb;
for Indra accompanied by the Maruts!
O Indra accompanied by the Maruts, drink Soma here, as you drank of the pressed [juice] at the Śāryāta [sacrifice];
under your guidance, O hero, in your protection, the well-sacrificing poets seek to win [favor].
You are taken with a support;
for Indra accompanied by the Maruts!
This is your womb;
for Indra accompanied by the Maruts!
Indra accompanied by the Maruts, the bull, drink Soma according to your will for battle, for exhilaration;
pour into your belly the wave of sweetness; you are the ancient king of the pressed [juices]!
You are taken with a support;
for Indra accompanied by the Maruts!
This is your womb;
for Indra accompanied by the Maruts!
Indra accompanied by the Maruts, the bull, the growing one, who has no niggardly foe, the heavenly ruler,
all-conquering, fierce, the giver of strength—him we would call here for modern aid!
You are taken with a support;
for Indra accompanied by the Maruts!
This is your womb;
for Indra accompanied by the Maruts!
Great is Indra, who by his might, like Parjanya possessed of rain,
has grown through the praise-songs of Vatsa.
You are taken with a support;
for Great Indra, you!
This is your womb;
for Great Indra, you!
Great is Indra, manly, filling the people, and double-bulwarked, unyielding in strengths;
he has grown toward us for heroic deeds; wide, broad, well-made has he become through the makers.
You are taken with a support;
for Great Indra, you!
This is your womb;
for Great Indra, you!

Never are you barren, nor, O Indra, do you neglect the worshiper;
ever closer, O generous one, ever more is the gift of the god poured out!
You are taken with a support;
for the Ādityas, you!
Never are you heedless; you watch over both races;
O fourth Āditya, your pressed juice is full of vitality, the immortal [abode] you have assumed in heaven.
You are taken with a support;
for the Ādityas, you!
The sacrifice goes to meet the favor of the gods; O Ādityas, be gracious!
May your good will turn hitherward toward us, which shall find wider room even out of distress!
You are taken with a support;
for the Ādityas, you!
Shine forth, O Āditya, this is your Soma-drink; rejoice in it!
With that which is heavenly rain, I mix you!
With unbeguiled, benevolent guardians, O Savitṛ, guard our household today!
Golden-tongued, for newer welfare, protect [us]; may no speaker of evil have power over us!
You are taken with a support;
for the god Savitṛ, [you]!
You are taken with a support;
you are well-sheltering, well-founded;
homage to the great sprinkler!
This is your womb;
for all the gods, you!

You are taken with a support.
May I satisfy the cup of you, O drop, pressed by Bṛhaspati, possessing vital power, accompanied by your wife!
O Agni, accompanied by your wife, united with Tvaṣṭṛ, drink the Soma!
You are taken with a support.
You are the bay-colored one, belonging to the yoker of the bay steeds;
mounting the two bays;
for the two bays, you!
Of you, O divine Soma, whose praise-hymn is chanted, whose recitation is recited, whose sacrificial formula is offered,
may I satisfy the cup belonging to the one who has the bay steeds!
You two are the bays; you are the parched grains belonging to the one who has the bay steeds, with the Soma of Indra.
O Agni, you purify our life-spans; spur forth strength and nourishment for us;
drive misfortune far away!
You are taken with a support;
for Agni, endowed with splendor, [I take] you!
This is your womb;
for Agni, endowed with splendor!
That strength of his flared up, when Indra rolled together both
heaven and earth like a hide.
You are taken with a support;
for Indra, endowed with vigor, [I take] you!
This is your womb;
for Indra, endowed with vigor!
His banners have appeared, his rays across the peoples,
shining like fires.
You are taken with a support;
for the Sun, endowed with brilliance, [I take] you!
This is your womb;
for the Sun, endowed with brilliance!
The two bay steeds bear Indra of irresistible power,
to the praises of the ṛṣis and to the sacrifice of mortals.
You are taken with a support;
for Indra, possessor of the bay steeds, [I take] you!
This is your womb;
for Indra, possessor of the bay steeds!

For King Varuṇa made a broad path for the Sun to follow;
he made feet to step where there is no foot; and he turns away whatever pierces the heart.
A hundred are your physicians, O king, a thousand; may your good will be broad and deep;
drive Nirṛti far away; loosen from us whatever sin has been committed!
The face of Agni entered the waters, the Son of Waters protecting the divine power.
In house after house you will worship the kindling stick, O Agni; toward you the tongue of ghee shall rise up.
Your heart is in the ocean, within the waters; may the plants and waters enter into you;
we would worship you, of the sacrifice, O lord of the sacrifice, with the hymn of praise, with the words of homage, svāhā!
O Avabhṛtha [bath], O Nicuṅkuṇa, you are gliding softly, O Nicuṅkuṇa;
wash away from us the sin committed against the gods, by the gods;
wash away [the sin] committed against mortals, by mortals;
protect us, O god, from broad harm!
O divine waters, this is your embryo;
well-pleased, well-carried, I have carried it for you;
among the gods speak of us as well-doers!
Stepped upon is the fetter of Varuṇa!
Struck away is the fetter of Varuṇa!
Of you, cleansed in the waters, O divine Soma, praised by men, whatever portion is winning cattle, winning horses—invited, I take the portion of that invited [Soma].
Thrown back is the fetter of Varuṇa!
Homage to the fetter of Varuṇa!
Step up, bringing offspring, life-span, and brilliance; then may we be fragrant in our houses;
may the meters join with the Gāyatrī; may wealth and sacrifice adhere to us!
Well-pleased, the child has entered the waters.
O Unnetṛ, lead us up to better [fortune]!
You are kindling wood: may we thrive!
You are a fuel stick: may we kindle well!
You are splendor: place splendor in me!
I have now walked along the waters; we have united with their sap;
rich in milk have I come, O Agni; unite me with brilliance!
Unite me, O Agni, with brilliance, and with offspring, and with wealth;
may the gods know of me, may Indra know together with the ṛṣis!

The sacrifice has gone to the people of the gods;
thence may the blessing of the sacrifice come to me!
The sacrifice has gone to the Fathers, the serpents, the Gandharvas, the waters, the plants, the five peoples, to people;
thence may the blessing of the sacrifice come to me!
I grasp you for the support of the five winds;
I grasp you for the support of the five waters;
I grasp you for the support of the five backs;
I grasp you for the support of the five quarters;
I grasp you for the support of the five tribes!
The earth is ours;
the oblation is the gods';
the blessings belong to the sacrificer.
I grasp you for the support of the five-apertured caru!
You are the dear domain of the gods,
the unassailable place of sacrifice.
With the deities I grasp you for the deities!
Those gods, slayers of the sacrifice, thieves of the sacrifice, who sit upon the earth — / may Agni protect me from them; may we go to the righteous!
Those nights of yours, O Savitṛ, that lead to the gods, which have embraced the thousandfold sacrifice — / together with all houses and with offspring now at first, mounting heaven, cross through the dark realms!
Those gods, slayers of the sacrifice, thieves of the sacrifice, who sit in the intermediate space — / may Vāyu protect me from them; may we go to the righteous!
We have come, O Mitra and Varuṇa, to the choice portion of nights which is yours, / seizing the vault in the world of the righteous on the third back above the light of heaven!
Those gods, slayers of the sacrifice, thieves of the sacrifice, who sit in heaven — / may Sūrya protect me from them; may we go to the righteous!
Wherewith they brought together milk for Indra with the highest oblation, O Jātavedas, / therewith, O Agni, do you also make him thrive; place him in the preeminence among those of noble birth!
Possessing cows, O Agni, possessing sheep, possessing horses is the sacrifice, accompanied by men, an unforgettable companion; / rich in refreshing drink is it, O Asura, rich in offspring, long-lasting wealth, broad-bottomed, full of council-gatherings!
O Agni, protector of vows, I have kept the vow;
I have been able to do it;
we declare that to you;
protect that for us!
Thus is the fifth sthānaka complete, called Yajamāna, in the Iṭhimikā of the Caraka school in the glorious Yajurveda of the Kaṭhas.

The cooking of the rice mess, the Gārhapatya, the Āhavanīya, the midst of the gaming ground, the calling place—
this, truly, is the five-footed Virāj.
That very [Virāj] he attains;
that he secures.
For he who has secured it becomes the best among men.
If the time of the evening Agnihotra should pass by, he should offer, [saying:] 'At dusk of the morning, svāhā!'
That very [utterance] is the oblation there;
thereby it is not missed by him.
If the time of the morning Agnihotra should pass by, he should offer, [saying:] 'At day of the morning, svāhā!'
That very [utterance] is the oblation there;
thereby it is not missed by him.
He should not utter 'svāhā';
the utterance 'svāhā' separates the oblation of the Agnihotra.
When speech resounds, then he should offer;
for that is the vaṣaṭ-call of the Agnihotra.
[Saying:] 'Agni, united with day, with earth'—
Heaven and Earth, Agni—these indeed he pleases;
these he secures.
[Saying:] 'United with the god Savitṛ'—
for without Savitṛ there is no oblation.
[Saying:] 'United with Pūṣan, with the intermediate space'—
cattle, truly, are Pūṣan; cattle are the intermediate space;
cattle indeed he secures.
[Saying:] 'United with the night possessing Indra, svāhā!'—
over the night indeed he offers it;
for without Indra there is no oblation.
The gods, truly, offered the Agnihotra in the morning;
therefore the Bṛhat, the great, was born in the morning.
[Saying:] 'United with the day possessing Indra, svāhā!'—
connected with the Bṛhat, truly, is the day; connected with the Bṛhat is Sūrya;
both the day and Sūrya are conquered for him and offered upon.
Belonging to the Anuṣṭubh, truly, is the night; belonging to the Triṣṭubh is the day;
upon both the night and the day he thus offers.
With: 'O unassailable Agni, enjoy, svāhā!' he offers the second oblation;
just as one cuts off [portions] for Agni, just so is that.
With: 'You have pleased your own body, O Agni; you have sacrificed to Heaven and Earth; place vigor in us,' having anointed a blade of grass, he throws it [into the fire];
that is the completion, the setting at ease.
What is unappeased of the Agnihotra injures creatures;
when, having anointed a blade of grass, he throws it in,
thereby indeed he appeases it.
With: 'Agni, lord of the house, enjoy, svāhā!' he offers in the Gārhapatya with the dipping spoon;
just as one performs the wife's sacrifices, just so is that.
He, truly, reflects upon this,
that having placed [the pot] on the fire in the west, he offers in the east.
When he offers in the Gārhapatya with the dipping spoon,
thereby indeed he pleases him;
he makes him not brood.
In his progeny an Agnihotra-cow is born, day by day he becomes better off, whose Agnihotra is offered thus.

Protect my wealth, O praiseworthy one;
guard it for me.
My first name, O Jātavedas, which mother and father gave [me] of old—
guard that until my return; we will bear your name.
Protect my offspring, O manly one;
guard it for me.
Guard these houses for me, you two, O Mitra and Varuṇa;
undestroyed, uninjured, may Pūṣan protect them,
until our return.
Protect my food, O Purīṣya;
guard it for me.
You have guarded my wealth, O praiseworthy one;
give it back to me.
O Agni of a thousand eyes, hundred heads, hundred radiances,
hundred are your in-breaths, a thousand your out-breaths.
You rule over thousandfold wealth;
give us of that;
may we be sharers of that from you.
Both my name and yours, O Jātavedas, like two garments changing, we wander;
both of us bearing them for skill and for life, each into our proper bodies, O Jātavedas.
You have guarded my offspring, O manly one;
give it back to me.
O Agni, lord of the house, may I be a good lord of the house through you, the lord of the house;
may you be a good lord of the house through me, the lord of the house.
May our Gārhapatya [fire] shine unshared for a hundred winters for both of us.
You have guarded my food, O Purīṣya;
give it back to me.

At the Agnihotra a Stoma is to be yoked.
For he at whose Agnihotra a Stoma is yoked becomes wealthier.
With "Approaching the sacrifice..."—
these creatures indeed are [approached];
[with] "upa" [the Stoma is] a six-by-six Stoma;
he yokes just these [creatures].
With "Following his ancient radiance..."—
the gods indeed are the ancient [radiance];
they are the six-by-six Stoma;
he yokes just them
for the heavenly world.
With "Closer and closer now, O generous one, more and more to you..."—
this [earth] indeed is "closer";
having gone to the heavenly world, he firmly establishes himself on this [earth].
Those creatures which are about to come into being, they are "closer";
he yokes just them.
With "Around you let the invincible chariot..."—
with this [verse] he encloses the yoked Stoma.
With "Agni is the head, the peak of heaven..."—
this [verse] is a pair, possessing seed, rich in cattle;
that Gāyatrī which is the first among the draught-poles, that one is this; with the Gāyatrī he has attended [it].
With "Both of you, Indra and Agni, to invoke..."—
Indra is the sun;
he enters Agni by night;
he praises both of them together;
that Triṣṭubh which is the first among the draught-poles, that one is this; with the Triṣṭubh he has attended [it].
With "This one here was first established by the establishers..."—
for Agni was first established here in this world;
that Jagatī which is the first among the draught-poles, that one is this; with the Jagatī he has attended [it].
With "This is your seasonal womb..."—
for this [earth] is the womb of Agni, Agni of the sun;
that Anuṣṭubh which is the first among the draught-poles, that one is this; with the Anuṣṭubh he has attended [it].
With "I have sung of Dadhikrāvan..."—
that cattle-granting form of Agni which is connected with Dadhikrāvan,
that very form of his he seizes;
cattle become attentive to him.
Agni indeed is cattle;
just as an ox grows old, as a horse, as a man, so does this established [fire] grow old;
he is to be attended with the verses of the establishment of the fires;
for these are his bodies;
with them he makes him young again;
when he attends with the meters,
with them he makes him young again;
as he was when first established, so does he become for him.
He attends [him];
this is his yoking;
this is his self-restraint;
this is his petitioning;
just as one, having fetched [offerings], pays homage to a superior, even so is that.

In the first half of the month he should attend [Agni] with a verse addressed to Agni and Soma.
For that which belongs to the full moon is the share of Agni and Soma;
to both of them he commits him;
the two, not departing, guard him.
In the second half of the month [he should attend] with one addressed to Indra and Agni;
for that which belongs to the new moon is the share of Indra and Agni;
to both of them he commits him;
the two, not departing, guard him.
Agni and Soma commit him to the first half of the month;
Indra and Agni to the second half of the month.
Handing him over in succession, indeed, the gods, not departing, guard him who, knowing thus, attends Agni.
"For what purpose is Agni attended?" they ask.
That indeed is Agni's meal,
when one attends Agni;
for Agni this meal is prepared.
He attends in the evening, not in the morning;
therefore in the evening [food is served] for a guest against sin, and not so in the morning.
There is a benediction for sacrifice after sacrifice;
when one attends Agni,
he prays for this very benediction of the Agnihotra.
Prajāpati created creatures;
he created Agni first of all;
for him he ordained this share, namely the attendance on the fire.
When one attends Agni,
he enriches him with his own share.
Agni indeed longed for yoking, as a horse desires a chariot;
when one attends Agni,
he yokes him; he fulfills his desire.
He who fulfills the desire of a mortal becomes wealthier;
he prospers and becomes wealthier who, knowing thus, attends Agni.
With "upa..."
he unites with [creatures] by this [verse].
With "Agni is the head..."
he pours out seed by this [verse].
With "Both of you, Indra and Agni..."—
Indra and Agni are the in-breath and the out-breath;
he establishes in-breath and out-breath by this [verse].
With "This one here was first established..."
he deposits the embryo by this [verse].
With "Following his ancient radiance..."
he forms the udder by this [verse].
With "This is your seasonal womb..."
he both brings forth and causes to grow by this [verse].
Fitted indeed are the offspring born to him who, knowing thus, attends Agni.

To the creator, the wise, the one to be known, a respectful word to the bull, the virile,
from whence fear comes, let there be freedom from fear for us; I sacrifice away the slights of the gods.
Attend upon Agni with firewood; awaken the guest with ghṛta;
offer the oblations into him.
O Agni, that flame of yours, offered with ghṛta, shines upward,
lapping at the mouth of the ladles.
Let my ghee-holding ladles go toward you, O beloved;
O Agni, enjoy our oblations.
O Agni, unite the offspring and the quarters together with the cattle;
place in him the realms that were in the impulse of Savitṛ.
I unite your heart; united let your mind be;
united let your bodies be; united let your breath of life be.
Those bodies of yours that are dear, united in affection let your hearts be;
let your soul be united in affection, united in affection my bodies.
The Aśvattha that, pregnant with the Śamī, grew upon you,
that of yours I take together with the sacrificial banners.
Into myself I take Agni first, together with the increase of wealth for luster here, O gods;
in myself I place offspring, in myself prosperity; may we rejoice for a hundred winters with good heroes.
That Agni of ours, O ancestors, who, immortal, entered mortals inside the hearts,
him we grasp around in our own self; let him not go away abandoning us;
let us not go away abandoning him.
O great one, mistress of the clan, seat of truth, facing hither come to this support of riches;
pregnant, generate the noble Jātavedas, who goes in front of the sacrifices.
A sweet wave has come up from the ocean, placing further onward for universal sovereignty;
both those liberal patrons and we, may we gather sweet refreshment and vigor together.
So much were you in the beginning;
when spreading out apart, O goddess,
driven by the gods you dispersed through greatness; you became firm with gravel in the third heaven;
you conquered the worlds, the four intermediate regions.
Rise up, O Agni, from Mother Earth; enter the clans from the great abode;
the pious, swift in the contest, placed you, the oblation-bearer, the guardian of the world.
Whatever of heaven or of earth has come into being from the two well-acting, generous ones,
on their back let Jātavedas sit, generating offspring and wealth for us.
Agni the gleaming, with gleaming chariot, whose course is golden, Vaiśvānara sitting in the waters, finding light,
penetrating, hasty, encompassed with powers, energetic, of fair splendor, the gods have placed here.
Whatever un-afflicted thing of the earth came into being along with you,
that let Agni give to Agni; let this one be placed upon that.
Whatever un-afflicted thing of the atmosphere, of heaven, came into being along with you,
that let Agni give to Agni; let this one be placed upon that.

At eight paces [the fire] is to be established by a Brahmin.
The Gāyatrī has eight syllables;
the Brahmin has the Gāyatrī meter;
he establishes him in the proper place of his own meter.
Just as a father invigorates his son, even so his own meter invigorates him.
At eleven paces it is to be established by a Rājanya.
The Triṣṭubh has eleven syllables;
the Rājanya has the Triṣṭubh meter;
he establishes him in the proper place of his own meter.
Just as a father invigorates his son, even so his own meter invigorates him.
At twelve paces it is to be established by a Vaiśya.
The Jagatī has twelve syllables;
the Vaiśya has the Jagatī meter;
he establishes him in the proper place of his own meter.
Just as a father invigorates his son, even so his own meter invigorates him.
"May I obtain the unlimited," thus indeed he establishes the fire;
it is to be established without limit,
for the attainment of the unlimited.
As far as he might think with his eye, at that distance he should establish it;
the eye is truth;
he establishes him in truth.
It is to be established in the forenoon;
this is indeed the auspicious day;
he establishes him on an auspicious day.
At dawn, before the rising of the sun, it is to be established;
in this world Prajāpati created creatures;
they were procreated;
it is to be established thus for procreation.
Night belongs to Agni, day belongs to Indra;
if he should establish it when the sun has risen, he would depart from the color belonging to Agni;
if when it has not risen, from that belonging to Indra.
The latter is to be established when [the sun] has not risen, the former when it has risen;
he obtains both colors of Indra and Agni.
The night is Asuric by color, the day is bright.
He who establishes it when the sun has risen establishes it in the bright.
By night [it is] when it has not risen, by day when it has risen;
he who establishes it when the sun has risen establishes it by day.
Men do not quite know the lunar mansion, as it were;
for they deliberate, as it were; but indeed by the risen [sun] is the auspicious day;
he who establishes it when the sun has risen establishes it on an auspicious day.
"Seven are your kindling-sticks, O Agni, seven tongues, seven seers, seven dear domains;
knowing the seven Hotṛ-offices, fill the seven wombs with ghee."
As many as are the bodies of Agni,
he who, establishing him, leaves them thirsty, he becomes thirsty.
So many are the bodies of Agni, sixfold seven by seven;
those indeed of his he gratifies;
he does not become thirsty.
With a full ladle he offers to Prajāpati in thought;
Prajāpati is full;
he reaches Prajāpati.

The oblation has been poured out, brought near, [and] remains unsprinkled.
Then, having sprinkled the central gaming-place and thrown down the dice, he offers [with the verse]: "Varuṇa of firm vow has sat down in the homesteads for universal sovereignty, he of good counsel."
Three times indeed did the Virāj stride forth: the Gārhapatya, the Āhavanīya, [and] the central gaming-place.
He makes him stride after the striding of the Virāj;
he stands firm;
he attains a firm foundation.
He offers for the upholding of order;
he makes him one who upholds order.
As Savitṛ the supporter, they slay a cow;
they play for it;
they present it to those sitting in the assembly;
by that, he is sought [and] gratified for him.
"Now indeed Bṛhaspati…"—thus he offers in the summoning place with a verse containing the word "mantra" addressed to the All-Gods;
he grasps the sacred formulation (mantra) for him;
coming together, they deliberate upon it.
Together indeed were the gods and men at the Odanapacana.
Those men encroached upon the gods.
Having held back food from them, the gods strode up to the Gārhapatya.
They followed them thither.
Those men indeed encroached upon the gods.
Having held back cattle from them, the gods strode up to the Āhavanīya.
They followed them thither.
Those men indeed encroached upon the gods.
Having held back the sacrifice from them, the gods strode up to the assembly (sabhā).
They followed them thither.
Those men indeed encroached upon the gods.
Having held back the Virāj from them, the gods strode up to the summoning place (āmantraṇa).
Thence they did not pursue them further.
These indeed are the transitions of the gods.
He who knows thus strides up to a better and better world.
This indeed is the unconquered truth of the gods, namely the summoning place.
Therefore, one should go to the summoning place early in the morning;
he goes to truth itself.
Therefore, one should not go to the summoning place uninvited;
therefore, one should not speak untruth in the summoning place.
Bearer (vahni) indeed is the name of the Odanapacana;
a bearer is born to him who knows thus.
The homestead is the Gārhapatya;
he becomes possessed of a homestead who knows thus.
The hearth is the Āhavanīya;
the sacrifice bows toward him who knows thus.
Far-extended is the central gaming-place;
he extends with offspring and cattle who knows thus.
Unattained is the summoning place;
he who seeks to reach him does not reach him who knows thus.

A goat is to be given
for having the same origin.
A garment is to be given;
a garment belongs to all deities;
for the reaching of the deities.
An increasing sacrificial gift is to be given;
the sacrificer indeed increases after the increase of the sacrificial gift.
A sacrificial gift that brings near [milk] is to be given;
a milch-cow indeed is one that brings near;
for she brings near milk;
nothing becomes less for him who establishes the fire.
A milch-cow is to be given to the Hotṛ,
for the winning of blessings;
an ox to the Adhvaryu;
he wins the very bearer of the sacrifice.
A chariot is to be given;
a divine chariot indeed is yoked, namely the sacrifice;
by means of the human chariot he mounts the divine chariot.
A pair of cattle is to be given,
for the winning of a pair.
Six are to be given;
six indeed are the seasons;
he establishes himself in the seasons.
Twelve are to be given;
twelve months are the year;
for the attainment of the year.
If he should fall away beyond twelve, he should give twenty-four;
twenty-four syllables has the Gāyatrī;
the Gāyatrī is the mouth of the sacrifice;
he does not step beyond the Gāyatrī, the mouth of the sacrifice.
There are three oblations;
three are these worlds;
he attains these worlds.
Three times did the Virāj stride forth;
he makes him stride after the striding of the Virāj.
In that [there is one] to the purifying [Agni] (pavamāna),
by that he purifies him;
in that [there is one] to the cleanser [Agni] (pāvaka),
food indeed is the cleanser;
by that he bestows food upon him;
in that [there is one] to the bright [Agni] (śuci),
by that he makes him pure, fit for sacrifice, worthy of worship.
"May I be possessed of cattle, may I be possessed of offspring, may I be possessed of splendor"—thus indeed he establishes the fire.
Breath indeed is the purifying one;
from breath cattle are born;
in that it is for Agni Pavamāna,
by that he generates cattle for him.
Waters indeed are the cleansers;
from the waters offspring are born;
in that it is for Agni Pāvaka,
by that he generates offspring for him.
Yonder sun indeed is the bright one;
he is the bestower of splendor;
in that it is for Agni Śuci,
yonder sun bestows splendor upon him.
The gods and the asuras were in conflict.
As they were going into victory, that Agni deposited his bodies in three places: a third in cattle, a third in the waters, a third in yonder sun.
In that these oblations are poured out,
he gathers together those very bodies of his;
with his body intact, with his splendor intact, he is thus established.
These worlds indeed are these oblations.
They have separate sacrificial grasses;
he fills these worlds.
If they were to have separate sacrificial grasses,
he would not leave
space for generation;
offspring indeed are born to what is deficient.
The two latter [oblations] are to be performed on the same sacrificial grass;
he made space for generation;
upon it he is born.
We know this world, we know that [intermediate world]; yonder [highest world] we either see or do not see;
therefore the two latter [oblations] are to be performed on the same sacrificial grass.
Cattle indeed are these oblations,
the sacrificial cakes are clarified butter,
Agni is Rudra.
If he were to pour out immediately after, he would consign the cattle to Rudra; he would become without cattle.
It is to be poured out after a year;
by means of the year he appeases him.
Loose indeed and of different origin is made of the sacrifice when he pours out after a year;
it is to be poured out immediately after,
for firmness, for having the same origin.

They deliberate: "Is the fire to be established or not to be established by one who is about to establish the fire on the morrow?"
It is indeed to be established.
For without sacrifice is he who is without fire.
A tied goat should stay through that night.
Of the splendor of Agni is the goat;
it is that very Agni that he establishes.
It should be spotted;
for it belongs to all deities.
He who is about to establish the fire on the morrow should observe a vow that night;
he should not eat meat;
he should not approach a woman.
Every man has fire [within him];
Agni would depart from him.
For this is his womb;
from this he is produced, [namely] he who brings him out.
The right [fire] that is churned, is churned for brahma-splendor alone.
Moreover, he makes him having the same origin with the [two] fires.
Yet it is difficult to keep;
they should churn his day by day,
or he should bring it from wherever [he can].
This indeed is his un-won body;
that very [body] he wins.
Where he sees [fire] blazing in the distance, from there he should bring it;
these deities indeed become manifest;
them indeed he wins.
He should bring it from a roasting-pan for one who desires food;
this indeed is his food-eating body;
that very [body] he wins.
From the house of a Brahmin or a Vaiśya who is nourished like an Asura, he should bring it;
whatever that nourishment is, whatever food, that he wins.
In his house, however, he should not eat from it.
Three times he raises [it] up;
three are these worlds;
these worlds indeed he wins.
In that he raises [it] up,
this world he wins thereby.
In that he runs forward toward the east,
the atmosphere [he wins] thereby.
In that he establishes [it],
yonder [world he wins] thereby.
Having established it, he should not lift it up again;
that is a departure;
he becomes worse off.
From the life-breaths indeed Prajāpati created creatures;
they were generated.
He should lift it up by that measure,
for generation.
He should not lift it up too high;
Agni would burn up his life-breaths; he would perish.
He should not place it down low;
that is a departure;
he becomes worse off.

Eaters of what is not sacrificed were his gods formerly;
now he turns toward eaters of what is sacrificed.
They depart with his vigor;
he wins vigor indeed;
he makes them partakers of a share.
They sacrifice in yonder [fire], they offer in this one; they cook in yonder [fire],
for the uninterruptedness of this.
Moreover, he established him in being Vaiśvānara.
Those indeed who sacrificed with the sacrifice prospered.
Men do not quite know the sacrifice;
therefore not everyone prospers.
Formerly, indeed, they used to sacrifice with the New and Full Moon sacrifices accompanied by sacrificial gifts.
This boiled rice that is cooked,
he gives it as this sacrificial gift,
for the prosperity of the sacrifice.
He has performed a sacrifice by that which he sacrifices;
by this boiled rice that is cooked, he has performed a pious work;
he indeed is one who has performed sacrifice and pious works who cooks this.
Prajāpati indeed assigned portions to the gods.
He thought: "I have excluded myself from the sacrifice."
He saw this boiled rice;
he prepared it as a portion for himself.
Prajāpati's portion indeed is this.
He should cook a great, boundless [portion];
boundless is Prajāpati;
he wins Prajāpati indeed.
The gods and the Asuras contended.
Those gods sacrificed toward Prajāpati;
the Asuras offered into one another's mouth.
Those gods cooked this boiled rice;
they poured it out as a portion for Prajāpati.
Seeing that portion,
Prajāpati turned toward the gods.
Thereupon the gods prospered, the Asuras were defeated.
He who, knowing thus, brings the Anvāhārya [rice] prospers himself; his rival is defeated.
Some gods are to be praised, others are to be served.
The gods are to be praised, the Brahmins are to be served.
By the sacrifice he pleases those to be praised; by the Anvāhārya those to be served.
Both become pleased at his sacrifice.
"Prajāpati's portion you are, full of vigor, full of milk;
undecaying you are, for undecayingness [I accept] you;
Undecaying by name you are;
do not decay for me;
protect my in-breath and out-breath;
protect my equalized breath and diffused breath;
protect my upward breath and form;
vigor you are;
place vigor in me;
come unto me!"
With these words,
he prospers Prajāpati directly.

Prajāpati is the Daśahotṛ;
the new- and full-moon sacrifices are the Caturhotṛ;
the Cāturmāsyas are the Pañcahotṛ;
the Soma sacrifice is the Saptahotṛ.
The Agnihotra is the Daśahotṛ;
the new- and full-moon sacrifices are the Caturhotṛ;
the animal sacrifice is the Ṣaḍḍhotṛ;
the Soma sacrifice is the Saptahotṛ.
The breath is the Daśahotṛ;
the eye is the Caturhotṛ;
the ear is the Pañcahotṛ;
speech and the self are the Saptahotṛ.
The Agnihotra is offered for long life; he attains full life who knows thus.
The full-moon [sacrifice] is offered for the eye; [it is] not for the seizure of his eye who knows thus.
The new-moon [sacrifice] is offered for the ear; [it is] not for the seizure of his ear who knows thus.
The Soma sacrifice is offered for speech and for the self; [it is] not for the seizure of his speech nor of his self who knows thus.
Prajāpati is the Daśahotṛ.
Being one, he has the greatest share of the utterances, and being one he becomes many, who knows thus.
The full-moon [sacrifice] is offered for fiery splendor.
A Brahmin, indeed, belongs to Agni;
Agni is connected with the gāyatrī, has the gāyatrī metre;
he has made his own two deities far-extended;
no one else has power over his acquisition and preservation, who knows thus.
The new-moon [sacrifice] is offered for long life;
he attains full life who knows thus.
The Soma sacrifice is offered for the heavenly world;
by it he goes to the heavenly world.

The Naimiṣyas sat at a sacrificial session.
Rising up, they begged twenty-seven heifers among the Kuru-Pañcālas.
Vaka Dālbhi said to them:
"Divide these among yourselves;
I will go to this Dhṛtarāṣṭra Vaicitravīrya;
he will make a house for me."
He went to him;
he did not heed him;
he drove him away:
"These cows, O wretched Brahmin!"
He said:
"Paśupati kills cows;
cooking them further off, roam about!"
[thinking:] "For them, may the god-impelled one pour out food for the king for me!"
he cooked them, cutting out the thighs.
While he was cooking, [the kingdom] dried up.
He offered a cake of black rice on eight potsherds to Agni accompanied by Rudra.
Whatever belonged to Dhṛtarāṣṭra dawned upon as scattered and fleeing.
The diviners found out:
"This Brahmin is practicing sorcery against you;
take refuge with him!"
He sought to win him over;
he gave him much.
He offered a cake of white rice on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat.
Then indeed it dried away.
When practicing sorcery, he should offer a cake of black rice on eight potsherds to Agni accompanied by Rudra.
Agni is Rudra;
he delivers him over to Rudra.
It is of black rice;
black indeed is darkness;
darkness is death;
he causes him to be seized by death.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat when this god assails creatures, when he desires: "May it dry away!"
This is Agni's healing body, the fragrant one;
he runs up to it with its share;
with it he heals them.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat about whom they hear that he has died when he has not died.
Foul-smelling indeed is he heard of who is heard of as having died;
that very body of Agni which is fragrant, to that he runs up with its share;
with it he makes him fragrant.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni Surabhimat whom they accuse without having slain.
Seized by defilement indeed is he whom they accuse without having slain;
that very body of Agni which is fragrant, to that he runs up with its share;
with it he purifies him.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni the food-eater who desires: "May I be an eater of food."
Agni is the food-eater among the gods;
he runs up to him with his share;
he makes him an eater of food.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni possessed of food who desires: "May I be possessed of food."
Agni is possessed of food among the gods;
he runs up to him with his share;
he makes him possessed of food.
He should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni the lord of food who desires: "May I be the lord of food."
Agni is the lord of food among the gods;
he runs up to him with his share;
he makes him the lord of food.
He should offer it all around for a year;
the year is the giver of proper food;
he runs up to him with his share;
he makes him an eater of food, possessed of food, and a lord of food.
A Brahmin should offer a cake on eight potsherds to Agni Vasumat for all desires.
Whatever one finds, that is wealth;
Agni is possessed of wealth among the gods;
he runs up to him with his share;
he grants him all desires.

One who is born after should offer a caru to Bṛhaspati made of after-ripening rice.
Bṛhaspati, indeed, was born after among the gods;
he came round to the foremost place.
Bṛhaspati is the deity of him who is born after;
he takes hold of him;
he leads him round to the foremost place.
Bṛhaspati is the brahman;
the brahman is the father of the Brahmin;
the father has power over the son, whether forwards or backwards, not vice versa;
the father, the brahman itself, leads him round to the foremost place.
After-ripening, indeed, are these rice grains;
he who is born after—them he now leads round to the foremost place.
In that he prepares the offering,
he takes hold of them;
they lead him round to the foremost place.
The sacrificial grass is regrown; the kindling wood is regrown,
for success.
One who desires relatives should offer a caru to Bṛhaspati;
its accompanying and offering verses should contain [the word] 'group'.
A Brahmin, indeed, belongs to Bṛhaspati by deity;
he provides his own deity with an abundance.
The accompanying and offering verses contain [the word] 'group';
he makes him possessed of a group through his relatives.
One should offer a caru to Brahmaṇaspati;
its accompanying and offering verses should pertain to the Maruts, if one should desire: 'May there be the power of the brahman.'
Brahmaṇaspati is the brahman;
the Maruts are the commonalty;
he subjects the commonalty entirely to the brahman;
suddenly there is the power of the brahman.
One should offer a caru to Aryaman who desires: 'May my subjects be desirous of giving.'
He, indeed, is Aryaman who gives;
upon giving these subjects subsist;
they become desirous of giving to him.
One should offer a caru to Indra and Bṛhaspati for a nobleman who desires to thrive.
The gods, indeed, were afraid of the nobleman being born;
they pursued him and bound him with a cord.
Bound, indeed, is this nobleman born;
if he were born unbound, nothing would remain;
he would eat all these subjects completely down.
The nobleman belongs to Indra by deity;
Bṛhaspati is the brahman;
by the brahman he releases him from the binding;
he, unbound, spreads out for manly vigor.
Twice-twisted gold is the sacrificial fee,
for cutting off that cord.
One who desires long life should offer a caru of a hundred kṛṣṇala weights in ghee to Prajāpati.
The gods, indeed, feared death;
they sought refuge with Prajāpati;
he sacrificed for them with this offering.
Gold is immortality;
with immortality he placed immortality in them;
thence, indeed, they became immortal.
He who fears death should sacrifice with this [offering].
Gold is immortality;
with immortality he places immortality in him.
It consists of a hundred kṛṣṇalas;
man has a hundred [years of] life, a hundred manly vigors;
he wins life and manly vigor.
It is to be performed to the Viśve Devas,
for he then sacrificed for all the gods;
it is to be performed to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati is the womb;
he firmly establishes himself in the womb.
Four kṛṣṇalas each form a portion,
for success.
They carry it around to the Brahman priest;
the Brahman priest is the brahman;
by the brahman he places life in him in one continuous stream.
He who desires, 'May I become wealthier,' should sacrifice with this.
This, indeed, is man's heavenly world, that he becomes wealthier in this world;
he thrives and becomes wealthier who, knowing thus, sacrifices with this.
One who has reached prosperity should offer a caru to Sūrya;
its cover should be a golden ornament,
with a silver one below.
Yonder sun is the end; having reached the end of prosperity, a man turns back;
from the end he grasps the end;
he does not become worse than he is when sacrificing.
Yonder sun was this [world];
he roamed burning this [world];
the gods, seizing him with these two, made him go to yonder world;
he did not shine;
they offered five golden kṛṣṇalas at the fore-offerings;
gold is brilliance;
with brilliance they placed brilliance in him;
thence he grasped brilliance;
he became brilliant along these five directions.
This [earth] is silver; yonder [sky] is golden;
seizing him with these two, he makes him go to yonder world.
They say: 'He did not shine,' for indeed he did not shine.
He should offer five golden kṛṣṇalas at the fore-offerings;
gold is brilliance;
with brilliance he unites him;
he becomes brilliant along these five directions.

One who desires spiritual lustre should offer a caru to Soma and Rudra made of white rice grains; there should be churned ghee from a white cow with a white calf in it.
Svarbhānu the Asura pierced the sun with darkness.
He did not shine out.
For him the gods sought an expiation.
They sacrificed for him with this offering.
With it they struck away the darkness from him.
Covered by darkness is he who, being fit for spiritual lustre, does not possess spiritual lustre.
He strikes away darkness from him;
he unites him with spiritual lustre.
The ghee is from a white cow with a white calf, the rice grains are white.
Like this indeed is spiritual lustre;
he unites [him] with spiritual lustre.
They sprinkle with ghee;
they wipe off with ghee,
for non-appeasement;
for water is appeasement.
They should not even carry water into his house that night.
He should sacrifice in an enclosed space,
for enclosing spiritual lustre.
The caru is a span in size upwards, and so across.
So great indeed is man;
as far as his vital breaths extend, as great as his self is, from that he strikes away darkness.
They proceed together with the rays [of the sun];
that sun, rising, strikes away darkness from him.
He should sacrifice at the full moon of Tiṣya.
Tiṣya indeed is Rudra, the moon is Soma.
Belonging to Soma are the plants towards the east, belonging to Rudra towards the west.
They wither towards the east;
they wither towards the west.
Visibly he makes them prosper.
He who sacrifices with this is liable to become skin-diseased;
for he strikes away excessively from him.
He should also recite Manu's verses among the kindling verses.
Whatever Manu said was medicine;
he makes medicine for him.
'The refreshing rivers flow near'—with this Nārāśaṃsa verse he encloses,
for appeasement.
It has six feet;
six indeed are the seasons;
he establishes himself in the seasons.
However, in that case he is certainly liable to become skin-diseased;
for ascending, he goes against [excessive] fiery energies.
One desiring cattle should offer a caru to Soma and Pūṣan as a subsequent offering.
Soma indeed is the Brahmin's own deity;
Pūṣan is cattle;
he strengthens his own deity with cattle;
he provides a skin [for himself].
One who practices sorcery should offer a caru to Soma and Rudra made of black rice grains.
Soma indeed is the lord of these creatures; Rudra is Agni; the lord rules over what is to be killed and what is not to be killed.
Having begged him off from the lord, he hands him over to Rudra.
It is of black rice grains;
black indeed is darkness, darkness is death;
he causes him to be seized by death itself.
The sacrificial grass is made of reed grass,
for breaking down.
The kindling wood is of vibhītaka,
for splitting apart.
One should offer a caru to Soma and Rudra for rivalry or for [obtaining] a second [partner].
Half of everything should belong to one and half to the other: half should be of white rice grains, half of black; half of the sacrificial grass should be of reed grass, half of darbha; half of the kindling wood should be of vibhītaka, half of another tree.
Soma indeed is the Brahmin's own deity;
he generates a second [partner] for him in his own deity.
That it is half and half,
that is for duality.
One should offer a caru to Soma and Rudra in milk for one who is diseased.
Belonging to Soma is the living, belonging to Agni the dead.
Neither living nor dead is he who is diseased;
from both of them he ransoms him by their share.
It is in milk;
milk indeed is milk;
man is milk;
milk is diseased in him who is diseased;
with milk he wins milk for him.
He who is the Hotṛ indeed drags him out swallowed by Soma and Rudra;
he is liable to die.
He should give an ox to him;
he should kill it;
he should eat of it;
that indeed is the expiation there.
One desiring offspring or desiring cattle should offer a caru to Soma and Rudra.
Soma indeed is the layer of semen;
Rudra is Agni, he is the procreator;
Soma places semen in him;
Agni procreates.
He obtains offspring or cattle, for whichever desire he offers.

One who is going to sacrifice with this [rite] should offer on the previous day an eleven-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni and Viṣṇu, a caru to Sarasvatī, and a caru to Bṛhaspati.
Agni indeed is all the deities; Viṣṇu is the sacrifice; Sarasvatī is speech; Bṛhaspati is the brahman.
These very deities he takes hold of on the previous day.
The vital breaths indeed are the deities;
having taken hold of the vital breaths, he fasts.
A sick man should offer tomorrow an eight-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni, a caru to Soma, a caru to Aditi, a caru made of barley to Varuṇa, and a twelve-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni Vaiśvānara.
To Agni indeed go the flesh-parts of a deceased man;
to Soma, the sap;
to the earth, the body.
This [earth] is Aditi;
for in her he lies.
These deities indeed have power over a man;
from them he rouses him upward.
Belonging to Soma is he while alive; belonging to Agni when deceased.
Neither alive nor dead is he who is sick;
from both of them he ransoms him with their share.
Seized by Varuṇa is he who is sick.
In that there is one to Varuṇa,
he frees him from Varuṇa.
It is made of barley.
It is a span in size.
Agni Vaiśvānara is the year; life is the year;
he establishes him in the year, in life.
He attains a complete lifespan.
These offerings are five.
Man is fivefold;
he secures the man.
'You are Agni's life,' [saying this] he places gold in the ghee.
The gold is born of Agni; mortals make life for Agni;
with them he bestows life on him.
'Indra's breath,' [he says].
Having these deities indeed are the breaths;
these he places in him.
Having these deities are the directions;
according to their deity he rouses him up from the directions.
He makes five oblations of these.
Man is fivefold;
he wins the man.
All the officiating priests speak around him;
all the officiating priests treat him.
They speak along the hand of the Brahman priest.
The Brahman is the brahman;
by the brahman alone they place life in him at once.
'Because you slithered,' [he says];
thereby he explains the birth, connection, and power of ghee.
'With the purifying stoma, you,' [he says].
Only so much exists as cups, stomas, and metres;
with as much as exists, with so much he treats him.
'I raise up, I release, let him lift up,' [he says].
Just as one raises up, releases, and lifts up one condemned to death, even so does he now raise him up, release him, and lift him up.
'The long-lived gods,' [saying this] he hands it to him.
These gods indeed are long-lived;
through them he bestows life on him.
He drinks it.
He drinks life itself.
Whatever of that ghee he would, as it were, suck out from the gold—
gold is brilliance; ghee is life;
from brilliance he places life in himself.
'Make him, O Agni, for life, for lustre,' [saying this] he ties on the gold.
Agni indeed is all deities;
gold is life;
with all deities he binds it on for him.
'Sharpen, O Varuṇa, sharp might,' [he says];
he whets him.
'Bestow protection on him, O Aditi, like a mother,' [he says].
This [earth] is Aditi;
he establishes him upon her.
'So that all the gods may let him reach old age,' [he says];
he makes him reach death from old age.
'The breath of the Aśvins, of Mitra and Varuṇa, of Bṛhaspati,' [he says].
Having these deities indeed are the breaths;
these he places in him;
these he arranges in him;
these he makes not depart from him.

One who desires to thrive should pour out three puroḍāśa cakes.
Three are these worlds;
having obtained these very worlds, he secures vigor and virility.
Each successive [cake] is larger;
for each successive of these worlds is larger, they are one above the other;
for these worlds are just like this.
There are twelve potsherds,
for they are three four-potsherd [cakes],
for success.
He cuts off [a portion] of all of them with a sprinkling of ghee,
without making a blunder.
If he does not cut off from one, he makes a blunder;
no blunder befalls him.
The first is made of rice, then one made of barley, then one made of rice.
Rice grains are reddish as it were;
this world is reddish as it were;
that [world] is reddish as it were.
Barley grains are white as it were;
the intermediate space is white as it were.
Through the intermediate space these worlds are continuous;
[this is] for the continuity of these worlds.
He should make the last one of wild rice,
for non-tautology.
Three milch cows are the sacrificial gift, three tārpya-cloths, three pieces of gold.
What they embroider into tārpya-cloths is the form of the yajus formulas;
gold [pieces] are of the sāman chants; milch cows are of the meters.
These, indeed, are the forms of the sacrifice;
through its forms he secures the sacrifice.
A garment belongs to all the deities;
when he gives garments,
he secures all the deities.
Gold is splendor and spiritual luster;
when he gives pieces of gold,
he secures splendor and spiritual luster.
A milch cow is threefold-successful: self, calf, and milk;
when he gives milch cows,
he makes him threefold-successful.
He should recite all the meters;
for this is the complete sacrifice;
for all the meters are recited at a Soma sacrifice.
When the sacrifice was emitted, its afterbirth wrapped around it;
that became the tārpya-cloth.
The sacrifice is the Traidhātavī [offering];
when the sacrificer puts on the tārpya-cloth,
he covers himself with his own womb.
He should also recite an uṣṇih and a kakubh among the kindling verses.
The uṣṇih is the virility of the kakubh, the kakubh of the uṣṇih;
he secures them endowed with virility.
He should not conclude with a jagatī;
this is the end of the meters;
he would reach the end, he would cut off the sacrifice.
With the triṣṭubh, "O Agni, three are your steeds, three your seats," he concludes.
The triṣṭubh is vigor and virility; he does not reach the end;
he establishes him in vigor and virility.

A horse must not be accepted.
Indeed, this one has teeth in both jaws;
he who accepts [it] chews up his own faculty and virility;
therefore it must not be accepted.
Varuṇa accepted it first;
he went to his own deity;
Varuṇa seized him.
He sacrificed with this oblation to Varuṇa,
for freedom from Varuṇa.
He who accepts a horse should sacrifice with this oblation to Varuṇa,
for freedom from Varuṇa.
It is on four potsherds;
the horse has four feet;
manifestly he releases him from Varuṇa's fetter.
As to that, they say:
"Indeed, he is not released from the others by that which releases him from one."
As many horses as he may accept, so many four-potsherd [cakes] he should pour out.
Here and here is Varuṇa's fetter;
wherever Varuṇa's fetter is, from there he releases him from Varuṇa.
There is one extra;
from that which they do not think of, he releases him thereby;
moreover, he appeases by sacrifice what is yet to come of Varuṇa.
Varuṇa seizes him even more who, having sacrificed with this [offering], accepts another horse.
If he intends to accept another horse, he should perform [an offering] to Sūrya and Varuṇa.
He accepts [it] with the deity Sūrya alongside this world;
he releases him from Varuṇa with that which is for Varuṇa.
There is also a caru for Apāṃ Napāt.
The horse is without a womb, born in the waters;
making him possessed of a womb, he firmly establishes him.
There are twenty-one kindling verses;
twenty-one-fold is the year:
five seasons, twelve months, three are these worlds, that sun is the twenty-first; this is Prajāpati.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati;
he obtains the horse.
"O King Varuṇa, whatever fetter of yours has the gāyatrī meter, that [fetter] of yours I appease by sacrifice with this, svāhā to it!
O King Varuṇa, whatever fetter of yours has the triṣṭubh meter, the jagatī meter, the anuṣṭubh meter, that [fetter] of yours I appease by sacrifice with this, svāhā to it!"
Thus [he says].
So many are the meters;
the meters are Varuṇa's fetters;
with them he seizes him whom Varuṇa seizes.
Here and here is Varuṇa's fetter;
wherever Varuṇa's fetter is, from there he releases him from Varuṇa.
There are two [oblations] for Apāṃ Napāt;
whatever two watery fetters he has, by those two he releases him from them.
He offers these six [oblations];
six are the seasons;
he firmly stands in the seasons.

For a full year, indeed, his meters are exhausted, whoever sacrifices with soma.
The Devikās are the meters;
he renders the meters unexhausted, renewing their strength.
A four-year-old unbred cow is the sacrificial gift;
for she is hope;
he is anointed toward hope;
he creates hope for him.
One who desires cattle should sacrifice with the Devikās.
The Devikās are the meters;
the meters are a pair: Anumati is the Gāyatrī, Rākā the Triṣṭubh, Sinīvālī the Jagatī, Kuhū the Anuṣṭubh, Dhātṛ the Vaṣaṭkāra.
Because two are lower and two are higher, that is a pair;
because Dhātṛ is the Vaṣaṭkāra, that is a pair.
From that very pair he generates cattle for him.
The full moon day that is former is Anumati; that which is later is Rākā.
The new moon day that is former is Sinīvālī; that which is later is Kuhū. The moon is Dhātṛ.
Because it dwells full in one and diminished in the other, that is a pair.
Because they see the one and not the other, that is a pair.
Because the moon is born from the new moon day, that is a pair.
From that very pair he generates cattle for him.
One who desires offspring should sacrifice with the Devikās.
He should place Dhātṛ last.
The Devikās are females, Dhātṛ is the male;
the offspring, turning away, take the seed;
in them turning away, this seed is placed.
As to that, they say:
"Indeed, he does not place it away because the former [female] is impregnated."
He should place Dhātṛ in between;
he makes all of them rejoicing in the male.
He finds a son, but he becomes one who walks behind [others];
for he leads him around behind with women.
If a [son] should be born, having placed Dhātṛ in front, he should then offer this very [sacrifice];
he leads him around to the very front.
A sick person should sacrifice with the Devikās.
He should place Dhātṛ in the middle.
Dhātṛ is the year;
for the year is confused for him, and so he falls sick;
having arranged the year in the middle for him, he then regenerates him from this pair.
These unappeased meters are capable of wiping him out;
he should seize an animal victim,
for appeasement, for non-wiping-out.
Some animals stand in virility, others do not stand in virility.
Those that are puroḍāśas in front stand in virility;
those that are puroḍāśas behind do not stand in virility.
Those that stand in virility attend while enjoying;
those that do not stand in virility perish.
Those that are puroḍāśas in front stand in virility;
they are released toward Prajāpati as a support.
In the pots in which the somas are placed, in those he should place the Devikās;
for soma is a placer of seed.
One who gives away all his possessions should sacrifice with the Devikās.
Indeed, he who gives all is deprived of cattle.
The Devikās are meters, the meters are cattle;
here he sees the cattle in the lowest place, where he divides them;
from wherever he sees them in the lowest place, from there he regenerates them.
Whoever sacrifices with soma should sacrifice with the Devikās.
The Devikās are the meters;
he renders the meters unexhausted, renewing their strength.

Viśvarūpa, son of Tvaṣṭṛ, had three heads; he was the nephew of the Asuras.
With one head he drank soma;
with one he consumed food;
with one he drank liquor.
Indra thought:
"Surely this one will become this [all]!"
He grappled with him;
he brought about a joint death with him.
He said to a carpenter standing [by]:
"Run up, cut off his heads!"
The carpenter, having run up, cut off his heads with an axe.
Therefore a carpenter's head is held [liable];
therefore his food is unfit to be eaten.
The head of his that was the soma-drinking one became a kapiñjala bird;
the one that was the food-eating one, a partridge;
the one that was the liquor-drinking one, a sparrow;
therefore it chirps as if continuously burning.
Tvaṣṭṛ, whose son was slain, brought soma without Indra.
He pressed it below in a thousand-holed [vessel];
then they say:
in a hundred-holed [vessel].
Indra, having taken it away from him by force, drank it off through a tube.
He was deprived of the soma-drinking in every direction.
Therefore soma is not to be drunk by one uninvited;
he becomes deprived of the soma-drinking.
What was released from his nostrils, those two became lions;
what [came] from his eyes, those two became tigers;
what [came] from his ears, those two became wolves;
what [came] from below, that was the Sautrāmaṇī.
What he first spat out, that became the jujube [kuvala];
what [he spat out] second, that was the badara [jujube];
what [he spat out] third, that was the karkandhu [jujube].
The two Aśvins healed him;
they made him sacrifice with this Sautrāmaṇī.
Whatever virilities he was then deprived of, those having obtained, they placed in him.
He for whom soma flows over should sacrifice with this;
for he from whom soma flows over is deprived of virility;
whatever virility there is, that he places in him.
This very [rite] also takes place in the royal consecration;
for he who is consecrated by the royal consecration is deprived of virility;
for he slays Vṛtra;
whatever virility there is, that he places in him.
Desiring to flourish, either a brahmin or a nobleman should sacrifice with this very [rite].
Virility is indeed so much;
whatever virility there is, that he places in him.
Regarding that, they say:
"This is a sacrifice for the afflicted; one who is not afflicted should not sacrifice with it."
Regarding that, they say:
"One should sacrifice indeed;
for every person is afflicted;
everyone desires to flourish;
therefore one should sacrifice."
In that there is [a cup] for the Aśvins:
for the two Aśvins healed [him].
In that there is [a cup] for Sarasvatī:
Sarasvatī is indeed speech;
for they healed [him] with speech.
In that there is [a cup] for Indra:
for they placed that virility in Indra.
With kuvala groats he mixes the [cup] for the Aśvins;
with badara groats, that for Indra;
with karkandhu groats, that for Sarasvatī.
Sarasvatī is speech;
he places sweetness in his speech itself.
Moreover, if that speech speaks ill of him, it again speaks well of him.
The Adhvaryu should meditate in his mind on the two lions;
the Pratiprasthātṛ on the two tigers;
the sacrificer on the two wolves.
There is liquor;
whatever virilities he was then deprived of, those having obtained, the two place in him.

He should set it down on the head of a brahmin;
thereby it is fit for sacrifice.
Liquor is food;
food is fit for sacrifice;
thereby it is fit for sacrifice.
There is one introductory verse, one offering verse;
he places virility in him in a single way.
He offers it as a whole oblation;
he places all virility in him.
He should make a brahmin drink it;
thereby it is made an oblation.
It may be drunk by himself;
he places virility in himself.
That is not to be heeded.
Liquor is evil;
he is seized by evil in the middle when he passes through [the middle];
he releases him from evil from the middle itself.
If he were to offer in the northern fire, he would not turn away from evil; a remedy would be made.
Because he offers in the southern fire,
he turns away from evil; a remedy is made.
Because he causes it to trickle out,
for this [evil] trickles away, as it were.
That which was his deprivation, that which was liable to death, went then to the ancestors.
Because they consecrate with [verses] containing the word 'ancestor',
they stir him up from the ancestors.
Because [they do so] with three each,
for the ancestors are in the third world.
Because there are four [verses],
they stir him up from these four quarters.
It has a hundred syllables;
man has a life of a hundred [years] and a virility of a hundred [powers];
he obtains life and virility.
He buys the shoots from a eunuch with lead;
for a eunuch is untruth, lead is untruth, liquor is untruth;
by means of untruth he buys untruth from untruth.
A mare is the sacrificial gift,
for she is swift;
liquor is swift, as it were;
for success.

Svarbhānu, the Asura, pierced the sun with darkness.
It did not shine out.
From it the gods took away the darkness.
What they took away first, that became a black sheep;
what [they took away] second, that was a reddish-brown [sheep];
what [they took away] third, that was a white [sheep];
what they took away from the bone, that became a barren sheep.
Now at that time this [earth] was bald and hairless.
The gods seized that [sheep] for Aditi, for desire;
by it they made hair grow on her;
then this [earth] received hair.
He should seize this very [sheep] thus for Aditi, for desire;
whatever desire he desires, that he wins.
A downward ray of that sun stood opposite the Cātvāla trench;
these two united as a pair; she became a barren sheep.
Now at that time these plants were fruitless.
The gods seized her for the Ādityas, for desires;
by her they made [the plants] bear good fruit;
then these [plants] bore fruit.
He should seize both of these thus:
one for Aditi for desire, the other for the Ādityas for desires;
he seizes [them] from both creations for desire.
He should seize a ewe for Sarasvatī, from whom speech departs.
Sarasvatī is speech;
Sarasvatī departs from him from whom speech departs.
That it is for Sarasvatī,
having obtained speech directly, he secures it.
It has not shed its teeth,
for wholeness;
it is unblemished,
for completeness.
Whichever is born the last of triplets, he should seize it for vigor;
vigor is desire.
These two are born to separate teats,
and this one is born to vigor itself;
he secures vigor by obtaining it with this.
The sacrificial post is made of udumbara wood.
Where the gods divided vigor, from there the udumbara tree was born.
That it is made of udumbara wood,
[is] for the securing of vigor.
One desiring offspring or one desiring cattle should seize this very [animal] for Soma and Pūṣan.
Soma is the placer of semen;
Pūṣan is the begetter;
Soma places semen in him; Pūṣan begets [it];
he finds offspring or cattle, for whichever desire he seizes [it].
Desiring to thrive, he should seize a white he-goat for Vāyu.
Vāyu is the swiftest of the gods;
he runs up to him with his own share;
he leads him most quickly to prosperity;
he wipes off;
he runs forward;
he leads him to prosperity.
This one [who thrives] becomes active, both meritorious and as it were so-so.
Desiring relatives, he should seize for Vāyu Niyutvat a white he-goat with spotted ears.
These creatures, bound by the nose, follow Vāyu hither and thither.
Vāyu is the breath;
Vāyu is the clan of the gods;
the Niyuts are the guide, the clan of the gods;
he runs up to him with his own share;
he brings the clan to him.
That it is for [Vāyu] Niyutvat,
he makes a support,
for non-wiping-out.
For desires, he should seize a white he-goat for Vāyu.
He who desires, desires subtly;
Vāyu blows subtly along the gods;
he runs up to him with his own share;
he blows along his desires.

One who has a rival should seize a dwarf [animal] dedicated to Viṣṇu.
Viṣṇu indeed won these worlds;
he drove out the Asuras from these worlds.
He indeed wins these worlds;
he drives out the rival from these worlds.
He should sacrifice on uneven ground,
for uneven, as it were, are these worlds.
He should seize [an animal] with speckled thighs dedicated to Indra and the Maruts, from which kṣatriya the clan would withdraw aside.
Indra indeed is the nobility; the Maruts are the clan.
He attaches the clan right to the nobility.
It is one with speckled thighs;
he puts the clan right behind him,
for non-mingling.
One who desires cattle should seize [an animal] with a raised spine dedicated to Indra.
Indra indeed uncovered Vala;
a thousand followed out after him.
Before it, this [animal] pierced upwards;
seeing these worlds, wishing to pierce upwards, it shrank together.
Therefore it is compressed, with its neck bent back.
He should seize this one before the thousand;
he obtains a thousand cattle;
he indeed uncovers Vala.
When he should obtain a thousand cattle, then he should seize a dwarf [animal] dedicated to Viṣṇu,
for firm standing.
Upon this, indeed, that thousand stood;
being stood upon, it branched apart.
Therefore it is stretched out sideways, as it were.
He should seize these thus on both sides,
for seizing the thousand.
Sacrifice, indeed, desired Dakṣiṇā;
he united with her.
She conceived an embryo;
casting it aside, she ran away.
Aditi noticed it lying cast aside;
she brought it [to herself];
she placed it under her thighs;
it grew under her thighs.
Therefore it is stretched sideways, as it were,
for it grew under the thighs.
Viṣṇu noticed it;
he laid claim to it.
Concerning it they went for an inquiry; they decided it for Viṣṇu.
Therefore they say: 'The son belongs to the maker alone.'
By whichever dwarf he would wish to prosper, for that one he should beforehand offer a caru to Aditi.
Aditi indeed made this one grow;
he ransoms it from her.
With that [animal] free from debt, ransomed, fit for sacrifice, and released, he indeed prospers.
One who would hope for kingship should seize a brown bull, with a prominent chest, dedicated to Soma.
Soma indeed is king of the gods;
Soma is the deity of him who hopes for kingship.
He runs to his own deity with his share;
he leads him to kingship.
In that it has a prominent chest,
that is the form of virility.
He should seize a red bull, with a prominent chest, dedicated to Indra the thunderbolt-wielder, for a nobleman who is either practicing sorcery or wishing to thrive.
Noble indeed is the nobleman by deity;
the thunderbolt is his virility.
He places the thunderbolt in him;
by it he conquers;
he indeed thrives.
In that it has a prominent chest,
that is the form of virility.
Whichever is born on the Ekāṣṭakā, he should let that one loose.
If two should be born, he should let both loose.
If a second should be born in a year, he should let that one loose after a year.
He should offer an eight-potsherd [cake] to Agni when they are born;
cattle indeed belong to Agni;
from him he ransoms them.
He should offer a twelve-potsherd [cake] to Agni Vaiśvānara when the year has come round;
the year indeed is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he ransoms them from the year until the time they become fit for sacrifice.
Year after year he should offer a twelve-potsherd [cake] to Agni Vaiśvānara;
the year indeed is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he ransoms them year after year.
With both of them ransomed on all sides, fit for sacrifice, and released, he indeed prospers.
On this [day], indeed, Indra was born;
he is the most vigorous of the gods.
Therefore, whichever of cattle is born on the Ekāṣṭakā, that becomes vigorous.
Vigorous becomes he who, knowing thus, sacrifices with these two.
He should seize the former for Indra the destroyer of rivals;
a rival is evil;
he indeed wards off evil.
The latter for Indra who overcomes Vṛtra;
a rival is evil;
having warded off evil, he then becomes an overcomer of Vṛtra by this;
he enters upon self-rule.
No other among his equals becomes wealthier than he who, knowing thus, sacrifices with these two.
A hundred is the sacrificial fee of one; a horse drawing the yoke in front is that of the other.
Whatever two Brāhmaṇas are at hand in this [sacrifice],
he should seize [the animals] without letting them loose in this manner;
by both he indeed prospers.
Whatever he gives there, that is the sacrificial fee.

One whose father and grandfather were virtuous but who does not attain that should seize a he-goat dedicated to Agni [and] a bull dedicated to Soma.
Agni is all the deities;
he seizes the deities right from the mouth.
Verily, he is deprived of Indra's power, of the Soma-drinking, whose father and grandfather are virtuous but who does not attain that.
A brāhmaṇa belongs to Soma by deity;
Indra's power is his Soma-drinking, in that [the animal] is dedicated to Soma;
he causes him to prosper with Indra's power, with the Soma-drinking.
One who is excluded, or who has been excluded for a long time, should seize a milch cow with calves of various ages dedicated to Heaven and Earth.
Verily, he is excluded from Heaven and Earth who is excluded, or excluded for a long time.
In that it is dedicated to Heaven and Earth,
he gives him a share in Heaven and Earth.
Verily, he is like one [belonging to] various ages who is excluded, or excluded for a long time;
various-aged is his deity.
In that it is [a cow] with calves of various ages,
his own beast through that is of this [deity] alone.
He should seize a calf dedicated to Vāyu on the morrow.
Vāyu, verily, is the calf of these two;
he swells these two;
he causes [them] to yield.
He runs to him with his own share;
he causes these two to yield for him.
One whose father and grandfather did not drink Soma, but who himself desires to drink, should seize this dappled [beast] produced along with [another] dedicated to Indra and Agni.
Verily, he is deprived of Indra's power, of the Soma-drinking, whose father and grandfather do not drink Soma.
A brāhmaṇa belongs to Agni by deity;
Indra's power is his Soma-drinking, in that [the offering] is dedicated to Indra and Agni;
by his own deity he continues the Soma-drinking.
Verily, the Soma-drinking of him whose father and grandfather do not drink Soma is produced along with [another];
in that it is produced along with [another],
his own beast through that is of him;
in that it is dappled,
that is the form of [an offering] belonging to two deities.
One who desires, 'May a well-proportioned son be born to me,' should seize a gelded [beast] dedicated to Tvaṣṭṛ.
Tvaṣṭṛ, verily, is the fashioner of forms;
he runs to him with his own share;
he fashions forms for him.
One who has a rival should seize [a beast] with a black blaze dedicated to the Aśvins.
The gods, verily, drove the Asuras away from the day;
what was their own, what was their property, what was to be known, with that they entered into the night.
The gods saw this [beast] with a black blaze dedicated to the Aśvins;
they seized it;
by it they followed them into the night.
The Aśvins, verily, are Heaven and Earth;
having hemmed them in from below with Heaven and Earth, and from beyond with the sun, they drove them from the night.
What was their own, what was their property, what was to be known, that they wrested away.
Then the gods prospered, the Asuras were defeated.
The Aśvins, verily, are Heaven and Earth;
having hemmed in the rival from below with Heaven and Earth, and from beyond with the sun, he drives [him] from day and night;
what is his own, what is his property, what is to be known, that he wrests away;
he prospers in himself, his rival is defeated.
In that it has a blaze,
that is the form of the day;
in that it is black,
that is of the night;
he drives him out from both colours.
Indra, verily, desired Vilisteṅgā, the Dānavī;
he lived among the Asuras;
he became a woman among women, a man among men;
he thought himself to be seized by Nirṛti, as it were.
He saw this unmanly [beast] dedicated to Indra and Nirṛti;
in that form in which he lived, that he seized.
What of it was not to be cut off into portions, with that he proceeded first:
'Going away to the south, on a natural cleft, may Nirṛti partake, being pleased! Svāhā!'
Then, returning, he concluded the other [part] dedicated to Indra.
Having cut off a portion for Nirṛti first, he then placed Indra's power and manly vigour in himself through [the part] dedicated to Indra.
Then, verily, he prospered.
One who desires to prosper, who thinks himself to be seized by Nirṛti as it were, should seize an unmanly [beast] dedicated to Indra and Nirṛti.
What of it would not be to be cut off into portions, with that he should proceed first:
'Going away to the south, on a natural cleft, may Nirṛti partake, being pleased! Svāhā!'
Then, returning, he should conclude the other [part] dedicated to Indra.
Having cut off a portion for Nirṛti first, he places Indra's power and manly vigour in himself through [the part] dedicated to Indra;
he indeed prospers.
In battle, one who desires, 'May this army not conquer,' should seize a wide-horned bull dedicated to Indra.
Indra, verily, is the leader of battle;
Indra is the bringer of victory;
he runs to him with his own share;
he leads these away.
In that it is wide-horned,
that is the form of dispersing.
At the junction of two tribes, one who desires, 'May I fall upon both tribes,' should seize a wide-horned bull for Indra the Destroyer.
He falls upon both tribes;
they go about slaying one another.
In that it is wide-horned,
he causes the two to go to complete destruction.

He should seize three bulls with a blaze in spring, three with white humps in summer, three with white posteriors in autumn.
In that they have a blaze,
thereby he places fiery splendour right at the front.
In that they have white humps,
thereby he places lustre right in the middle.
In that they have white posteriors,
thereby he places spiritual lustre right at the top.
They are seized all around the year.
The year, verily, is manly vigour;
he obtains the manly vigour that is the year itself.
They are nine.
Nine are the breaths;
he places the breaths in himself.
They should belong to Indra; one desiring to prosper should sacrifice [with them].
Yonder sun, verily, is Indra;
he prospered by means of these;
he placed fiery splendour and manly vigour in himself by means of these.
He indeed prospers, he places fiery splendour and manly vigour in himself, who knowing thus sacrifices with these.
He should seize a tenth one belonging to Prajāpati, of all forms, when the year has come round,
for the attainment of the year.
Prajāpati is the womb;
he is established in the womb itself.
It is of all forms
for the attainment of all,
for the acquisition of all.
They are ten.
Ten are the breaths;
the breaths are manly vigour;
he is established in manly vigour itself.
One desirous of food should seize two milch-cows belonging to Heaven and Earth, having a common mother.
Heaven and Earth, verily, rule over food.
From yonder it rains, in this one [earth] it is established.
These two are the bestowers of food;
he approaches these two with their share;
these two bestow food upon him.
They are having a common mother;
for these two are, as it were, having a common mother.
On the next day he should seize for these two a calf dedicated to Vāyu.
Vāyu, verily, is the calf of these two;
he causes these two to swell;
he causes [them] to give [milk].
He approaches him with his share;
he causes these two to give [milk] to him.
One desirous of spiritual lustre should seize [an animal] with a white back dedicated to Bṛhaspati.
Bṛhaspati, verily, is sacred power;
Bṛhaspati is the bestower of spiritual lustre.
He approaches him with his share;
he bestows spiritual lustre upon him.
A back, as it were, he becomes, who prospers.
In that it has a white back,
he makes him a back itself.
A younger brother who desires precedence should seize [an animal] black with a blaze dedicated to the Aśvins.
The Aśvins, verily, were younger brothers among the gods;
they reached the forefront.
The Aśvins are the deity of this one who is a younger brother;
he takes hold from behind of them;
they lead him around to the forefront.
He who is a younger brother is seized by evil;
evil is black, as it were.
In that it is black,
he strikes away evil itself.
In that it has a blaze,
thereby he places fiery splendour right at the front.
One who is sick should seize [an animal] black with a blaze dedicated to the Aśvins.
The Aśvins, verily, are the physicians of the gods;
the Aśvins are the deity of this one who is sick;
he approaches them with their share;
they heal him.
He who is sick is seized by evil;
evil is black, as it were.
In that it is black,
he strikes away evil itself.
In that it has a blaze,
thereby he places fiery splendour right at the front.
One desirous of offspring or desirous of cattle should seize an unmanly [animal] dedicated to Tvaṣṭṛ.
Prajāpati, verily, desiring to emit offspring,
did not find a second pair.
Having made this form, he united with himself by means of his thumb;
thence he emitted offspring.
Tvaṣṭṛ is the producer of the pair;
he approaches him with his share;
he produces cattle for him from this pair.
By this form, verily, Prajāpati emitted offspring;
by the very form by which Prajāpati emitted offspring, from that very form he is propagated with offspring and cattle.
He who is afraid of impotence should seize an unmanly [animal] dedicated to Indra.
Indra, verily, struck away this skin, this evil;
he approaches him with his share;
he strikes away from him that skin, that evil.
A bull is the sacrificial fee;
a bull, verily, is manly vigour;
he places manly vigour in him.

The metres, verily, envied the sacrifice.
The Vaṣaṭ-call, having approached, cut off the head of the Gāyatrī.
Then the sap that flowed out became a barren cow.
Because it flowed according to will (vaśam), that is the nature of the barren cow (vaśā).
The blood, verily, which flowed first,
that Bṛhaspati received;
she became the reddish one;
therefore she belongs to Bṛhaspati.
That which flowed second, Mitra and Varuṇa received;
she became the two-formed one;
therefore she belongs to Mitra and Varuṇa.
That which flowed third, the All-gods received;
she became the multiform one;
therefore she belongs to the All-gods.
That which sprinkled upward, Bṛhaspati received;
he became an ox (ukṣan);
that is the nature of an ox (ukṣatva).
That which escaped beyond, those are these other forms.
One desirous of spiritual lustre should seize a reddish [cow] dedicated to Bṛhaspati.
He is deprived of sap, verily, who, being fit for spiritual lustre, does not possess spiritual lustre.
This sap of the metres is sacred power, [is] Bṛhaspati;
by sacred power itself he places fiery splendour and sap in him;
he becomes possessed of spiritual lustre.
One desirous of rain should seize a two-formed [cow] dedicated to Mitra and Varuṇa.
Mitra and Varuṇa, verily, are day and night;
Parjanya rains following day and night;
for he rains by night or by day.
These two rule over rain;
he approaches them with their share;
they, pleased, bring down rain for him.
One desirous of offspring should seize a two-formed [cow] dedicated to Mitra and Varuṇa.
Mitra and Varuṇa, verily, are day and night; offspring are born following day and night;
for they are born by night or by day.
He propagates them following day and night.
He should seize a multiform [cow] dedicated to the All-gods for desires.
The All-gods, verily, are desires;
he approaches them with their share;
they grant him all desires.
One desiring to prosper should seize an ox dedicated to Bṛhaspati.
He is deprived of sap, verily, who, being fit for prosperity, does not prosper.
This sap of the metres is sacred power, [is] Bṛhaspati;
by sacred power itself he places prosperity and sap in him;
he indeed prospers.
One desirous of lustre should seize a white [cow] dedicated to Sūrya.
Yonder sun, verily, is the bestower of lustre;
he approaches him with his share;
he bestows lustre upon him.
Practising sorcery, he should seize a brown [cow] dedicated to Brahmaṇaspati.
Brahmaṇaspati, verily, is sacred power;
by sacred power itself he directs [sorcery] against him.
This, verily, is the form of sacred power: that it is brown;
by the form itself he whets it.

You are taken with a support.
Sitting on wood, sitting among men, sitting in life, I take you agreeable to Indra.
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
You are taken with a support.
Sitting in waters, sitting in ghee, sitting in beings, I take you agreeable to Indra.
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
You are taken with a support.
Sitting on earth, sitting in the intermediate space, sitting in the firmament, I take you agreeable to Indra.
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
You are taken with a support;
O cup, beneficial to all men, within the sage he has sat down.
The three supreme ones which were born, the divine vessel was pressed down;
of them, the uncoiled ones, I have grasped the sap, the nourishment.
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
You are taken with a support.
I have raised up the sap of the waters, the bright [fluid] gathered together in the sun;
what is the sap of the sap of the waters, that highest of yours I take.
This is your womb;
for Indra, you!
Creating deeds by this station, for he, glowing, wide, is a path to the choice [reward];
he has risen up against the foundation, the tip of the mead, when his own self sent forth the body.
You are taken with a support;
for Prajāpati, you!
This is your womb;
for Prajāpati, you!
Surely, as those possessing barley reap the barley in due order, having separated it,
make here and there the food of those who have not gone to the reverence-paying of the sacrificial grass!
You are taken with a support;
for Prajāpati, you!
This is your womb;
for Prajāpati, you!

The gods indeed saw the sacrifices separately: 'This one for me, this one for you.'
Then they all saw this [sacrifice].
They strove for it;
they ran a race for it;
Bṛhaspati won it.
Bṛhaspati, indeed, is the domestic priest of the gods.
Indeed, what priestly power the domestic priest hears, that belongs to the king;
with it he made Indra sacrifice.
He attained self-rule.
That is this self-ruling sacrifice;
he who sacrifices with this attains self-rule.
Because Bṛhaspati won it,
therefore a brāhmaṇa should sacrifice [with it];
because he made Indra sacrifice,
therefore a nobleman [should sacrifice with it].
The gods, indeed, brought forth the sacrifices separately;
then Prajāpati brought forth this one.
In it the gods desired a share;
to them he gave the meters as their victories.
Then Prajāpati sacrificed with this;
he attained self-rule.
That is this self-ruling sacrifice;
he who sacrifices with this attains self-rule.
Food, indeed, is vigor;
he who knowing thus eats food, indeed wins vigor.
Soma is the Vājapeya;
he who knowing thus drinks Soma, goes indeed to vigor.
For as many of the gods as drank Soma, they went to vigor;
therefore everyone desires to drink Soma.
Speech, indeed, is the impulse of vigor.
That speech, when created, divided fourfold.
Three quarters are in these worlds, one quarter among the beasts.
That which is in heaven, is in the Bṛhat [sāman], it is in the thunder;
that which is in the intermediate space, is in the wind, it is in the Vāmadevya [sāman];
that which is on earth, is in fire, it is in the Rathantara [sāman];
that which is among the beasts, what remained over of it, that they placed in the brāhmaṇa;
therefore the brāhmaṇa speaks both kinds of speech: divine and human.
He performs heroic deed by speech who knows thus.
By that which is in the Bṛhat and Rathantara [sāmans], the sacrifice comes to him;
by that which is among the beasts, [he attains] the sacrifice without ritual error.
Indeed, it comes to him from an unexpected quarter, who knows thus;
for he knows as much as speech is.
For by speech one gives;
by speech it is given away.
He who gains [wealth] through verses and praise-songs, from him one should not accept [gifts];
for through untruth he gains [it];
for verse is untruth, praise-song is untruth.
From an intoxicated person one should not accept [gifts];
for an intoxicated person is untruth;
for when he becomes sober, then that hurts him.

These seventeen dual cups belong to Prajāpati.
Seventeenfold, indeed, is man, belonging to Prajāpati: four limbs, head and neck, trunk, speech as the seventh, [and] ten breaths.
Upon limb after limb, indeed, evil clings to man.
In that he draws the cups alternately,
he releases him from evil from limb after limb.
Splendor, indeed, is Soma; evil is the wine cups.
In that, when the time has come, they run forward to the east with the Soma [cups], [and] backward to the west with the wine cups,
he cleanses him thoroughly from evil.
Therefore they say: 'The Vājapeya sacrificer is purified indeed,'
for he cleanses him thoroughly from evil.
These five cups are drawn;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
he wins the sacrifice itself.
They all belong to Indra;
all at once he bestows Indra-power upon him.
This is the highest food of the gods, namely Soma;
this is the lowest nourishment of men, namely wine.
In that both the Soma cups and the wine cups are drawn together,
by the highest food he wins the lower nourishment.
This is the luster of priestly power, namely Soma;
wine is the impurity of food.
In that both the Soma cups and the wine cups are drawn together,
by the luster of priestly power he places luster in himself;
by the impurity of food he strikes away impurity.
He wins the world of the gods by the Soma cups, the world of men by the wine cups.
He saves himself by the Soma cups, his wife by the wine cups.
Therefore they say: 'The Vājapeya sacrificer comes into being in yonder world,'
for by the sacrifice it has been won by him.
With the Sāvitrī verse, 'O god Savitṛ, urge forth the sacrifice, urge forth the lord of sacrifice unto good fortune,' he offers at each joint.
This is an omitted sacrifice;
who indeed knows how much of it they perform, how much not?
[It is] for wholeness, for impulse.
With 'In the impulse of vigor the great mother,' he brings down the chariot;
this is Aditi;
impelled both by her and by Savitṛ, he brings [it] down
for the winning of vigor.
With 'Within the waters is immortality, within the waters is healing,' he washes the horses;
the horses are born of the waters;
from their own womb he brings them forth;
and he makes them fit for offering, worthy of sacrifice.
With 'May Vāyu [yoke] thee, or may Manu [yoke] thee,' he yokes them;
a human is not worthy to yoke them;
with the deities he yokes them.
With 'O Son of Waters, of swift impulse,' he wipes their foreheads;
he repeats what has already been said before.

Now these additional cups [are drawn],
for the securing of whatever supreme food is not yet secured.
Now these animal victims [are offered],
for the securing of the sacrificial rites.
In that there is one sacred to Agni,
he thereby attains the Agniṣṭoma;
for the Agniṣṭoma is established in Agni.
In that there is one sacred to Indra and Agni,
he thereby attains the Ukthas;
for the Ukthas belong to Indra and Agni.
In that there is one sacred to Indra,
he thereby attains the Ṣoḍaśin;
for the Ṣoḍaśin belongs to Indra.
He wins the self by the Agniṣṭoma, out-breath and in-breath by the Uktha, vigor by the chant of the Ṣoḍaśin, speech by the Atirātra, offspring by the chant of the Bṛhat.
In this world he establishes himself by the Agniṣṭoma, in the intermediate space by the Uktha, in the heavenly world by the chant of the Ṣoḍaśin, in the paths leading to the gods by the Atirātra, in the firmament by the chant of the Bṛhat.
So many, indeed, are the divine worlds;
as many divine worlds as there are, them he attains.
Now this ewe sacred to Sarasvatī [is offered],
for the securing of yonder seventeenth chant which is not yet secured.
Now this dappled barren cow sacred to the Maruts [is offered].
The gods and the asuras were in this world;
half were the gods, half were the asuras.
Those gods saw this dappled barren cow sacred to the Maruts.
They seized it;
by it they tore away the second world, the world of the asuras.
Of whomsoever they cut off [the sacrificial portion], his is the divine world; of whom they do not cut off, his is the asura world.
By this [victim] he tears away the second world, the world of the rival;
for the rival is in the second world.
The victim sacred to Sarasvatī is the last of these five;
the victim sacred to Sarasvat is the last of the other seventeen.
Sarasvatī, indeed, is speech;
with speech he extends the sacrifice.
What of the sacrifice a knowing one does not perform, or what an unknowing one omits, that is a flaw;
that he arranges with speech, with Sarasvatī.
These seventeen animal victims sacred to Prajāpati are seized.
Seventeenfold is Prajāpati;
he obtains Prajāpati.
They are of one color;
he places vigor in him in a single manner.
The victim sacred to Sarasvat is the last;
Sarasvatī, indeed, is speech;
he establishes himself in speech.

The sāman is the Rathantara,
for the winning of blessings.
This [earth], indeed, is the Rathantara;
upon it he is anointed.
The Vājapeya, indeed, is out of womb [in origin], being enunciated, belonging to Prajāpati,
having an enunciated sāman;
thereby it is out of womb.
In that the morning pressings are unenunciated,
thereby it has a womb.
The morning pressings are unenunciated;
inenunciated is Prajāpati;
he obtains Prajāpati.
They praise with verses containing the word 'prize' (vājavatī) at midday.
Food, indeed, is the prize,
for the securing of food.
[They praise] with verses containing the word 'bright' (citravatī) at the Ārbhava-pavamāna,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
The Āṣṭrādam̐ṣṭra is the last of the Ukthas;
he connects it with the subsequent two chants.
The sāman is the Bṛhat.
What is redundant, that is redundant towards yonder world;
the Bṛhat, indeed, is capable of attaining yonder world.
The Bṛhat is virility, vigor;
he firmly establishes himself in virility, in vigor.
They praise on verses to Viṣṇu containing [the name] Śipiviṣṭa.
This, indeed, is Prajāpati's form that abides in cattle: that which contains [the name] Śipiviṣṭa.
Therefore they praise on verses containing [the name] Śipiviṣṭa.
Redundant, indeed, is this; redundant is Śipiviṣṭa;
by means of the redundant he obtains the redundant.
// Thus the fourteenth chapter, named Vājapeya, in the Iṭhimikā of the Caraka recension of the glorious Yajus, in the Kāṭhaka, is complete.

There is a vessel made from a naturally fallen branch of an aśvattha tree.
They milk a white [cow] having a white calf.
It solidifies by itself, is churned by itself, dissolves by itself.
Then he pours out the oblation sacred to Mitra and Bṛhaspati.
He divides those rice grains into two parts.
Those that are smaller, they cook into that caru sacred to Bṛhaspati.
When that is cooked, then, having covered that aśvattha vessel, he pours in clarified butter using a strainer.
There he pours the larger ones.
That caru sacred to Mitra becomes cooked by itself.
They make half the altar;
half is naturally made. They mow half the sacrificial grass;
half is naturally cut. They make half the fuel;
half is naturally made.
A white-backed [bull] is the sacrificial gift for the [offering] sacred to Bṛhaspati;
a horse for that sacred to Mitra,
or that white [cow] having a white calf.
For Agni the lord of the house (gṛhapati), an [offering] of fast-growing rice on eight potsherds;
for Savitṛ the impeller, an [offering] of satīna-peas on eight potsherds;
for Soma the lord of the forest, a caru of śyāmāka-grain;
for Bṛhaspati the lord of speech, a caru of wild rice (nīvāra);
for Indra the eldest, an [offering] of year-old rice on eleven potsherds;
for Mitra the lord of truth, a caru of āmbā-grain;
for Varuṇa the lord of order, an [offering] of barley on ten potsherds;
for Rudra the lord of cattle, a caru of gavidhukā-seeds.
'May Savitṛ impel you of impellings;
Agni of household matters;
Soma of the trees of the forest;
Bṛhaspati of speeches;
Indra of seniorities;
Mitra of truths;
Varuṇa of orders;
Rudra of cattle.
O gods, impel this one, free from rivals, to great dominion, to great seniority, to great kingship, to great rule over the people, to great overlordship of the whole universe!'

'Divine Waters, child of the waters!
You are givers of the realm;
grant the realm, svāhā!
You are the bull-wave;
you are givers of the realm;
grant the realm, svāhā!
You are the bull-army;
you are givers of the realm;
grant the realm, svāhā!
You are the lord of the waters;
you are givers of the realm;
grant the realm, svāhā!
You are never failing;
you are overflowing;
you are full of vigor;
you dwell in the fold;
you have the luster of the sun;
you have the skin of the sun;
you are soothing;
you are subject [to our will];
you are mighty;
you are the vigor of the Maruts;
you are supporters of people;
you are supporters of all;
you are unassailable, the sap of waters and plants;
you are most famed;
you are givers of the realm;
grant the realm, svāhā!'
'I have taken the divine waters rich in sweetness, full of strength, attentive to the royal consecration (rājasūya), / with which they sprinkled Mitra and Varuṇa, with which they led Indra across his foes.'
'Divine Waters rich in sweetness, mingle together, winning great dominion for the kṣatriya! / Sit, unassailable, full of strength, bestowing great splendor upon the kṣatriya!'
'You are unsuppressed, kin of speech, born of heat, / the gift of Soma.
Bright ones, I purify you with the bright; / shining ones, I purify you with the shining.
May the divine Savitṛ purify you with an unbroken strainer, with the rays of the sun, / svāhā, you waters of the royal consecration!'
'Feasting together, splendid, unassailable, one in strength, clad in water, / Varuṇa made a dwelling in the rivers, the child of the waters, within the most motherly ones.'
'O Rudra, whatever highest name of yours is Krivi, unto that it is offered;
it is sacrificed to Yama, svāhā!
Soma, Indra, Varuṇa, Mitra, Agni—may these gods who uphold the order uphold the order; may they impel speech for him!'

The bahiṣpavamāna is ninefold; the ājya [stotras] are fifteenfold;
the midday pavamāna is fifteenfold; the pṛṣṭha [stotras] are seventeenfold;
the ārbhava pavamāna is seventeenfold; the agniṣṭoma [sāman] is twenty-one-fold.
The pavamānas of the abhiṣecanīya are thirty-four-fold; the ājyas are fifteenfold;
the pṛṣṭhas are seventeenfold; the agniṣṭoma is twenty-one-fold, and the ukthas [likewise]; the entire daśapeya is seventeenfold.
The bahiṣpavamāna of the keśavapanīya is twenty-one-fold; the ājyas are seventeenfold;
the midday pavamāna is seventeenfold; the pṛṣṭhas are fifteenfold;
the ārbhava pavamāna is fifteenfold; the agniṣṭoma is ninefold, and the ukthas [likewise]; the ṣoḍaśin is twenty-one-fold; the night is fifteenfold; the sandhi is ninefold.
Then this two-night [rite] is the vyuṣṭi; as many as are the days and nights of the year, so many are its stotra-verses.

Agni surveyed the front of the dawns; the first Jātavedas [surveyed] the days; / and you have stretched along the rays of the sun in many places, along heaven and earth.
Having traversed the path, the courser shakes off all assailants;
he seeks to perceive Agni in the great seat with his eye.
Stepping onto the earth, o courser, seek Agni with your luster;
having covered [it] with the ground, tell us from where we may dig him.
The sky is your back, the earth your seat, the intermediate space your breath, the ocean your womb;
having discerned with your eye, step upon the assailants.
Step up for great good fortune from this place, o wealth-giving courser;
may we be in the favor of the earth, digging Agni in her lap.
The wealth-giving courser has stepped up, near to the good realm, well made, of the earth;
thence may we dig Agni of beautiful appearance, climbing up to heaven, onto the highest firmament.
I sprinkle Agni with thought, with ghee, who dwells facing all worlds, / broad, large with transverse vigor, widest food, violent, discerning.
I sprinkle you with speech, with ghee; with mind free from demons may you enjoy that;
Agni, of youthful beauty, of desirable color, not to be assailed, bristling with his body.
The sage Agni, lord of the prize, has stepped around the oblations, / bestowing treasures upon the giver.
Around you, o sage Agni, o mighty one, we place a fortress, / of bold color day by day, the slayer of the deceitful.
You, o Agni, with days; you, desiring to shine; you from the waters; you out of the stone;
you from the trees; you from the plants; you, manly-minded, are born pure from men.

Let flow the divine waters, sweet, for the health of offspring;
from their resting-place let the plants arise, bearing good fruit.
May Vāyu Mātariśvan unite your heart that has expanded, you who are spread out upward;
you who move with the breath of the gods, to that one, o god, be vaṣaṭ, to you.
Well-born with light, he has reached the sun, the shelter and armor;
put on the garment of all forms, o Agni, radiant with light.
Arise, favored by sacrifice, with divine grace toward us;
and to be seen with great light, with shining glows, o Agni, come with high praises.
Stand upright for our aid, like god Savitṛ;
upright, bestower of prize, when with anointed celebrants we call upon [you].
Born as a child of the two worlds, o Agni, lovely, distributed among the plants,
a brilliant infant anointed through the darknesses, roaring, you have gone forth from the mothers.
Be firm, strong-limbed; be swift, o courser, racer;
be broad, easy to sit upon, bearer of Agni's soil.
Be kind to human offspring, o Aṅgiras;
do not scorch heaven and earth, nor the intermediate space, nor the trees.
Let the courser go forth, whinnying, braying, flying, the donkey;
bearing Agni who is in the soil, may he not fall before his lifespan.
The bull bearing the bull Agni, the child of the waters, oceanic;
o Agni, come to the feast.
Ṛta is truth, ṛta is truth.
O plants, receive this Agni, who comes kindly here toward you;
driving away all languor and diseases, sitting down, strike away ill will from us.
O plants, rejoice, having flowers and good fruit;
this your timely child has sat down in his ancient seat.
Shining brightly with broad radiance, drive back foes, demons, and diseases;
may I be in the refuge of well-protecting, lofty Agni, easy to invoke, under his guidance.
Since the waters are joyous, bestow sap upon us,
for great and delightful sight.
Whatever is your most auspicious essence, share it with us here,
like loving mothers.
To that one we would go swiftly, to whose house you enliven [us];
and, o waters, produce [vigor] for us.

Seen, the gold disc has shone out widely, shining for glory, unendurable life;
Agni became immortal through his powers when heaven, rich in seed, begot him.
Night and Dawn, like-minded, of different forms, coming together, nurse the one child;
the gold disc shines between heaven and earth; the gods, givers of wealth, maintained Agni.
The sage puts on all forms; he has impelled good fortune for two-footed and four-footed;
Savitṛ, the choice one, has looked out upon the firmament; he shines following the march of Dawn.
You are the eagle, winged;
the Trivṛt is your head;
the Gāyatra is your eye;
the Bṛhat and Rathantara are your wings;
the Stoma is your body;
the meters are your limbs;
the Yajus formulas are your name;
the Vāmadevya Sāman is your form;
the Yajñāyajñīya is your tail;
the hearth-mounds are your hooves;
you are the eagle, winged;
go to heaven;
fly to the light!
You are the stride of Viṣṇu that slays the rival;
mount the Gāyatrī meter;
stride along the earth!
You are the stride of Viṣṇu that slays the assailant;
mount the Triṣṭubh meter;
stride along the atmosphere!
You are the stride of Viṣṇu that slays the ill-wisher;
mount the Jagatī meter;
stride along heaven!
You are the stride of Viṣṇu that slays the enemy;
mount the Anuṣṭubh meter;
stride along the quarters!
Agni roared like thundering heaven, licking the earth, anointing the plants;
born instantly, kindled, he looked abroad; he shines between both worlds with his beam.
O Agni, returning one, return to us with life, with luster, with offspring, with wealth, with gain, with wisdom.
O Agni, Aṅgiras, let there be a hundred turnings to you, a thousand returns to you;
then with the thriving of prosperity bring back to us what was lost, bring back to us wealth!
Return again with vigor, return again, o Agni, with nourishment and life;
protect us again from distress!
Return together with wealth, o Agni; swell with a stream
all-nourishing from every side!
I have brought you; you have been inside; stand firm, unvacillating;
let all the clans desire you; establish sovereignty among us!
Untie the highest fetter from us, o Varuṇa, the lowest, loosen the middle one;
then may we, o Āditya, in your vow, be sinless for Aditi.
At the head of the dawns he stood high, upright, having gone forth from darkness he came with light;
Agni, with handsome limbs, with his gleaming beam, as soon as born filled all dwellings.
The goose sitting in the pure, the Vasu sitting in the atmosphere, the Hotṛ sitting at the altar, the guest sitting in the house,
sitting among men, sitting in the best [place], sitting in truth, sitting in the sky, born of water, born of kine, born of truth, born of the rock, the truth!
Sit in the lap of this mother, knowing all pathways, o Agni;
do not scorch her with heat, nor with flame; shine within her with bright light!
Within the pot, o Agni, in your own seat with luster,
heating her with heat, o Jātavedas, be kind!
Having become kind to me, o Agni, then sit down kind;
having made all quarters kind, sit here in your own womb!

From heaven first Agni was born, from us the second time Jātavedas;
the third time in the waters the manly-minded one; kindling him unceasingly, the devout person begets him.
We know your three threefold forms, o Agni; we know your seat distributed in many places;
we know that spring from which you have come; we know your highest name which is in secret.
In the ocean the manly-minded one, watching over men, kindled you within the waters, o Agni, in the udder of heaven;
the buffaloes gladdened you standing in the third realm, in the womb of truth.
Agni roared like thundering heaven, licking the earth, anointing the plants;
born instantly, kindled, he looked abroad; he shines between both worlds with his beam.
Eager, purifying, the messenger, wise, Agni the immortal was set among mortals;
the ruddy one sends up smoke, bearing it repeatedly, reaching up to heaven with bright radiance.
Raiser of splendors, foundation of riches, impeller of inspired thoughts, guardian of Soma,
the Vasu, son of strength, king in the waters, shines at the head of the dawns, being kindled.
The king of the whole world was born; being born, he filled both worlds;
the rock, passing away, split even the firm [pen], when the five peoples sacrificed to Agni.
Whoever today shall make for you, o god Agni of auspicious radiance,
a cake with ghee,
lead him further forward toward greater wealth, toward god-ordained brilliance, o youngest one!
Make him share in glorious renown, o Agni; make him share in the recited hymn;
he shall be dear to the sun, dear to Agni; he shall burst forth with what is born, with what is to be born.
Seen, the gold disc has shone out widely, shining for glory, unendurable life;
Agni became immortal through his powers when heaven, rich in seed, begot him.
You, o Agni, the sacrificers have day by day taken as all good things to be chosen;
desiring wealth along with you, the eager ones opened the pen rich in kine.

This here in front is Bhū [the earth];
his breath is derived from Bhū;
spring is derived from breath;
the gāyatrī belongs to spring;
from the gāyatrī comes the Gāyatra [sāman];
from the Gāyatra comes the Upāṃśu [cup];
from the Upāṃśu comes the Trivṛt [stoma];
from the Trivṛt comes the Rathantara [sāman];
Vasiṣṭha is the seer.
Through you, grasped by Prajāpati, I take breath for creatures.
This on the south is Viśvakarman;
his mind is derived from Viśvakarman;
summer is derived from mind;
the triṣṭubh belongs to summer;
from the triṣṭubh comes the Svāra [sāman];
from the Svāra comes the Antaryāma [cup];
from the Antaryāma comes the Pañcadaśa [stoma];
from the Pañcadaśa comes the Bṛhat [sāman];
Bharadvāja is the seer.
Through you, grasped by Prajāpati, I take mind for creatures.
This in the west is Viśvavyacas [all-embracing];
his eye is derived from Viśvavyacas;
the rains are derived from the eye;
the jagatī belongs to the rainy season;
from the jagatī comes the Ṛk-sama [sāman];
from the Ṛk-sama comes the Śukra [cup];
from the Śukra comes the Saptadaśa [stoma];
from the Saptadaśa comes the Vairūpa [sāman];
Jamadagni is the seer.
Through you, grasped by Prajāpati, I take the eye for creatures.
This on the north is Svar [light];
his ear is derived from Svar;
autumn is derived from the ear;
the anuṣṭubh belongs to autumn;
from the anuṣṭubh comes the Aiḍa [sāman];
from the Aiḍa comes the Manthin [cup];
from the Manthin comes the Ekaviṃśa [stoma];
from the Ekaviṃśa comes the Vairāja [sāman];
Viśvāmitra is the seer.
Through you, grasped by Prajāpati, I take the ear for creatures.
This above is Mati [thought];
her speech is derived from Mati;
winter is derived from speech;
the paṅkti belongs to winter;
from the paṅkti comes the Nidhanavat [sāman];
from the Nidhanavat comes the Āgrāyaṇa [cup];
from the Āgrāyaṇa come the Triṇava and Trayastriṃśa [stomas];
from the Triṇava and Trayastriṃśa come the Śākvara and Raivata [sāmans];
Viśvakarman is the seer.
Through you, grasped by Prajāpati, I take speech for creatures.
Fill the space, fill the gap, and then sit firm!
Indra, Agni, and Bṛhaspati have seated you in this womb.
These dappled cows, well-milking, mix the Soma
at the birth of the gods, the clans, in the three realms of heaven.

The eighteen-month-old calf is the age;
the gāyatrī is the meter.
The two-year-old bull is the age;
the triṣṭubh is the meter.
The thirty-month-old calf is the age;
the virāj is the meter.
The three-year-old bull is the age;
the uṣṇih is the meter.
The four-year-old bull is the age;
the anuṣṭubh is the meter.
The five-year-old bull is the age;
the bṛhatī is the meter.
The draft ox is the age;
the kakubh is the meter.
The milch cow is the age;
the jagatī is the meter.
The draft bullock is the age;
the paṅkti is the meter.
The bull is the age;
the satobṛhatī is the meter.
The he-goat is the age;
the yuvala is the meter.
The ram is the age;
the viśāla is the meter.
The tiger is the age;
the unassailable is the meter.
The lion is the age;
the chadis is the meter.
Man is the age;
the tandra is the meter.
Dominion is the age;
the mayanda is the meter.
The support is the age;
the sovereign is the meter.
The summit is the age;
Prajāpati is the meter.
Viśvakarman is the age;
Parameṣṭhin is the meter.

Indra and Agni, do you two strengthen the unswerving brick.
With your back you force apart heaven and earth and the intermediate space.
You are the queen, the eastern quarter;
you are the sovereign ruler, the southern quarter;
you are the imperial ruler, the western quarter;
you are the self-ruler, the northern quarter;
you are the overlady, the high quarter.
Protect my life;
protect my out-breath;
protect my through-breath;
protect my in-breath;
protect my sight;
protect my hearing;
swell my mind;
quicken my speech;
protect my self;
grant me light.
Mā is the meter;
pramā is the meter;
pratimā is the meter;
asrīvayas is the meter;
paṅkti is the meter;
uṣṇih is the meter;
anuṣṭubh is the meter;
virāj is the meter;
bṛhatī is the meter;
gāyatrī is the meter;
triṣṭubh is the meter;
jagatī is the meter;
the earth is the meter;
the intermediate space is the meter;
heaven is the meter;
the year is the meter;
the lunar mansions are the meter;
speech is the meter;
mind is the meter;
agriculture is the meter;
gold is the meter;
the cow is the meter;
the she-goat is the meter;
the horse is the meter.
Agni is the deity;
Vāta is the deity;
Sūrya is the deity;
Candramas is the deity;
the Vasus are the deity;
the Rudras are the deity;
the Ādityas are the deity;
the Maruts are the deity;
the All-Gods are the deity;
Indra is the deity;
Varuṇa is the deity;
Bṛhaspati is the deity.
You are the summit, the ruler;
you are firm, supportive;
you are the sustainer, the bearer.
For life [I place] you;
for splendor [I place] you;
for agriculture [I place] you;
for security [I place] you.
The controller is the ruler;
you are the controller, the restrainer;
you are firm, the maintainer.
For food [I place] you;
for nourishment [I place] you;
for wealth [I place] you;
for prosperity [I place] you.

The swift is the trivṛt;
the shining is the fifteenfold;
the sky is the seventeenfold;
the forward-hasting is the eighteenfold;
heat is the nineteenfold;
the attacking is the twentyfold;
the supporter is the twenty-onefold;
splendor is the twenty-twofold;
the collecting is the twenty-threefold;
the womb is the twenty-fourfold;
the embryos are the twenty-fivefold;
might is the twenty-sevenfold;
resolve is the thirty-onefold;
the foundation is the thirty-threefold;
the vault of the tawny sun is the thirty-fourfold;
the firmament is the thirty-sixfold;
the turning away is the forty-eightfold;
the support is the catuṣṭoma.
You are the share of Agni;
the lordship is of the consecration;
the brāhmaṇa is won;
the trivṛt is the stoma.
You are the share of Indra;
the lordship is of Viṣṇu;
the kṣatriya is won;
the fifteenfold is the stoma.
You are the share of the men-watchers;
the lordship is of Dhātṛ;
the place of origin is won;
the seventeenfold is the stoma.
You are the share of Mitra;
the lordship is of Varuṇa;
the rain of heaven and the wind are won;
the twenty-onefold is the stoma.
You are the share of the Vasus;
the lordship is of the Rudras;
the quadruped is won;
the twenty-fourfold is the stoma.
You are the share of the Ādityas;
the lordship is of the Maruts;
the embryos are won;
the twenty-fivefold is the stoma.
You are the share of Aditi;
the lordship is of Pūṣan;
might is won;
the twenty-sevenfold is the stoma.
You are the share of the divine Savitṛ;
the lordship is of Bṛhaspati;
the matching quarters are won;
the catuṣṭoma is the stoma.
You are the share of the barley-months;
the lordship is of the non-barley-months;
offspring are won;
the forty-fourfold is the stoma.
You are the share of the Ṛbhus;
the lordship is of all the gods;
the created world is quieted;
the thirty-threefold is the stoma.

With one they praised;
creatures were produced;
Prajāpati was the overlord.
With three they praised;
the sacred word was created;
Brahmaṇaspati was the overlord.
With five they praised;
beings were created;
the Lord of Beings was the overlord.
With seven they praised;
the seven seers were created;
Dhātṛ was the overlord.
With nine they praised;
the fathers were created;
Aditi was the overlady.
With eleven they praised;
the seasonal [periods] were created;
the seasons were the overlords.
With thirteen they praised;
the months were created;
the year was the overlord.
With fifteen they praised;
the royal power was created;
Indra was the overlord.
With seventeen they praised;
cattle were created;
Bṛhaspati was the overlord.
With nineteen they praised;
the Śūdra and the Ārya were created;
day and night were the overladies.
With twenty-one they praised;
solid-hoofed cattle were created;
Varuṇa was the overlord.
With twenty-three they praised;
small cattle were created;
Pūṣan was the overlord.
With twenty-five they praised;
wild animals were created;
Vāyu was the overlord.
With twenty-seven they praised;
heaven and earth separated;
the Vasus and Rudras came along after them;
they alone were the overlords.
With twenty-nine they praised;
trees were created;
Soma was the overlord.
With thirty-one they praised;
creatures were created;
the dark and bright fortnights were the overlords.
With thirty-three they praised;
beings found rest;
Prajāpati the Supreme was the overlord.

O Agni, thrust away our born rivals; repel the unborn, O Jātavedas;
speak in our favor, favorably minded, without anger; may we be in your three-armored shelter when rising up.
With might thrust away our born rivals; repel the unborn, O Jātavedas;
speak in our favor, being well disposed; may we prosper; thrust away our rivals.
The forty-fourfold is the stoma;
splendor is the wealth.
The sixteenfold is the stoma;
might is the wealth.
You are the loose earth of the earth, named apnas; may all the gods praise you as such.
Rest here, backed by stomas, rich in ghee; sacrifice wealth for us, accompanied by offspring.
Eva is the meter;
varivas is the meter;
śambhū is the meter;
paribhū is the meter;
ācchad is the meter;
manas is the meter;
vyacas is the meter;
sindhu is the meter;
samudra is the meter;
sarira is the meter;
kakubh is the meter;
trikakubh is the meter;
kāvya is the meter;
aṅkupa is the meter;
kṣurobhraja is the meter;
akṣarapaṅkti is the meter;
padapaṅkti is the meter;
viṣṭārapaṅkti is the meter;
ācchad is the meter;
pracchad is the meter;
saṃyac is the meter;
viyac is the meter;
bṛhat is the meter;
rathantara is the meter;
nikāya is the meter;
vīvadha is the meter;
giras is the meter;
bhrājas is the meter;
saṃstup is the meter;
anuṣṭup is the meter;
eva is the meter;
varivas is the meter;
vayas is the meter;
vayaskṛt is the meter;
viśāla is the meter;
viṣpardhas is the meter;
chadis is the meter;
dūrohaṇa is the meter;
tandra is the meter;
aṅkāṅka is the meter.

You are the ray;
for dwelling [I take] you;
quicken dwelling.
You are forward movement;
for order [I take] you;
quicken order.
You are following;
for heaven [I take] you;
quicken heaven.
You are the junction;
for the intermediate space [I take] you;
quicken the intermediate space.
You are the support;
for the earth [I take] you;
quicken the earth.
You are the prop;
for rain [I take] you;
quicken rain.
You are the forward blower;
for the day [I take] you;
quicken the day.
You are the blower along;
for the night [I take] you;
quicken the night.
You are the willing one;
for the Vasus [I take] you;
quicken the Vasus.
You are the beacon;
for the Rudras [I take] you;
quicken the Rudras.
You are the bright light;
for the Ādityas [I take] you;
quicken the Ādityas.
You are might;
for the fathers [I take] you;
quicken the fathers.
You are the thread;
for offspring [I take] you;
quicken offspring.
You are the conqueror of armies;
for cattle [I take] you;
quicken cattle.
You are the wealthy one;
for plants [I take] you;
quicken plants.
You are the victorious one, with pressing stones yoked;
for Indra [I take] you;
quicken Indra.
You are the overlord;
for out-breath [I take] you;
quicken out-breath.
You are the supporter;
for in-breath [I take] you;
quicken in-breath.
You are the creeper;
for sight [I take] you;
quicken sight.
You are the granter of vigor;
for hearing [I take] you;
quicken hearing.
You are the trivṛt;
for the trivṛt [I take] you.
You are the savṛt;
for the savṛt [I take] you.
You are the pravṛt;
for the pravṛt [I take] you.
You are the anūvṛt;
for the anūvṛt [I take] you.
You are the growing together;
you are the growing apart;
you are the growing forth;
you are the growing after;
you are Vasuka;
you are Vasyaṣṭi;
you are Veṣaśrī;
at the impulse of the god Savitṛ, praise Bṛhaspati.

You are the queen,
the eastern quarter;
the Vasus are your divine overlords;
Agni is the resister of weapons.
May the threefold stoma support you on earth;
may the ājya uktha prop [you] for steadfastness;
[may] the Rathantara sāman [support you in] the intermediate space for firm standing.
May the first-born seers among the gods spread you out according to the measure and breadth of heaven,
and this supporter and overlord;
may all of them, in agreement, seat you and the sacrificer on the back of the firmament, in the heavenly world.
You are the ruler,
the southern quarter;
the Rudras are your divine overlords;
Indra is the resister of weapons.
May the fifteenfold stoma support you on earth;
may the prauga uktha prop [you] for steadfastness;
[may] the Bṛhat sāman [support you in] the intermediate space for firm standing.
May the first-born seers among the gods spread you out according to the measure and breadth of heaven,
and this supporter and overlord;
may all of them, in agreement, seat you and the sacrificer on the back of the firmament, in the heavenly world.
You are the sovereign,
the western quarter;
the Ādityas are your divine overlords;
Soma is the resister of weapons.
May the seventeenfold stoma support you on earth;
may the marutvatīya uktha prop [you] for steadfastness;
[may] the Vairūpa sāman [support you in] the intermediate space for firm standing.
May the first-born seers among the gods spread you out according to the measure and breadth of heaven,
and this supporter and overlord;
may all of them, in agreement, seat you and the sacrificer on the back of the firmament, in the heavenly world.
You are the autonomous ruler,
the northern quarter;
the Maruts are your divine overlords;
Varuṇa is the resister of weapons.
May the twenty-onefold stoma support you on earth;
may the niṣkevalya uktha prop [you] for steadfastness;
[may] the Vairāja sāman [support you in] the intermediate space for firm standing.
May the first-born seers among the gods spread you out according to the measure and breadth of heaven,
and this supporter and overlord;
may all of them, in agreement, seat you and the sacrificer on the back of the firmament, in the heavenly world.
You are the overlady,
the lofty quarter;
the All-Gods are your divine overlords;
Bṛhaspati is the resister of weapons.
May the twenty-sevenfold and thirty-threefold stomas support you on earth;
may the vaiśvadeva and āgnimāruta ukthas prop [you] for steadfastness;
[may] the Śākvara and Raivata sāmans [support you in] the intermediate space for firm standing.
May the first-born seers among the gods spread you out according to the measure and breadth of heaven,
and this supporter and overlord;
may all of them, in agreement, seat you and the sacrificer on the back of the firmament, in the heavenly world.

This in the east is Harikeśa, the ray of the sun;
his general and troop-leader are Rathakṛtsa and Rathaujas;
Puñjikasthalā and Kṛtasthalā are his two apsarases;
sorcerers are his weapon;
monsters are his missile.
May they protect us;
may they help us;
him whom we hate and who hates us, I place in their jaws.
This in the south is Viśvakarman;
his general and troop-leader are Rathasvana and Rathecitra;
Menakā and Sahajanyā are his two apsarases;
biting beasts are his weapon;
murder by men is his missile.
May they protect us;
may they help us;
him whom we hate and who hates us, I place in their jaws.
This in the west is Vidadvasu;
his general and troop-leader are Rathaprota and Asamaratha;
Pramlocantī and Anumlocantī are his two apsarases;
tigers are his weapon;
serpents are his missile.
May they protect us;
may they help us;
him whom we hate and who hates us, I place in their jaws.
This in the north is Saṃyadvasu;
his general and troop-leader are Tārkṣya and Ariṣṭanemi;
Viśvācī and Ghṛtācī are his two apsarases;
waters are his weapon;
wind is his missile.
May they protect us;
may they help us;
him whom we hate and who hates us, I place in their jaws.
This above is Arvāgvasu;
his general and troop-leader are Senajit and Suṣeṇa;
Urvaśī and Pūrvacitti are his two apsarases;
the roaring [thunder] is his weapon;
lightning is his missile.
May they protect us;
may they help us;
him whom we hate and who hates us, I place in their jaws.

I seat you in the seat of Ayu, in the shade of the well.
Homage to the sea;
homage to the eye of the sea.
Snorting like a horse longing for pasture, when he stood abroad in the great enclosure,
then the wind blows after his flame; then may your path be black.
May Prajāpati seat you on the back of the earth, full of light;
for all in-breath, out-breath, [and] diffused breath, bestow all light;
Agni is your overlord.
May Viśvakarman seat you on the back of the intermediate space, full of light;
for all in-breath, out-breath, [and] diffused breath, bestow all light;
Vāyu is your overlord.
May Parameṣṭhin seat you on the back of heaven, full of light;
for all in-breath, out-breath, [and] diffused breath, bestow all light;
Sūrya is your overlord.
Madhu and Mādhava are the two spring seasons;
you are the internal union of Agni.
May heaven and earth be fit; may waters and plants be fit;
may the fires be fit separately, united in vow for my supremacy.
The fires that are of one mind between heaven and earth,
fitting with these two spring seasons, may they enter into [me] like the gods into Indra.
Śukra and Śuci are the two summer seasons;
Nabhas and Nabhasya are the two rainy seasons;
Iṣ and Ūrj are the two autumn seasons;
Sahas and Sahasya are the two winter seasons;
Tapas and Tapasya are the two cool seasons;
you are the internal union of Agni.
May heaven and earth be fit; may waters and plants be fit;
may the fires be fit separately, united in vow for my supremacy.
The fires that are of one mind between heaven and earth,
fitting with these two cool seasons, may they enter into [me] like the gods into Indra.
You are the measure of a thousand;
you are the counterpart of a thousand;
you are the limit of a thousand;
you belong to a thousand;
for a thousand [I take] you.
May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni:
one and ten,
ten and a hundred,
a hundred and a thousand,
a thousand and ten thousand,
ten thousand and a hundred thousand,
a hundred thousand and a million,
a million and ten million,
ten million and a hundred million,
a billion and a middle,
and the end and the highest number.
May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni.
You are the seasons, increasing the order;
you are season-abiding, touching the seasons;
dripping ghee, dripping honey,
vigorous, full of the svadhā-call,
granting wishes by the name of the Virājes.
May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni.

Homage to you sparkling ones, the hearts of the gods.
Homage to the discerning ones.
Homage to the destroyers.
Homage to the eradicators.
O runner, lord of food, poor one, dark blue and red,
of these offspring, of these men, of these beasts,
do not be afraid, do not harm, let not anything of ours be afflicted.
These prayers we offer to Rudra, the strong one with braided hair, ruling over heroes,
so that well-being may be for our two-footed and four-footed, [and] everything in this village may be nourished and free of disease.
That form of yours, O Rudra, which is auspicious, auspicious and always healing,
auspicious and healing of suffering—with that be gracious to us, that we may live.
May the missile of Rudra bypass us, may the ill-will of the furious, malevolent one bypass [us].
Unstring your stiff [bows] away from our patrons; O bountiful one, be gracious to our children and offspring.
Most bountiful one, most auspicious one, be auspicious and well-disposed to us.
Placing your weapon upon the highest tree, clad in a skin, go forth;
bearing the Pināka, go forth.
O scattering one, bright red one, homage be to you, O blessed one;
those thousands of missiles that are yours, let them strike down another than us.
A thousandfold are the thousands of missiles in your two arms;
O blessed one, being their master, make their faces turn away.
Innumerable thousands are the Rudras over the earth;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those Bhavas that are in this great billowing flood, the atmosphere;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those Rudras who are blue-necked, white-throated, having resorted to heaven;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those Śarvas who are blue-necked, white-throated, roaming below upon the earth;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those who in the woods are yellow as young grass, blue-necked, bright red;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those who pierce people in their foods and drinking vessels;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those who are overlords of spirits, hairless or braided-haired;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those protectors of paths who dwell on food and fight for grain;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those who wander about the fords, bearing darts and quivers;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Those Rudras, so many or even more, who have spread across the quarters;
at a thousand leagues away we unstring their bows.
Homage be to the Rudras who are in heaven, whose arrows are rain:
to them ten [fingers extended] to the east, ten to the south, ten to the west, ten to the north, ten upwards;
homage be to them;
may they be gracious to us;
whomsoever we hate and whoever hates us, him I place in their jaws.
Homage be to the Rudras who are in the atmosphere, whose arrows are wind:
to them ten [fingers extended] to the east, ten to the south, ten to the west, ten to the north, ten upwards;
homage be to them;
may they be gracious to us;
whomsoever we hate and whoever hates us, him I place in their jaws.
Homage be to the Rudras who are on the earth, whose arrows are food:
to them ten [fingers extended] to the east, ten to the south, ten to the west, ten to the north, ten upwards;
homage be to them;
may they be gracious to us;
whomsoever we hate and whoever hates us, him I place in their jaws.

“Now the impulse of vigor,” thus six [verses].
May all Maruts today, all with help, may all kindled fires be [with us];
may all gods come here with help, may all wealth be [ours] in this contest for vigor.
“Unto me of vigor…”
“Now of vigor…”
May vigor from the seven quarters, or from the four distances,
may vigor protect me here with all the gods in winning wealth.
May vigor impel the gift to me today, may vigor make the gods grow through oblation;
for in the impulse of vigor it bows down; as lord of vigor may I win all regions.
Vigor in front and in the middle of us, may vigor fashion the gods with the seasons;
for in the impulse of vigor it bows down; as lord of vigor may I become all regions.
I unite myself with the milk of the earth, I unite myself with waters, with plants;
so may I win vigor from Agni.
Milk on earth, milk in the plants, milk in heaven, in the intermediate space put milk;
rich in milk may the quarters be for me.

Conquering by truth, whose abode is truth, Agni is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the plants, called Delights;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
Very gracious, sun-rayed, Soma is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the asterisms, called Bekuris;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
Joined, having all sāmans, the Sun is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the rays, called Swift;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
Feeding, well-winged, the sacrifice is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the priestly fees, called Praises;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
Prajāpati, the all-maker, Mind is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the ṛk-verses and sāmans, called Desires;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
Vigorous, all-encompassing, the Wind is the gandharva;
his apsarases are the waters, called Strengths;
may he protect this our priesthood and nobility;
to him svāhā, vaṭ!
To them svāhā, vaṭ!
O lord of the world, whose dwellings are above, O lord of wide rule!
Bestow great shelter upon this priesthood, upon this nobility, svāhā!
You are the ocean, vaporous, wet-dropping;
bringing welfare, bringing delight, blow towards me, svāhā!
You belong to the Maruts, the host of Maruts;
bringing welfare, bringing delight, blow towards me, svāhā!
You desire help, you are active;
bringing welfare, bringing delight, blow towards me, svāhā!

I yoke Agni with might, with ghee, the heavenly eagle, great in vigor;
by him may we fly to the summit of the ruddy one, ascending light in the highest firmament.
These two wings of yours, unaging, winged, with which you strike away the rakṣases, O Agni,
with both may we fly to the world of the virtuous, whither went the ancient sages of old.
The drop, the skilled falcon, possessing truth, the golden-winged bird, active,
great, firmly seated in the assembly;
homage be to you;
do not harm me!
You are the head of heaven, the navel of the earth, the sap of waters and plants;
all-lived, broad shelter, homage to the path! Resting on the head of all, you stand above.
In the ocean is your heart, within life; grant water, cleave the water-holder;
from heaven, from Parjanya, from the intermediate space, from the earth,
from there protect me with rain.

May the seasons make you grow, O Agni, the years, the sages, what things are true;
shine together with heavenly radiance, illuminate all directions of the earth.
Be fully kindled, O Agni, and awake him; rise up for great good fortune;
and let not your worshiper come to harm, O Agni; may your priests be renowned, not others.
These Brahmins choose you, O Agni; be kind to us in our dwelling, O Agni;
slaying rivals, O Agni, conquering plotters, watch heedfully in your own home.
Fix wealth right here, O Agni; let not the humblers humble you, who were piled before;
may your domain be easy to manage, O Agni; may your worshiper grow unvanquished.
Take hold, O Agni, with your own dominion; strive in alliance with a friend, O Agni;
shine here, O Agni, to be invoked by many for the central position among kinsmen and kings.
Beyond insults, beyond enmities, beyond ignorance, beyond ruin today,
for do you cross over all perils, O Agni, and then give us wealth accompanied by heroes.
Invulnerable, Jātavedas, unvanquished, widely ruling, supporting dominion, shine here, O Agni;
freeing mortals from all diseases, guard us all around today with kindly [powers] for increase.
When you freed him from the realm of the beyond, from Yama's curse, O Bṛhaspati,
may the two Aśvins, the physicians of the gods, push back death from him by their powers, O Agni.
Looking upward from darkness to the higher light,
to the god among the gods, the Sun, the highest light, have we come.
O Bṛhaspati, O Savitṛ, awaken him; sharpen further him who is already sharp;
make him grow for great good fortune; may all the gods rejoice in him.
Supporting dominion, Agni, whose brilliance is unblunted, possessing a thousandfold [strength], may he shine when kindled;
upon the height of the earth, Jātavedas ascended to the intermediate space, he went to heaven.

He wipes the juhū ladle.
The juhū is indeed the head of the sacrifice;
he takes hold of the head of the sacrifice itself.
With the juhū the gods indeed called the Virāj;
that is the juhū-nature of the juhū.
Agni is indeed the Virāj;
he thus calls Agni, the Virāj.
The meters departed from the gods, desiring a portion at the sacrifice, saying: 'We will not carry the offering for you who have no shares.'
To them they gave this [ghee] taken in four ladlefuls: for the introductory verse, for the offering verse, for the deity, for the vaṣaṭ-call.
Because he offers with four ladlefuls,
he makes the meters possessors of portions.
He offers [mantras] to Savitṛ,
for instigation.
It indeed departs from the head of the sacrifice if that which does not belong to Agni is performed at the beginning of the sacrifice.
When, having taken [it] eight times, he offers these eight,
the gāyatrī has eight syllables, Agni is connected with the gāyatrī;
thereby it does not depart from the head of the sacrifice.
For instigation,
[the mantras] to Savitṛ.
'They harness the mind'—these contain the mind;
for the mind is harnessed in the beginning;
by the mind the sacrifice is extended.
'Agni is light'—this contains light.
Savitṛ indeed brought together this light of the earth;
whatever of it was lowered here, that he brings together by this.
There are six verses;
six indeed are the seasons;
he establishes himself firmly in the seasons.
There are two yajuses;
two-footed is the sacrificer;
for firm support.
If he should desire: 'I would furnish the deity with the sacrificial renown through the oblation,' he should place a verse at the end;
he furnishes the deity itself with the sacrificial renown through the oblation.
If he should desire: 'I would furnish the sacrificer with the sacrificial renown through the oblation,' he should place a yajus at the end;
he furnishes the sacrificer himself with the sacrificial renown through the oblation.
'Prosper the chant through the verse'—
for prosperity.
'The Rathantara by the Gāyatra, the Bṛhat having the Gāyatra as its course'—
this [earth] is indeed the Rathantara, that [heaven] is the Bṛhat.
By stepping along with the forward step of the oblation through attainment, the sacrificer becomes wealthier;
by stepping along with the backward step of the oblation, he becomes worse off.
Reciting all of them together at once, he should offer with the last [formula];
he steps along with the forward step of the oblation itself, he becomes wealthier.
He should offer with each one individually if he should desire: 'May the sacrificer be uniform.'
He makes the yajuses of various potencies.
These are eight, the oblation is the ninth;
he makes the head of the sacrifice threefold;
for threefold is the head of the sacrifice.
Standing upright, he offers;
for standing upright, one is more full of virility.
He offers with a full ladle;
full is Prajāpati;
he obtains Prajāpati himself.

Om.
With verses to Savitṛ he takes the spade,
for impulsion.
This [earth] is the gāyatrī, the intermediate space the triṣṭubh, the sky the jagatī, the directions the anuṣṭubh;
with the meters he turns it toward himself from these worlds.
It is sharpened on both sides.
With a plowshare sharpened on only one side, indeed, this much food is produced.
Food is Arka; Arka is Agni;
for the securing of food, of Arka.
He should make it a cubit long.
The sacrifice is measured by man;
[he makes it] measured by the sacrificial man.
He should make it a fathom long;
so great is the vigor in man;
[he makes it] measured by vigor.
He should make it of unmeasured [length],
for the securing of what is unmeasured.
He should make it from a tree that bears fruit;
this is the most vigorous of trees,
for the sake of [possessing] vigor.
He should make it spotted, of bamboo, hollow.
Agni departed from the gods;
he entered bamboo.
He put on these armors which are the joints.
He moved through this world because it is hollow.
Wherever he dwelt, that he burned out, and that became spotted.
Because it is spotted, of bamboo, and hollow,
it is most connected with Agni,
for prosperity,
and also for securing whatever [part] of him is hidden in it.

Agni departed from the gods;
wherever he went, Prajāpati looked after him.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati.
When they go with a horse,
[it is] for the discovery of Agni.
By this, indeed, the gods overcame the Asuras at the end.
When they go with a horse,
[it is] for the overcoming of the rival.
The demons did not surpass this one;
when they go with a horse,
[it is] so that the demons do not surpass [them].
They lead the horse in front, the donkey behind,
for the separation of the worse from the better;
therefore the worse follows behind the virtuous one going in front.
He gathers with a donkey;
therefore among animals putting forth seed this one is the youngest equally,
for Agni burned out its seed.
Because he gathers nourishment and Arka into this [earth] with it,
therefore this one is the longest-lived upon her.
"Run forward swiftly, O courser; harness the donkey, you two," [he says];
this refers to the horse and donkey.
"In every yoke, in every prize, we call Indra, the stronger friend, for help," [he says];
for help, for the prize, indeed, Agni is piled.
"Come hastening forward, trampling upon curses," [he says];
abundant indeed is the rival of him who thrives;
he thrives who piles Agni.
The horse is a thunderbolt;
sin, curse, is the rival;
with the thunderbolt indeed he tramples upon sin, the rival.
"In Rudra’s overlordship of the troops, being salutary, come," [he says];
animals belong to Rudra;
having begged the animals from Rudra for himself, he performs the rite.
"Traverse the wide intermediate space," [he says];
for he has the intermediate space as his deity at this time.
"Together with Pūṣan as yoke-mate," [he says];
Pūṣan indeed is the guide of paths;
for attainment.
"From the seat of the earth bring Agni, of the earth-mound, like the Aṅgirases," [he says];
Agni's dwelling is the earth-mound; the Aṅgirases gathered him at first with the deities;
he gathers him with his dwelling, with the deities.
"Let us approach Agni of the earth-mound like the Aṅgirases," he should say to whomever he meets;
every man has Agni;
he deprives him of his prize.
Having announced to Prajāpati, Agni should be piled;
having announced to the king,
for he is closest to Prajāpati.
This [earth] is Prajāpati;
her ear is the ant-hill;
when he addresses [the earth] having dug up the mound of the ant-hill,
he piles Agni having announced it to Prajāpati himself.
They hear of him having piled Agni: "So-and-so has piled Agni";
for it is an ear;
he speaks to the ear.

"Agni has looked along the forefront of the dawns," [he says],
for discovery.
"The prize-winner having reached the path shakes off all combatants," [he says];
with this [verse] he drives away combatants.
"Having stepped upon the earth, O prize-winner, seek Agni with thy light," [he says];
Prajāpati sought him with the previous [verse] and found him with the following one;
he seeks with the previous one and finds with the following one.
"The sky is thy back, the earth thy dwelling-place," [he says].
The horse belonging to Prajāpati is indeed armed with the thunderbolt;
with its hair [it surpasses] animals with teeth on both jaws, with its teeth those with teeth on one side;
with the thunderbolt indeed he holds down the rival.
Where the counterpart of the sacrifice is performed, there counterpart animals are born.
"Step forth! He has stepped forth," with two counterpart [formulas] he makes [him] step forth;
therefore counterpart animals are born;
with two;
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
Whatever oblation the Adhvaryu offers where there is no fire, the Adhvaryu becomes blind,
and the demons smite the sacrifice.
Agni desired the wives of Varuṇa;
his fiery splendor flew away;
it became gold.
When he throws down gold and offers,
he offers in what possesses Agni,
for success;
the Adhvaryu does not become blind,
and the demons do not smite the sacrifice.
"I besprinkle Agni with thought, with ghee," [he says];
thought is swifter than speech;
with thought indeed he reaches the oblation.
"Dwelling opposite all the worlds," [he says];
therefore he dwells facing toward all creatures.
"Finding the widest, impetuous food," [he says];
he makes food sweet for him.
"I besprinkle thee with speech, with ghee," [he says];
he repeats what has just been uttered before.
"Mayest thou enjoy it with a mind free of demons," [he says],
for the driving away of the demons.
"Agni of youthful splendor, of desirable appearance," [he says];
he places respect in him.
With two [verses] he offers;
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
At each head of the sacrifice, indeed, the demons wish to slay the sacrifice.
When he traces around,
[it is] for the driving away of the demons.
With three [verses] he traces around;
three are these worlds;
from these worlds he drives away the demons.
"Around the prize-lord, the poet," with a gāyatrī he traces around;
the gāyatrī is the brahman;
with the brahman he encloses it.
"Around thee, O Agni, we as a fortress," with an anuṣṭubh;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh encompasses all meters;
with speech he encloses all meters.
"Thou, O Agni, with days," with a triṣṭubh;
vigor is the triṣṭubh;
with vigor he encloses it.
With an anuṣṭubh he traces around in the middle;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
he places speech in the middle;
therefore speech speaks in the middle.
Fiery splendor is the gāyatrī,
the sacrifice is the anuṣṭubh,
sensory power is the triṣṭubh;
with fiery splendor and sensory power he encloses the sacrifice on both sides in himself.

With two [verses] to Savitṛ he digs,
for impulsion.
He finds smoke with the previous [verse], light with the following one;
with two;
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
"Thou art the back of the waters, the womb of Agni," [saying this] he takes the lotus leaf;
for this is the back of the waters, the womb of Agni.
With the lotus leaf he collects [the earth];
with his own womb he collects him,
for appeasement,
so that he will not burn through.
With a black antelope skin he collects [it];
the black antelope skin is the sacrifice;
with the sacrifice he collects the sacrifice,
so that it will not spill;
for the sacrifice does not spill in the sacrifice.
This indeed is the form of the brahman, namely the black antelope skin;
with the brahman and with the ṛc and sāman he collects him.
"Ye are shelter and ye are armor," [saying this] he spreads them;
the lotus leaf is this [earth], the black antelope skin that [sky];
these two indeed he thus spreads out.
"Both unrent and thick," [he says];
for both of these are unrent and thick.
"Capacious," [he says];
therefore these two are capacious.
"Bear the purīṣya Agni," [he says];
for he is purīṣya.
"Dwell together, O finders of light, turned toward each other in your self with your chest," [he says];
therefore these two, being distinct, face each other.
"About to bear within you Agni, full of light, never failing," [he says];
he places in him never-failing light.
"Thou art purīṣya, all-supporting," [he says];
for he is purīṣya, all-supporting.
"Atharvan first churned thee forth, O Agni," [he says];
Prajāpati is Atharvan;
Prajāpati churned him in the beginning;
Prajāpati indeed generates him.
"And thee, Dadhyañc the ṛṣi," [he says];
Dadhyañc Ātharvaṇa was full of fiery splendor;
with him he collects him.
"And thee, Pāthya the bull," [he says];
he repeats what has just been uttered before.
With four [verses] he heaps [the earth] together;
four are the meters;
with the meters he heaps him together.
With gāyatrī [verses] he should heap together for a brāhmaṇa;
for the brāhmaṇa belongs to the gāyatrī;
with triṣṭubh [verses] for a rājanya;
for the rājanya belongs to the triṣṭubh;
with jagatī [verses] for a vaiśya;
for the vaiśya belongs to the jagatī;
according to each one's meter.
With both he should heap together for one whom he might desire to become better off;
fiery splendor is the gāyatrī, sensory power the triṣṭubh;
he places fiery splendor and sensory power united in him.
With eight [verses] he heaps together;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
Agni belongs to the gāyatrī;
as great as Agni is, so great he collects him.
"Sit down, O Hotṛ," [he says];
he causes the deities to sit down in him.
"Down, O Hotṛ," [he says];
[he causes] men [to sit down].
"Sit down together," [he says];
[he causes] birds [to sit down].
"For he was born noble at the forefront of the days," [he says];
he generates gods and men seated together in him.

"Release the divine waters rich in sweetness," [he says],
for the establishment of the plants.
"May the plants, well-fruited, spring up from their place of standing," [he says];
therefore the plants bear fruit.
"May Vāyu Mātariśvan unite [thee]," [he says];
therefore Vāyu bears the rain.
Vāyu is breath;
he places breath in him.
"To him, O god, let there be vaṣaṭ for thee," [he says].
There are six seasons;
he places rain in the seasons;
therefore it rains season by season.
If he were to say "vaṣaṭ" perceptibly, the vaṣaṭ-call would become exhausted of power;
if he were not to say "vaṣaṭ", the demons would destroy the sacrifice.
"To him, O god, let there be vaṣaṭ for thee," [he says];
[uttered] imperceptibly, the vaṣaṭ-call does not become exhausted of power,
[and] the demons do not destroy the sacrifice.
If he were to offer with clarified butter, he would afflict the earth with burning heat.
He pours out water,
for appeasement, so that [the earth] will not burn through.
"Well-born with light," [he says];
now indeed he is born, when he is collected;
just when he is born, he places light in him.
"He has attained a sheltering protection, the light of heaven," [he says];
sheltering protection is the sacred word (brahman);
he establishes him in the sacred word.
"O Agni, radiant one, put on a multiform garment," [he says].
The meters are Agni's garment;
he clothes him with the meters.
The anuṣṭubh is Agni's dear body;
he clothes him with his dear body.
He who knows thus becomes a possessor of clothing.
Bound up, this [bundle] is indeed Varuṇa's wrath.
"Stand up, O thou of fair sacrifice; erect for our aid," [he says];
he impels Varuṇa's wrath upwards.
With two [verses];
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
"Being born, thou art the embryo of the two rodases," [he says].
These two [worlds] are the two rodases;
he is the embryo of these two;
he establishes him in these two.
"O Agni, lovely, distributed among the plants," [he says];
therefore Agni is along all the plants.
"Go forth from the mothers, neighing loudly," [he says].
The plants are indeed his mothers;
he leads him forth away from them.
"Be firm, strong-limbed," [he says];
he places firmness in the donkey;
therefore this one among the beasts is the best bearer of burdens.
"Be friendly to human offspring, O Aṅgiras," [he says].
Now he would scorch the offspring;
[this is] for appeasement.
"Do not scorch the sky and earth, nor the atmosphere, nor the trees," [he says];
he pacifies him towards these worlds.
"Let the prize-winner go forth, neighing loudly," [he says],
for attainment.
"Let him not fall before his life-span," [he says];
he places life in him;
therefore the donkey reaches the full span of life;
therefore, when a donkey dies, they are afraid.
"A bull bearing the bull Agni," [he says];
for as a bull he bears the bull.
"The embryo of the waters, oceanic," [he says];
for he is the embryo of the waters, oceanic.
"O Agni, come to the feast," [he says];
with Agni the gods spread this out in the beginning,
for enjoyment.
Deprived of his abode, not yet having reached firm standing, he now meditates upon the sacrificer and the Adhvaryu.
"Truth, order; truth, order," [he says];
this [earth] is order (ṛta), that [sky] is truth (satya);
he establishes him in these two.
"O plants, receive this Agni, auspicious, coming here towards you," [he says].
The plants are his share;
he unites him with them;
moreover, in those [plants] from which he displaced him, he establishes him.
"Flowering, well-fruited," [he says];
therefore the plants bear fruit.
With two [verses] he brings [it] down;
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
"With his glare," [he says], and loosens [the bundle];
he unties Varuṇa's wrath.
"Waters, you are indeed refreshing," [saying this] he brings the waters;
the waters are pacified;
with the pacified [waters] he pacifies his heat.
With three [verses];
threefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, so great is the heat of him he pacifies.
He mixes [the clay] with goat's hair.
A goat is Agni's dear body;
he mixes him with his dear dear body.
With gravel,
for firmness.
He mixes [it] with potsherds from a ruined site;
he afflicts the wild beasts with burning heat;
if he were to mix [it] with [potsherds] from a village, he would afflict domestic beasts with burning heat;
therefore these [wild beasts] are the smallest among the beasts that are born,
for they are stricken with heat.

"Mitra having united the earth," [he says];
this [earth/pot] is indeed Varuṇa's wrath;
with Mitra indeed he approaches Varuṇa's wrath.
"The Rudras having united the earth; united by the Vasus, by the Rudras," [he says];
with these deities Prajāpati indeed united it;
with them he unites it.
With some [materials] he holds out, with others he recites formulas;
he forms a pair;
and he secures the blessing of the sacrifice from the center.
"Thou art the head of Makha," [he says];
Makha is indeed the sacrifice;
he makes the head of the sacrifice.
"May the Vasus make thee with the gāyatrī metre," [he says];
with these deities Prajāpati made it;
with them he makes it.
He makes it three elevations high;
three are these worlds;
he attains these worlds.
He makes it five elevations high;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
he secures the sacrifice.
"Hold offspring in me," [he says];
this is the prayer, the milking of the metres.
He should make it a cubit in size;
the sacrifice is commensurate with man;
[it is made] commensurate with the sacrificial man.
He should make it as large as he can encompass with both arms;
this much is the virility in man;
[it is made] commensurate with virility.
He should make it a span in size;
this much is the mouth;
Agni is the mouth of the gods;
[it is made] commensurate with the mouth.
He should make it without measure;
for the securing of the unmeasured.
He should make it with four nipples;
for the milking of Aditi;
with six nipples,
for the milking of the seasons;
with eight nipples,
for the milking of the gāyatrī;
with two nipples,
for the milking of heaven and earth.
For sorcery he should make it nine-cornered;
the thunderbolt is threefold;
he hurls the thunderbolt at his rival.
"Thou art Aditi's girdle; may Aditi take thy rim," [saying this] he makes the rim with a yajus;
for humans make it without a yajus;
they distinguish it from a human vessel.
If it should break before baking, the sacrificer would suffer distress, his sacrifice would be destroyed.
"Having made the great ukhā," [he says];
he hands it over to the deities.
"May the Vasus fumigate thee with the gāyatrī metre," [he says];
with these deities Prajāpati fumigated it;
with them he fumigates it.
He fumigates it with horse dung;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati, Prajāpati is the sacrifice,
for having the same origin.
He fumigates with seven [formulas];
the ukhā is the head of the sacrifice;
seven are the vital breaths;
he places the breaths in the head;
therefore there are seven breaths in the head.

"The divine Aditi belonging to all the gods," [he says];
this [earth] is Aditi;
with Aditi he digs into Aditi,
so as not to hurt her;
for one's own does not harm one's own.
"May the wives of the gods [place] thee," [he says];
formerly the wives of the gods placed it here;
with them he places it.
"May the Dhiṣaṇās [kindle] thee," [he says];
the Dhiṣaṇās are knowledge;
with knowledge he kindles it around.
"May the Varutrīs [cook] thee," [he says];
the Varutrīs are the Hotrās;
with the Hotrās he cooks it.
"May the Gnās [bake] thee," [he says];
the Gnās are the metres;
with the metres he bakes it.
"May the wives with undamaged wings [bake] thee," [he says];
the wives are the spouses of the gods; they baked it originally;
with them he bakes it.
"May they bake it twice," he says;
therefore grain ripens twice a year.
He bakes it with six [formulas];
six indeed are the seasons; with the seasons the wives of the gods baked it;
with them he bakes it.
This [ukhā], when kindled around, is Varuṇa's wrath;
"Of Mitra who supports the peoples," [he says];
with Mitra he approaches Varuṇa's wrath.
"May god Savitṛ unearth thee," [saying this] he unearths it with a Savitṛ verse,
for impulsion.
"Unwavering on earth, fill the quarters and directions," [he says];
therefore Agni shines through all directions.
"Arise, become great, stand upright, be firm," [he says];
he makes it firm.
If it should break, the sacrificer would suffer distress, his sacrifice would be destroyed.
"O Mitra, I entrust this ukhā to thee," [he says];
Mitra is the sacred power;
to the sacred power he entrusts it.
If it should break, he should unite [the fragments] with the potsherds and make another;
that indeed is the atonement of the sacrifice.
"May the Vasus pierce thee with the gāyatrī metre," [he says];
with metres it is made, with metres it is fumigated, with metres it is baked;
the metres pierce the metres by their own abode.
A vessel that is not pierced belongs to the asuras;
when he pierces it,
he places it among the gods.
He pierces it with goat's milk;
for she is most connected with Agni;
for prosperity,
and also with the highest milk.

He pours out an [offering] on eleven potsherds to Agni and Viṣṇu.
Agni is indeed all deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he takes hold of both the deities and the sacrifice.
Agni is the mouth of the gods;
Viṣṇu is the furthest end;
he has grasped together the two ends of the sacrifice.
Then there is a caru in ghee for the Ādityas.
The Ādityas, going from here as the highest, went to that world.
These creatures are of the Ādityas, nearest to the Ādityas;
he approaches his own deity.
It is in ghee;
for the Ādityas have ghee as their portion.
Then there is [a cake] on twelve potsherds for Agni Vaiśvānara.
The year is indeed Agni Vaiśvānara;
twelve-monthed is the year;
[it is] for the attainment of the year.
Whoever piles the fire without a womb, [that fire] enters into the womb of the sacrificer;
it burns him up.
The year is indeed Agni Vaiśvānara;
the year is the womb of Agni.
In that it is for Agni Vaiśvānara,
he piles it with its womb.
This is indeed the dear body of Agni, namely the Vaiśvānara one.
In that it is for Agni Vaiśvānara,
having made an oblation for his dear body, he receives the dear body.
Agni Vaiśvānara is indeed desire.
In that he pours out [an offering] for Agni Vaiśvānara,
he attains that desire for which desire it is given.
In that it is on twelve potsherds,
twelve-monthed is the year;
the creatures that fall away, fall away from the year; those that are established, are established in the year;
having found a firm standing in the year, he bears the fire.

He offers six Ādhīta-yajus formulas.
There are six seasons;
he consecrates him by means of the seasons;
by means of the seasons he raises up its vigor.
He offers seven of these;
there are seven vital breaths;
he consecrates him by means of the vital breaths.
He offers [them] separately;
therefore the vital breaths have different potencies: the eye, the ear, speech.
He offers the last one with an anuṣṭubh;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
he places speech as the highest;
therefore speech, highest of the vital breaths, speaks what is articulated.
The anuṣṭubh is all metres;
cattle are metres;
cattle are food;
he secures cattle, food.
In that he heats [the pan] in the flame, by that he secures what has been;
in that [he heats it] on embers, by that what is to be.
It should be heated on embers;
for what is to be is greater than what has been.
"Do not break well, do not suffer damage," [he says];
he firmly establishes it.
"Rise up uninjured in this sacrifice," [he says],
for attainment.
He heats it with two [formulas];
the sacrificer is two-footed;
[this is] for support.
[Fire] should be kindled around for one wishing to prosper;
the consecrated is an embryo;
he comes to be from here, as it were;
from here he generates him.
Having churned [the fire], he should place it down for one who has attained prosperity;
for he has come to be;
he approaches his own deity.
Bringing it from elsewhere, he should place it down for him regarding whom he might desire, "May I produce a rival for him";
he produces a rival for him.
He should place it down from an oven for one desiring food;
food is made in an oven;
he secures food in its own birthplace.
He should place it down from a tree that burns above for one desiring heaven;
this [fire] among the fires leads to heaven;
[this is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
"Whose food is wood, whose libation is ghee," [saying this,] he places down a scraped kṛmuka stick after anointing it with ghee;
Agni's dear body, with it he entered the kṛmuka;
ghee is fiery splendor;
he anoints his dear body with fiery splendor.
He places down reeds;
reeds are vigor;
he bestows vigor upon him.
"From the further region," [with this verse he places down] an udumbara [stick];
the udumbara is vigor;
he bestows vigor upon him.
"From the furthest distance," [with this verse he places down] a vaikaṅkata [stick];
when Agni was created, his brilliance reached the vikaṅkata;
he secures that very [brilliance].
"Whatever [sticks], O Agni," [with this verse he offers];
formerly Agni would not burn what was not cut with an axe;
the ṛṣi Prayoga made that savory to him with these [verses];
in that he places down kindling-sticks with these [verses],
he makes the kindling savory to him;
his food becomes savory, who knows thus.
The metres are Agni's womb;
in that he places down [the sticks] with the metres,
he maintains him in his own womb.
"Night after night bringing [it] without slackening," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
"Kindling Agni on the navel of the earth," [he says];
by this he conquers armies.
"The marching hosts that," [with this verse he places a stick];
when Agni was born, the demons wanted to kill him;
those gods saw these verses;
with them they beat off the demons from him;
in that he places down kindling-sticks with these,
[it is] for striking down the demons.
Then this Mālimlava hymn [is used];
the gods and the asuras contended;
those gods saw this Mālimlava hymn;
with it they overcame the asuras;
in that he places down a kindling-stick with this,
[it is] for the overcoming of the rival.
Therefore, in the year they pile the fire, they are prone to seize a thief.
"The marching hosts that," [with this verse he places] an udumbara [stick];
where the gods distributed vigor, thence the udumbara arose;
he bestows vigor upon him when just born.
"With two tusks, O Agni, the robbers," [with this verse he places] an aśvattha [stick];
this is the vanquisher of rivals among trees;
[it is] for victory.
When he says, "With two tusks," he should meditate in mind upon him whom he hates;
mind is swifter than speech;
he presents him as an oblation to Agni.
"The robbers who are among men," [with this verse he places] a vaikaṅkata [stick];
when Agni was created, his brilliance reached the vikaṅkata;
he secures that very [brilliance].
"He who acts as an enemy towards us," [with this verse he places] one made of śamī;
[it is] for appeasement.
"Who scorns us and wishes to harm," [he says];
therefore one should not speak ill of the fire-builder.
"Sharpened is my prayer," [he says];
by prayer he sharpens dominion,
by dominion prayer;
therefore dominion having prayer as its domestic priest surpasses other dominions;
therefore prayer possessed of dominion surpasses other brahmins.
"I have raised up their arms, their luster, and their strength," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
"I destroy the enemies with prayer, I raise up my own," [he says];
according to the yajus formula.

"Visible, the gold disk has shone afar," [with this] he should put on the gold disk.
Agni is death; gold is immortality;
by immortality alone he shields himself from death.
It has twenty-one knobs,
for firm standing.
There are twenty-one worlds of the gods;
from them alone he repels the rival.
He should place the knobs on the outside;
from this world alone he repels him.
Fastening it on, he casts it around on both sides;
from both worlds indeed he excludes him.
"Night and Dawn," [he says];
by Day and Night alone he encompasses him.
"The wealth-giving gods maintained Agni," [he says];
the vital breaths indeed are the wealth-giving gods;
by the vital breaths alone he has maintained him.
The sling has six cords;
there are six seasons;
by the seasons alone he encompasses him.
It has twelve cords;
twelve months are the year;
by the year alone he encompasses him.
It is made of muñja-grass;
muñja-grass is vigor;
with vigor he encompasses him.
He should not carry it below the navel;
Agni would burn up his semen.
He should carry it above the navel;
above the navel is divine;
Agni is all deities;
in what is divine alone he carries the deities.
"[Assuming] all forms," with [this verse] to Savitṛ he puts it on,
for impulsion.
Sitting, he puts it on;
therefore sitting, offspring are brought forth.
"You are the eagle, winged," [he says];
these are Agni's requisites;
with these he brings Agni together;
this indeed is Agni's dear domain;
that alone he secures.
"Go to the sky; fly to the sun," [he says],
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
These worlds belonged to the Asuras.
The gods said to Viṣṇu: "As far as this youth strides, grant us so much!"
In one stride he strode across this [earth], the gāyatrī his metre; in one stride across the intermediate space, the triṣṭubh his metre; in one stride across the sky, the jagatī his metre; in one stride across the directions, the anuṣṭubh his metre.
The gods found these worlds of the Asuras;
thence the gods came to be, the Asuras were defeated.
He who, knowing thus, steps the strides finds these very worlds of his rival;
he himself flourishes; his rival is defeated.
"Agni has cried," [he says];
by this Vatsaprī Bhālandana secured Agni's dear domain;
by this alone he secures Agni's dear domain.
"O Agni, returning! O Agni, Aṅgiras! Together with vigor, with wealth!" with this he returns;
therefore domestic animals come home in the evening from the wilderness to the village.
With four [verses];
for animals are four-footed.
He turns around to the right;
therefore the right half of the body has greater vigor.
"I have brought you here," [he says];
for he brings him here.
"May all the clans desire you; fix dominion firmly upon us," [he says];
whomever he desires: "May he have dominion!" he should think of him in mind;
he indeed attains dominion.
"Loosen the highest bond from us, O Varuṇa," [he says];
Śunaḥśepa Ājīgarti, seized by Varuṇa, saw this [verse];
by it he was freed from Varuṇa's bond;
by it he loosens Varuṇa's bond.
"High on top of the dawns, tall he has stood," [he says];
darkness is evil;
by it he beats away evil.
"The swan dwelling in light," [he says];
by it he gratifies the companions.
"The real," [he says];
orderly indeed he establishes him upon the real.
He sets it upon a chair;
he who is borne in the fire-pan is consecrated among the Agnis;
therefore he sets it upon a chair.
If he were to set it below, embryos would fall out;
he sets it above,
for the retention of embryos,
to prevent falling out.
With three [verses] he attends [it];
there are three breaths: prāṇa, vyāna, apāna;
them alone he places in the sacrificer.
Three are these worlds;
in these worlds alone he prospers.

Now this is the Vātsapra [hymn].
By this, indeed, Vatsaprī Bhālandana obtained Agni's dear domain.
By this he obtains Agni's dear domain.
By this, indeed, he conquered the superior speech of the ṛṣis;
by this he conquers the evil superior speech.
By this, indeed, he conquered curses.
He who, knowing thus, worships with this, conquers curses.
In that he strides the strides,
thereby he sustains setting out on a journey.
In that he worships,
thereby [he sustains] peaceful dwelling.
On the previous day he strides;
on the following day he worships.
Therefore, the mind of some creatures is on journeying, that of others on peaceful dwelling.
Therefore, the wanderer rules over peaceful dwelling;
therefore, the wanderer settles upon what belongs to peaceful dwelling.
It is eleven [verses];
the triṣṭubh has eleven syllables;
the triṣṭubh is vigor;
he obtains vigor.
In the past, our [cart] had a single pole;
then the ṛṣis saw this two-poled setting up for Agni.
Therefore, they make a two-poled setting up for Agni.
In imitation of that, a two-poled cart is made here.
"Up may all the gods [bear] you," [he says];
all the gods are the vital breaths;
by the vital breaths he raises him up.
All the gods are men;
by men he raises him up.
"O Agni, let them bear [you] with [their] thoughts," [he says];
for whichever thought he raises him up, with that he makes him prosper.
"Go forth, O Agni, shining," [he says];
indeed, at that time he causes burning to creatures;
[this is] for appeasement.
"Agni has roared," [with this] he should recite over [it] if it should creak;
for indeed, they carry with a cart that groans;
therefore, the owner of a cart and the owner of a chariot are the most honored among guests.
"O lord of food, give us of [your] food," [he says];
he makes food savory for him.
"Free from sickness, vigorous," [he says];
"free from consumption," he says by this.
"Further promote the giver; place strength in our two-footed and four-footed [creatures]," [he says];
he invokes a blessing.
"Honor Agni with a kindling-stick," [saying this] he places the kindling-stick [on the fire], having anointed it with ghee;
just as one cooks [a dish] containing ghee for a Brahmin guest, even so is that.
With a gāyatrī he should place it for a Brahmin;
for the Brahmin belongs to the gāyatrī;
with a triṣṭubh for a Rājanya;
for the Rājanya belongs to the triṣṭubh;
with a jagatī for a Vaiśya;
for the Vaiśya belongs to the jagatī;
according to the respective metre.
"O divine waters, receive this ash," [saying this] he scatters the ash into the waters.
The waters are Agni's womb;
he makes him go to his own womb.
With three [verses];
Agni is threefold;
as great as Agni is, to that extent he makes him go to a firm standing.
He indeed casts Agni away who scatters the ash into the waters;
"having united with the mothers, shining, sit down again," [he says];
he places light in him.
He indeed is deprived of strength and cattle who scatters the ash into the waters;
"again with strength, together with wealth," [he says];
again he obtains strength and cattle.
"Be mindful of this speech of mine, O youngest [god]," [he says];
therefore, creatures having fallen asleep awaken.
"Be mindful, O patron, O bounteous one," [he says];
the cattle, having wandered having set out, coming back, sit down according to their place.
With two [verses];
the sacrificer is two-footed;
for firm standing.

Now [as for] these Nairṛta [bricks]:
[there are] three, black, baked with chaff.
In that they are black,
this colour, indeed, clings to him whom Nirṛti seizes.
This is indeed the share of Nirṛti.
In that there is chaff,
by appearance and by share he appeases Nirṛti.
"Beyond this darkness..." he takes [them] with [a verse] addressed to Vaiśvānara;
he sweetens [them].
They carry [them] to this [southwestern] direction.
To this direction, indeed, they carry him whom Nirṛti seizes.
This is Nirṛti's direction;
in her own direction he appeases Nirṛti.
He lays [them] down on a natural barren spot;
this, indeed, of it is seized by Nirṛti.
Where it is seized by Nirṛti, there he appeases Nirṛti.
He lays [them] down pointing away;
he appeases Nirṛti pointing away.
He lays down three;
threefold arranged, indeed, is man.
As great as his self is, from that he appeases Nirṛti.
"Which the goddess Nirṛti bound on you..." with these words he casts down the sling;
the bond, indeed, belongs to Nirṛti;
he frees him from Nirṛti's bond.
To the world of the fathers, indeed, go those who proceed to the south with the Nairṛta [bricks];
the Āhavanīya is the world of the gods.
When he worships the Āhavanīya,
he turns back to the world of the gods.
He worships with a triṣṭubh;
the triṣṭubh is vigour;
he places vigour in him.
With a single [verse];
singly he places vigour in him.

He recites in a whisper.
Unexpressed, indeed, is Agni at this time, when he is unseparated;
therefore he recites in a whisper.
He recites the metres.
By means of the metres, indeed, Agni reached the high altar;
therefore he recites the metres.
They traverse the course of the sun when they make the horse step forward and backward;
therefore that sun goes east and goes west.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati.
When he makes the horse step,
he builds it with Prajāpati himself.
"You are the back of the waters, the womb of Agni," [with this] he lays down the lotus leaf.
In its own womb he builds it,
for pacification, for not burning through.
"Growing, great, and [spreading] out, O lotus..." [he says];
for he who comes into being grows.
"Extend with the measure of heaven in breadth," [he says];
he extends it.
"Brahman born first in the east..." [with this] he lays down the golden disk.
With the creatures having Brahman at their head, Prajāpati prospered;
[it is] for prosperity.
It has twenty-one knobs,
for firm standing.
Twenty-one are the worlds of the gods;
from them he repels the rival.
He should make the knobs below,
for the subjugation of the rival.
Agni is not to be built on earth, nor in the intermediate space, nor in heaven.
If he were to build [it] on earth, he would burn up the plants with heat;
if in the intermediate space, the birds;
if in heaven, heaven.
Gold is immortality.
When he lays down the golden disk,
he builds Agni in immortality itself.
"The Golden Germ came into being in the beginning..." [with this] he lays down the golden man.
By this he has maintained the world of the sacrificer;
moreover, he builds Agni with light in the centre.
"The drop has spilled..." [with this] he touches [it];
he firmly establishes it in the Hotrās, in truth.
He attends [it] with the serpent-heads;
he hands it over to death itself;
moreover, whatever brilliance is in the serpent, that he obtains.
If he were to lay [them] down, he would be liable to die.
If he were to lay [them] down turned toward each other with the other heads, the domestic animals would be biting;
if turned away from each other, the wild [animals].
He should only speak the Yajus formula;
by that alone he obtains that brilliance;
thereby it is pacified.
Therefore, formerly, they did not wish to look upon him who builds the fire-altar,
for he has obtained all these brilliances: that which is in fire, that in the serpent, that in the sun.
Now, this rakṣas-slaying hymn of Vāmadeva is at the head of the sacrifice;
at the head of the sacrifice, indeed, the rakṣases wish to destroy the sacrifice;
[it is] for the slaying of the rakṣases.
It consists of five verses;
Agni is fivefold;
from as great as Agni is, he slays the rakṣases.
He offers, moving around all the directions;
from the directions indeed he slays the rakṣases.
He lays down two offering-ladles;
he lays down these two [worlds] indeed.
He lays [them] down silently;
for one should not obtain these two with a Yajus formula.
The man is the trunk, the two offering-ladles are the two arms;
when he lays down the two offering-ladles,
[it is] for having a body.
On the south, indeed, the rakṣases ate the offerings of the gods, carrying [them] off;
they screened them with kārṣmarya [wood].
In that he lays down [a ladle] made of kārṣmarya on the south,
[it is] for screening from the rakṣases.
He fills [it] with ghee.
The kārṣmarya is a vajra, ghee is a vajra;
in a vajra he places a vajra.
He lays [it] down with the Gāyatrī;
Agni is connected with the Gāyatrī, having the Gāyatrī metre;
with his own metre he causes him to prosper.
He fills the [ladle] of udumbara wood with curd.
Curd is food, the udumbara is vigour;
in food he places vigour.
He lays [it] down with the Triṣṭubh;
the Triṣṭubh belongs to Indra, Indra's power is food;
he obtains Indra's power, food.
This [earth] is the one made of kārṣmarya; that [heaven] is the one made of udumbara.
When he lays down the udumbara [ladle] to the north,
therefore that [heaven] is north of this [earth].
[The verse] contains the word 'head' [mūrdhan];
a head is made by these two.
Agni is to be built in the Virāj;
the offering-ladle is the Virāj;
when he lays down the two offering-ladles,
he builds Agni in the Virāj itself.

Whatever brick an unknowing Adhvaryu lays down first, with that he blocks the breath of the sacrificer, and of his offspring and of his domestic animals.
It is a naturally perforated [brick],
for the release of the breaths,
for the beholding of the heavenly world.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati.
When he makes the horse smell it,
he builds it with Prajāpati himself.
This [earth] indeed is the naturally perforated [brick];
it is this [earth] that he thus lays down.
If he should think, "My rival has surpassed me in front," he should push it forward to the east;
whichever rival of his has surpassed him in front, him he drives forward with this.
If he should think, "My rival is behind me," he should push it away to the west;
whichever rival is behind him, him he drives away with this.
He drives away a superior rival;
he repels an inferior [rival].
If he should think, "He is equal to me," he should shake it.
Whatever offspring this [earth] shook off, they perished;
this [earth] shakes him off.
Now this is the dūrvā-brick.
Agni is a domestic animal;
domestic animals do not delight in a place without pasture.
He thus makes an abode for the domestic animals,
for the retention of domestic animals.
"Growing up knot by knot...", [with this he lays it down]
for establishment.
"With a thousand and with a hundred...", [he says];
possessing a thousand, it is commensurate with Prajāpati.
He attains to a thousandfold nourishment of domestic animals, who, knowing thus, lays it down.
Now this is the Vāmabhṛt [brick].
By this, indeed, the gods took away the desirable wealth, the cattle, of the Asuras.
He takes away the desirable wealth, the cattle, of his rival by this.
It has two Yajus-formulas.
He takes away the world of his rival with that which has two Yajus-formulas;
for the rival is in the second world.
It has a golden head;
endowed with golden light he goes to the heavenly world.
Two glass beads are fastened symmetrically;
therefore the two eyes are symmetrical.
Now these two are the seed-pourers.
That [sky] indeed is Svarāj; this [earth] is Virāj.
Into a woman lying on her back the man pours seed;
that [sky] pours seed into this [earth];
this [earth] brings forth.
Agni consumes.
He builds Agni possessing seed;
he propagates offspring and consumes food, who, knowing thus, lays these two down.
"You are Svarāj," [with this] he lays it down on the south;
therefore the man lies beside the woman on the south.
If he should build for one in the earlier stage of life, he should lay down both together in the first layer;
turned toward each other they pour seed for him.
He should lay down either one for him whom he hates;
he deprives him of seed itself.
If he should build for one in the later stage of life, he should lay down one in the first layer, the other in the topmost layer;
he encloses the seed once poured by means of these two.
He lays down two seasonal [bricks],
for the maintenance of the seasons.
He lays down two each,
for the seasons are in pairs.
Now this is the thrice-marked [brick].
The gods and the Asuras did exactly the same in the sacrifice.
Whatever the gods did, that the Asuras did.
Those gods saw this thrice-marked [brick].
They laid it down with the mark upward;
then they overcame the Asuras;
thence the gods flourished, the Asuras perished.
He who, knowing thus, lays this down with the mark upward, it is for the non-overcoming by his rival; he himself flourishes, his rival perishes.
He should lay it down with the mark downward for him whom he hates;
he makes him follow the womb of the Asuras into ruin.
These worlds indeed are thrice-marked;
he attains these worlds by it.
From the clay with which they make the ukhā-pot, of that he should make this;
thereby all his bricks become possessed of light, possessed of Yajus-formulas.

The tortoise is the sacrificial essence of domestic animals.
When he lays down the tortoise,
the domestic animals attend upon him, recognizing this very sacrificial essence.
He lays it down alive,
for he is fit for sacrifice.
He has four feet;
there are four directions;
he establishes [himself] in the directions themselves.
"Great heaven and earth for us...", [he says];
by this he holds the forms of heaven and earth.
He anoints it with curds and with honey.
Honey is the sap of waters and plants;
when he anoints it with honey,
he secures the sap of waters and plants.
Curds is the sacrificial essence of domestic animals, [their] vigour;
when he anoints it with curds,
he places the sacrificial essence, vigour, into the domestic animals.
One Agni is built with bricks, another is built with cattle;
when he lays down the living tortoise,
thereby he makes him built with cattle.
He is the topmost step to the heavenly world;
just as one who knows the terrain, being knowledgeable, leads others directly, even so he leads him to the heavenly world.
Agni is to be built in the navel of Viṣṇu.
Whoever builds Agni without a navel, [Agni] enters the navel of the sacrificer;
he burns him up.
This is the navel of Viṣṇu,
when he lays down the mortar;
he builds Agni in the navel of Viṣṇu.
It is made of udumbara wood;
udumbara is vigour;
he places vigour in the middle, in the sacrificer and in his offspring;
therefore vigour invigorates offspring from the middle.
It is a span in size;
for so great is the navel of Viṣṇu.
He lays it down with a [formula] dedicated to Viṣṇu and suitable for ritual action;
for this ritual action is being performed.
Now this is the ukhā-pot.
Ojas and virility are collected
when the ukhā-pot [is placed];
he secures ojas and virility.
He secures the light of these worlds;
he places light in the middle;
he builds Agni with light in the middle;
therefore they worship the light from the middle.
He fills it with sand, with its own abode;
sand is the ashes of Agni Vaiśvānara;
by its form he secures Vaiśvānara.
If he should lay it down deficient, the sacrificer would become hungry;
he lays it down full;
the sacrificer becomes not hungry;
he eats inexhaustible food.
He fills it with ghee;
ghee is the dear domain of Agni;
with his dear domain he makes him prosper.

Agni is an animal.
This womb of the animal is prepared.
The Apasyā bricks are semen.
When he lays down the Apasyā bricks,
he places semen in the womb.
When the animal develops [in the womb], then it is born.
The Chandasyā bricks are cattle.
When he lays down the Chandasyā bricks after the Apasyā bricks,
[it is] for the propagation of cattle.
He lays down five [of each].
Cattle are fivefold.
He propagates the animal in its full measure.
This [earth] feared the excessive burning of Agni.
She created these Apasyā bricks.
She laid them down.
When the Apasyā bricks are laid down,
[it is] for her non-burning.
She indeed said: "He whose [altar] has these laid down shall eat food through the priesthood."
He who, knowing thus, lays these down eats food through the priesthood.
He lays down five in the east, facing west;
therefore an animal is born in the east facing west.
[He lays down] five in the south, facing north;
therefore the male lies beside the female from the south.
[He lays down] five in the west, facing east;
therefore semen is deposited from the west towards the east.
Five Chandasyā bricks are in the north.
The Chandasyā bricks are cattle; cattle have their abode in the north.
He gathers the cattle, when born, toward their own abode,
for the non-injury of the cattle.
He should not lay down the Apasyā bricks in the north;
if he were to lay them down, Varuṇa would seize the offspring with dropsy.
After the Apasyā bricks, he lays down the Prāṇabhṛt bricks.
Once the semen is poured, he places in it breath, mind, sight, hearing, and speech.
Therefore the animal is born breathing, seeing, hearing, and speaking.
Those containing the word 'breath'—"This one in front is earth..."—he lays down in the east;
he places breath in the front;
therefore an animal breathes forward.
Those containing the word 'mind'—"This one in the south is Viśvakarman..."—[he lays down] in the south;
he places mind in the south;
therefore the right half [of the body] possesses more mind;
therefore they attend to the mind from the south.
Those containing the word 'sight'—"This one in the west is Viśvavyacas..."—[he lays down] in the west;
he places sight in the west;
therefore an animal sees looking forward.
Those containing the word 'hearing'—"This light in the north..."—[he lays down] in the north;
he places hearing in the north;
therefore an animal hears more from the north.
Those containing the word 'speech'—"This thought above..."—[he lays down] in the middle;
he places speech in the middle;
therefore speech speaks from the middle.
He lays them down crosswise;
therefore cattle walk moving their limbs crosswise.
With those he laid down in the east, Vasiṣṭha prospered;
with those in the south, Bharadvāja [prospered];
with those in the west, Jamadagni [prospered];
with those in the north, Viśvāmitra [prospered];
with those in the middle, Viśvakarman [prospered].
He who thus knows their prosperity prospers.
He who thus knows their arrangement, for him it is arranged.
He who thus knows their connection becomes possessed of connections.
He who thus knows their foundation becomes possessed of a foundation.
He who thus knows their abode becomes possessed of an abode.
He who thus knows their firm foundation attains a firm foundation.
Possessed of a foundation, possessed of an abode becomes he who knows thus.

Now these [are the] Āśvinī [bricks].
This Agni is indeed a disrupted sacrifice.
Who knows that? Whatever of this is not performed, whatever is not piled, that he arranges with these, that he heals.
He lays down five of these.
Agni is fivefold;
as much as Agni is, so much medicine he provides for him.
With these he arranges the directions of Agni.
"On the back of Aditi I seat thee," [he says];
with this he supports these worlds.
He lays down the seasonal [bricks];
for the arrangement of the seasons.
He lays down five [of them];
five indeed are the seasons;
as many as the seasons are, so many he arranges.
They have identical beginnings, they have identical conclusions;
for the seasons have identical beginnings, have identical conclusions.
He differentiates [them] with a single vyāhṛti;
therefore the seasons differentiate.
Therefore the seasons of the same year have varied forms.
He lays down the seasonal [bricks] after the Āśvinī [bricks];
when the seed has been deposited, he lays down the seasons;
therefore, when the seed has been deposited, [the embryo] develops in accordance with the seasons.
He lays down the Vāyavyā [bricks] after the seasonal [bricks];
he places the breath into the seasons;
therefore these, going round equally, do not decay;
therefore the wind blows following the seasons.
He lays down the Apasyā [bricks] after the Vāyavyā [bricks];
therefore the wind carries the rain;
he secures the rain by means of these.
If he were to lay them down together, it would rain for one season.
He sets them down moving around;
therefore it rains season by season.
He lays down the Vayasyā [bricks] after the Apasyā [bricks];
the Vayasyā [bricks] are cattle;
when he lays down the Vayasyā [bricks] after the Apasyā [bricks],
therefore cattle have different minds and different habits;
they are of one mind regarding water alone.
Having laid down the Apasyā [bricks], he should lay down the Vayasyā [bricks] for him whom he desires: "May he have cattle."
Waters are the concord of cattle;
he piles Agni in the concord of cattle; he becomes possessed of cattle.
Having laid down the Vayasyā [bricks], he should lay down the Apasyā [bricks] for him whom he desires: "May he be without cattle."
He piles Agni in the discord of cattle; he becomes without cattle.
He lays down four in the front;
therefore in front an animal is thinner;
therefore the eye has four forms: two white, two black.
They contain the word 'head';
therefore in the front is the head of animals.
Five on each side;
for the unification of the animal;
therefore an animal slopes forward.
"The he-goat is strength," he lays down on the south;
"The ram is strength," on the north;
he lays down his shoulders.
"The tiger is strength," on the right wing;
"The lion is strength," on the left wing;
he places vigour in the wings.
"The tiger is strength," the eastern one;
"The lion is strength," the western one;
therefore the tiger is born in the east, the lion in the west.
"Man is strength," in the middle;
therefore man is the lord of animals.

"O Indra and Agni, do ye two make firm the unshakeable brick," [he says];
Indra and Agni are vigour and prowess.
This layer is the intermediate space;
the intermediate space is loose, as it were;
creatures are the intermediate space;
he places vigour and prowess in creatures.
It is naturally perforated;
for the emission of the breaths,
for the sighting of the heavenly world.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati;
when he causes the horse to smell [it],
he piles him by means of Prajāpati.
Now these [are the] direction-bricks.
When the gods were going to the heavenly world, their directions collapsed;
they saw these direction-bricks;
with them they made firm the directions.
When these are laid down,
[it is] for the support of the directions.
"Thou art the queen, the eastern direction," [he says];
therefore this is the queen of directions.
"Thou art the sovereign, the southern direction," [he says];
therefore this shines among directions.
"Thou art the imperial ruler, the western direction," [he says];
therefore here is imperial rule.
"Thou art the self-ruler, the northern direction," [he says];
therefore here is self-rule.
"Thou art the supreme ruler, the upward direction," [he says];
therefore this is the supreme ruler of directions.
He who, knowing thus, lays these down, becomes all these.
"Protect my life," [with this] he lays down ten in front;
nine indeed are the breaths in a man, the navel is the tenth;
he places the breaths in him.
"Bestow light upon me," [with this he lays down] the last one;
he places light as the highest;
therefore speech is the highest light among the breaths.
He lays down ten of these;
the virāj consists of ten syllables;
the virāj is the light among metres;
the eye is light;
he places the eye in front of creatures.
"The mā metre," he lays down on the south;
therefore the months turn to the south.
"The earth is the metre," in the west;
for firm establishment.
"Agni is the deity," in the north;
Agni is vigour;
he places vigour on the north;
therefore one who advances from the north conquers.
He lays down twelve on the south, twelve in the west, twelve in the north;
they amount to thirty-six;
the bṛhatī consists of thirty-six syllables;
the bṛhatī attained self-rule among metres;
he who, knowing thus, lays these down, attains self-rule.
These are thirty-six;
the bṛhatī consists of thirty-six syllables;
cattle belong to the bṛhatī; the intermediate space
is this layer; cattle are the intermediate space;
he places cattle in cattle.
Certain breaths have the Ādityas as their abode; others have the Aṅgirases as their abode.
Those in front have the Ādityas as their abode;
those in the back have the Aṅgirases as their abode.
"Thou art the head, the ruler," he lays down in front;
those breaths that have the Ādityas as their abode, he held them thereby.
"Thou art the controller, the ruler," in the back;
those breaths that have the Aṅgirases as their abode, he held them thereby.
He lays down seven in front, seven in the back;
therefore the lower breath has half a share of the upper breaths.
Prajāpati, having created creatures, desired regarding them: "May I be the head."
He saw these Mūrdhanyā [bricks]; by means of them he rose up;
he became their head.
That is the headship of the Mūrdhanyā [bricks].
He who, knowing thus, lays these down, becomes the head among his equals.

"The swift one, the trivṛt," [with these words] he lays down [a brick] in front.
The trivṛt is indeed the face of the sacrifice;
he arranges the face of the sacrifice in front.
"The firmament, the saptadaśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The saptadaśa is indeed food;
food propagates after the seventeen-month year.
Food comes to be on the south;
therefore a man eats food with his right hand.
Because he lays down [a brick] containing the saptadaśa on the south,
therefore birds fly following along with the right wing.
[He lays down a brick] containing the ekaviṃśa on the west,
for firm standing;
for birds rest upon [their tail] behind.
[He lays down a brick] containing the pañcadaśa on the north.
The pañcadaśa is indeed vigour;
he places vigour on the north;
therefore he who advances from the north conquers.
"Agni's share art thou, the overlordship of the consecration," [he says];
in front are the two trivṛt [formulas];
he arranges [them] in regular succession in the face of the sacrifice.
[He lays down a brick] containing the pañcadaśa on the south, [and a brick] containing the saptadaśa on the north,
for the unification of the two wings.
The saptadaśa is indeed food;
because he lays down [bricks] containing the saptadaśa on both sides,
he places food on both sides;
therefore a man eats food, grasping it with both hands.
It contains the ekaviṃśa,
for firm standing.
"Agni's share art thou, the overlordship of the consecration," [he says];
this and that the gods won by means of these [bricks];
this and that he wins by means of these;
these bricks are named Spṛts.
"With one they praised; creatures were disposed," [he says];
this and that the gods created by means of these [bricks];
all this he secures by means of these.
This [arrangement] is arranged following the arrangement of the Arkya;
food is arka; Agni is arka;
he eats food for whom, knowing thus, this is arranged.

The gods and the Asuras did indeed perform equally at the sacrifice.
Whatever the gods did, that the Asuras did.
Those gods saw this fourth layer;
they, reciting it in one way, laid it down in another way.
The Asuras did not follow after that.
Then the gods flourished, the Asuras were defeated.
He who, knowing thus, lays down this fourth layer, it is for the non-pursuit of his rival; he flourishes in himself, his rival is defeated.
"The swift one, the trivṛt," [with these words] he lays down [a brick] in front.
The trivṛt is indeed the face of the sacrifice;
he places the face of the sacrifice in front.
"The firmament, the saptadaśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The saptadaśa is indeed food;
he secures food on the south.
[He lays down a brick] containing the ekaviṃśa on the west,
for firm standing;
[a brick] containing the pañcadaśa on the north.
The pañcadaśa is indeed vigour;
he places vigour on the north;
therefore he who advances from the north conquers.
"Forward-rushing, the aṣṭādaśa," [with these words he lays down one] in front;
there are two trivṛt [formulas];
he places [them] in regular succession in the face of the sacrifice.
"Overcoming, the saviṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The saviṃśa is indeed food;
he secures food on the south.
"Splendour, the dvāviṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
In that it is two:
the sacrificer is two-footed;
[it is] for firm standing.
In that it is twenty:
two Virājs [of ten syllables]; the Virāj is food;
he stands firmly in the Virāj, in proper food.
"Heat, the navadaśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the north;
therefore the left of the two hands has more heat; it contracts the arm.
"The womb, the caturviṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] in front.
The caturviṃśa is indeed the face of the sacrifice;
he places the face of the sacrifice in front.
"Embryos, the pañcaviṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The pañcaviṃśa is indeed food;
he secures food on the south.
In that it is ten:
the Virāj has ten syllables; the Virāj is food;
he stands firmly in the Virāj, in proper food.
In that it is fifteen:
the sacrifice is fifteenfold;
he places a vajra on high,
for the slaying of the Rakṣases.
"Vigour, the triṇava," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
These worlds are the triṇava;
he stands firmly in these worlds.
"Gathering, the trayoviṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the north;
therefore the left of the two hands gathers more.
"Will, the ekatriṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] in front.
The ekatriṃśa is indeed speech;
he places speech in the face of the sacrifice.
"The summit of the ruddy one, the catustriṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
Yonder sun is indeed the summit of the ruddy one;
yonder sun is spiritual lustre;
he secures spiritual lustre on the south.
"Support, the trayastriṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
Thirty-three are the deities;
he stands firmly in the deities.
"The vault, the ṣaṭtriṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the north.
The vault is indeed the heavenly world;
he establishes the sacrificer in the heavenly world.

"Thou art Agni's share, the overlordship of the consecration," [with these words] he lays down [a brick] in front.
Agni is indeed the face of the sacrifice; the consecration is the face of the sacrifice; the trivṛt is the face of the sacrifice.
He places the face of the sacrifice in front.
"Thou art the share of the men-watchers, the overlordship of the creator," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
Those who are wise are the men-watchers; food is the creator; generation is the saptadaśa.
He covers food over for the creatures that have been brought forth;
therefore one who is born reaches out toward food.
"Thou art Mitra's share, the overlordship of Varuṇa," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
Mitra is in-breathing; Varuṇa is out-breathing;
he establishes in-breathing and out-breathing in the sacrificer.
"Thou art Indra's share, the overlordship of Viṣṇu," [with these words he lays down one] on the north.
Indra is vigour; Viṣṇu is vigour; the pañcadaśa is vigour;
he places vigour on the north;
therefore he who advances from the north conquers.
"Thou art the Vasus' share, the overlordship of the Rudras," [with these words he lays down one] in front.
The Vasus are the face of the sacrifice; the Rudras are the face of the sacrifice; the caturviṃśa is the face of the sacrifice;
he places the face of the sacrifice in front.
"Thou art the Ādityas' share, the overlordship of the Maruts," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The Ādityas are food; the Maruts are food; the pañcaviṃśa is food;
he secures food on the south.
"Thou art Aditi's share, the overlordship of Pūṣan," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
Aditi is a firm foundation; Pūṣan is a firm foundation; the triṇava is a firm foundation;
[it is] for a firm foundation.
"Thou art the divine Savitṛ's share, the overlordship of Bṛhaspati," [with these words he lays down one] on the north.
Bṛhaspati is the sacred word; the catuṣṣṭoma is the sacred word;
he places spiritual lustre on the north.
It contains the word 'Savitṛ';
therefore a gift sent forth towards the north [goes] to the brahmins.
"The support, the catuṣṣṭoma," [with these words he lays down one] in front.
The catuṣṣṭoma is indeed the face of the sacrifice;
he places the face of the sacrifice in front.
"Thou art the share of the barley-halves, the overlordship of the non-barley-halves," [with these words he lays down one] on the south.
The months are indeed the barley-halves; the months are food;
he secures food on the south.
"Thou art the Ṛbhus' share, the overlordship of all the gods," [with these words he lays down one] on the west.
The deities are thirty-three;
he establishes himself firmly in the deities.
"The revolution, the aṣṭācatvāriṃśa," [with these words he lays down one] on the north;
therefore the two hands are of equal strength.
This arrangement is arranged in accordance with the arrangement of the Arkya;
food is the arka; Agni is the arka;
he who knows thus, for whom this is arranged, eats food.
Those that are chief he lays down in front;
for him who knows thus, a chief [child] is born.
Those that contain food [he lays down] on the south;
for him who knows thus, an eater of food is born.
Those that contain a firm foundation [he lays down] on the west;
he who knows thus attains a firm foundation.
Those that contain vigour [he lays down] on the north;
for him who knows thus, a strong [child] is born.
He who knows thus the prosperity of these [bricks] prospers.

"O Agni, thrust away our born rivals," [saying this,] he lays [a brick] down in the east.
Whichever rival has already preceded him, that one he thrusts away by it.
"[Thrust away those] born through force," [saying this, he lays one down] in the west.
Whichever rival is behind him, that one he thrusts away by it.
He thrusts away the better rival [in front];
he thrusts back the worse rival [behind].
"The forty-four-fold stoma," [he says,] on the south;
"The sixteen-fold stoma," on the north.
The sixteen-fold is a vajra;
the triṣṭubh is a vajra;
the forty-four-fold belongs to the triṣṭubh.
The vajra is grasped with the left [hand] and hurled to the south.
Having driven away the rival, he hurls the vajra after him.
"The forty-four-fold stoma," he lays down on the south.
The forty-four-fold is spiritual lustre;
he secures spiritual lustre on the south.
"The sixteen-fold stoma," [he lays down] on the north.
The sixteen-fold is vigour;
he places vigour on the north.
Therefore one who marches forward from the north conquers.
"Thou art the earth's rubbish," [saying this, he places one] in the middle.
The middle of the self is the rubbish;
he piles Agni having a self.
These are the bricks named rival-less;
he has no rival, who knowing thus lays them down.
Now these are the virājs;
he places the virāj as supreme among the cattle;
therefore one who has cattle speaks the supreme speech.
He lays them down ten by ten,
for unification.
The gods, indeed, going to the heavenly world—those metres of theirs which were unexpressed, belonging to the heavenly world,
with them they went to the heavenly world.
The ṛṣis wished to know after them;
they fashioned them with bricks.
These are those metres that do not convey the sacrifice.
Whichever unexpressed metres of the gods belong to the heavenly world, with them he goes to the heavenly world, who knowing thus lays them down.
Now these are the stomabhāgā [bricks].
Bṛhaspati brought this support of the sacrifice;
that these are laid down,
[is] for the firm support of the sacrifice.
Bṛhaspati collected this brilliance of the sacrifice;
that these are laid down,
he piles Agni possessed of brilliance.
Prajāpati created creatures;
he created them with the stomabhāgās.
That these are laid down,
[is] for procreation.
On the south, indeed, the sacrifice of the gods collapsed;
they propped it up with the stomabhāgās.
That these are laid down on the south pointing northward,
[is] for the propping up of the sacrifice.
He lays down seven by seven,
for having identical strength.
Three in the middle,
for firm support.
Now these are the nākasad [bricks].
By the nākasads the gods went to the heavenly world;
that these are laid down,
[is] for conquering the heavenly world.
Nothing is painful to one who has gone to that world: that is the nākasad-nature of the nākasads.
These are the bricks named all-prṣṭha;
whatever is the brick of the pṛṣṭhas, whatever brilliance of the pṛṣṭhas [there is], that he secures, who knowing thus lays them down.
Now these are the pañcacūḍā [bricks].
The wives are the domain of the sacrificer;
in that he lays the pañcacūḍās on the nākasads,
he establishes the sacrificer firmly in heaven.
These are the Apsarases;
having assumed this form, becoming wives, they lie beside the sacrificer in yonder world.
He lays one down in the west facing east;
therefore the wife sits behind facing east.
These are the protectors of the sacrificer's body;
he reaches full life, who knowing thus lays them down.
These deities belong to the heavenly world;
with them he goes to the heavenly world.
These deities are fit for sorcery;
whomever he hates, about him he should say, "I place that one in your jaws";
he cuts him off from these deities; he quickly perishes.

"I settle thee in the seat of life," [he says];
he places life itself as highest;
therefore life is the highest of the breaths.
There is a naturally perforated [brick],
for the emission of the vital breaths,
for the sighting of the heavenly world.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati;
when he makes the horse sniff [the altar],
he piles him with Prajāpati himself.
Then this Vikarṇī [brick];
this Vikarṇī is indeed the stride of the gods;
because it is the Vikarṇī,
he makes him step out according to the stride of the gods.
[It is accompanied by] one having Vāyu (wind);
breath is Vāyu; the naturally perforated [brick] is life;
life and breath he places in him together.
He kindles him indeed in that he piles [the fire];
he makes it flare up by means of that which has Vāyu.
Having carried [the fire in the pan] which is the head of the year, he should pile the fire.
Agni Vaiśvānara is indeed the year;
his bricks are the days and nights;
he piles him having attained the bricks, he prospers.
If he were not to pile [it] thus, he would deprive him of his dear body;
since he piles [it] thus,
he enriches him with his dear body;
Vaiśvānara is piled here possessing his body.
He lays down the seasonal [bricks],
for the support of the seasons.
He lays them down two by two,
for the seasons are two by two.
He lays down four in the middle layer;
the middle layer is, as it were, the atmosphere;
the atmosphere is, as it were, loose;
there are four quarters;
he firmly establishes himself in the quarters.
The gods fashioned them from the seasons;
that is the nature of the seasonal [bricks] as seasonal;
he who, knowing thus, lays them down firmly establishes himself in season after season as it arrives.
They have different beginnings and identical conclusions;
therefore the single year has different forms.
Having laid down an avakā plant, he lays down the brick;
the avakā is indeed the womb of Agni Vaiśvānara;
he lays them down in their own womb,
for appeasement, that there be no burning.
He who, knowing thus, lays them down prospers.
Now these Viśvajyotis [bricks]:
these are indeed the breath-supporters of the sacrificer;
he holds breath by the first, circulating breath by the second, downward breath by the third.
These deities are belonging to the heavenly world;
by them he goes to the heavenly world.
These deities are luminous;
Agni becomes his light in this world, Vāyu in the atmosphere, Sūrya in heaven;
all these worlds become luminous for him.
They have [formulas mentioning] overlords;
by them he attains overlordship.
"Fill the space, fill the gap," [he says];
whatever of it is deficient, whatever is a gap, that he fills up.
"Then sit firm," [he says];
he strengthens it.
"Indra and Agni, Bṛhaspati [have settled thee]," [he says];
Indra and Agni are vigor and virility;
with vigor and virility he piles him.
"Those milk-yielding cows of his," [he says];
therefore there is marrow in every joint.
"The dappled ones mix the Soma," [he says];
the dappled one is food;
he secures food.
"In the three bright realms of heaven," [he says];
[there are] three pressings;
he piles him in the pressings.
The anuṣṭubh is the following [meter];
the anuṣṭubh is breath;
therefore breath moves along through all the limbs.
The anuṣṭubh is speech;
whatever of his here is not done, whatever is not piled, that he arranges by speech, that he heals.

Through the consecration, the virāj is to be obtained.
He should be consecrated for three nights;
the virāj is three-footed;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for six nights;
the seasons are six, [such is] the year;
the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for ten nights;
the virāj has ten syllables;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for twelve nights;
twelve months are the year; the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for thirteen nights;
thirteen months are the year; the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for fifteen nights;
fifteen are the nights of a half-month; half-month by half-month the year is obtained;
the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for twenty-four nights;
twenty-four are the half-months of the year; half-month by half-month the year is obtained;
the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for a month;
that which is a month is the year;
the year is the virāj;
he indeed obtains the virāj.
He should be consecrated for four months;
for four months indeed the Vasus bore him;
they conquered the earth [and] the gāyatrī metre;
for eight [months] the Rudras [bore him];
they conquered the intermediate space [and] the triṣṭubh metre;
for twelve [months] the Ādityas [bore him];
they conquered heaven [and] the jagatī metre;
thence indeed they went to distinction, to pre-eminence among the gods.
Therefore, having borne [the fire in the pan] for twelve months, he should pile the fire [altar];
twelve months are the year; the year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
his bricks are the days and nights;
with bricks obtained he piles him;
he prospers;
he indeed goes to distinction, to pre-eminence among his equals.
He sings the Gāyatra [sāman] in front;
the Gāyatra is indeed the head of the gods; Agni is of gāyatrī nature;
therefore he sings the Gāyatra in front,
on a [verse] addressed to Agni Pavamāna.
This one who purifies himself is indeed the breath;
Agni is the mouth of the gods;
he places the breath at the mouth itself;
therefore the breath is at the mouth.
He sings the Rathantara on the north, the Bṛhat on the south;
he lays down his wings indeed.
This [earth] is the Rathantara; that [heaven] is the Bṛhat;
by these two he encloses him.
He sings the Yajñāyajñīya at the tail, the Vāmadevya at the hip;
the Yajñāyajñīya is the self; the Vāmadevya is the body;
with a self, with a body, he piles him.
The heart is near the armpit;
therefore the heart is near the armpit; it is without a verse;
therefore the heart is boneless.
He sings around [him] with the Arkya [verses];
Agni is the arka;
he sings around the arka with the Arkya [verses],
for appeasement.

Between darkness and the heavenly world is indeed the year.
Luminous are these thousand-bearing bricks.
When he sprinkles [them] with gold chips,
with light he illuminates the year for him.
"Thou art the measure of a thousand, thou art the image of a thousand," [he says];
for these worlds are commensurate with a thousand.
They strike at Agni in that which is not done here for him, which is not piled.
Gold is immortality.
When he sprinkles with gold chips,
he unites his body with immortality.
"May these bricks be milch-cows for me, O Agni," [he says];
he makes them milch-cows;
they wait upon him yielding his wishes in yonder world.
The gods indeed excluded Rudra from the sacrifice;
he turned round upon the gods with drawn [bow];
those gods saw this Śatarudrīya;
with it they appeased him.
When he offers the Śatarudrīya,
with it he appeases him.
First he offers at knee-height;
he sacrifices away Rudra from this [earth];
then at navel-height;
he sacrifices away Rudra from the intermediate space;
then at a man's height;
he sacrifices away Rudra from that [heaven].
From here upwards he sacrifices away Rudra;
then at a man's height;
then at navel-height;
then at knee-height;
from yonder hitherward he sacrifices away Rudra.
That amounts to six;
six are the seasons;
from the seasons he sacrifices away Rudra.
He offers with gavīdhukā groats, or with wild sesame, or with butter of a kusaya, or with the milk of a wild beast.
If he were to offer with domestic [produce], he would make Rudra frequent the village;
with wild [produce] he sacrifices away Rudra towards the wilderness.
He offers with an arka-leaf;
Agni is the arka;
with the arka he sacrifices him away from the arka.
On the last brick of the northern wing he offers;
this is indeed the direction of Rudra;
having brought [it] back to his own direction, he sacrifices [him] away.
Agni is a beast;
now indeed he is born, when he is piled;
just as a new-born calf desires to reach the udder, so he now desires to reach his share;
he meditates upon both the sacrificer and the Adhvaryu.
When he offers the Śatarudrīya,
with his share he appeases him.
The Aṅgirases, going to the heavenly world, poured out the cauldron on a she-goat;
suffering heat, she touched a leaf;
that became the arka plant;
that is the arka-nature of the arka.
When he offers with an arka-leaf,
[it is] for possessing the same origin.
Whom he hates, in the track of his cattle he should throw [it] down;
whichever beast steps [on it] first, they incur misfortune.
Those [formulas] which are last, those he should make the sacrificer recite;
these deities indeed belong to the heavenly world;
with them he goes to the heavenly world.
These deities are indeed suitable for sorcery;
whom he hates, of him he should say: "I place that one in your jaws";
from these deities he tears him away;
instantly he perishes.

With 'The vigour in the stone, resting in the mountain,' he sprinkles around with water.
Water is the sap of Prajāpati.
With the sap of Prajāpati he gratifies it.
Gratified, pleased, not hungering, it waits upon him.
'In the stone be thy hunger; let thy heat fall upon him whom we hate,' [he says].
Whomsoever he hates, him he afflicts with its hunger and its heat.
Three times he sprinkles around;
three are these worlds;
from these worlds he appeases it.
Three times he goes around again without sprinkling;
that amounts to six;
six indeed are the seasons;
from the seasons he appeases it.
If he should practice sorcery, having gone around three times again without sprinkling, he should crush [the pot] on the right buttock, [saying,] 'Here do I crush so-and-so, descendant of so-and-so, son of so-and-so.'
Taking whatever heat is in the fire, he strikes him with it;
as it breaks, he undergoes misfortune.
In that he mentions his name,
he cuts off his very root.
When piled, this [fire] is the wrath of Varuṇa.
He drags across with a frog;
he passes away with the lifespan of a frog;
he harms neither domestic animals nor wild [animals].
Therefore does one come to it in spring and in the rainy season,
for it is affected by heat.
He drags across with avakā and with a cane-branch.
Avakā is the cream of the waters; cane is the flower of the waters.
Waters are appeased;
with the appeased ones he appeases its heat.
With three [verses];
threefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, so great does he appease his heat.
Prajāpati created offspring;
they hungered;
to them he gave food;
they said, 'It has come to us.'
Food is purifying.
In that they contain [the word] 'purifying',
[it is] for the eating of food.
Pleasant does Agni become for him who has piled [the altar].
He causes it to be dragged across with seven;
seven indeed are the metres;
with the metres he appeases its heat.
He drags across with eight;
eight-syllabled is the gāyatrī;
Agni is of the gāyatrī [nature];
as great as Agni is, so great does he appease his heat.
Having swept it together, he throws it upon the rubbish-heap;
this indeed is its heat;
in heat he places its heat.
Against whomsoever he bears hatred, at him he should throw it;
with heat he strikes him.
In ghee taken five times, he throws five chips of gold.
Gold is brilliance, ghee is lifespan;
with brilliance he sprinkles over its lifespan.
Moreover, with a fivefold offering he grasps Agni.
'Homage to thy wrath, to thy heat,' [saying this] he steps onto [it];
for having paid homage, they approach a superior.
He sprinkles crosswise,
for non-sameness.
'To the one seated among men, veṭ,' [he says],
for firm standing.
Some gods are eaters of oblations, others are non-eaters of oblations;
let the fire-piler eat, he secures both of these;
since he both sacrifices and sprinkles down,
he pleases them, he secures them; both become pleased with him at the sacrifice.
With a heavy fistful he sprinkles down;
for that belongs most to Prajāpati;
thereby for him it is neither offered nor unoffered.
He makes two shoes of black-antelope skin;
the black-antelope skin is the form of the brahman;
Agni is death;
by the brahman he conceals [himself] from death;
he conceals [himself] from death.
He should make either shoe apart from food-eating;
he conceals [himself] from death;
he secures food-eating.
He who piles the fire is deprived of vital breaths, of cattle.
He steps onto it first, [saying,] 'Giver of out-breath, giver of circulating breath';
with the vital breaths he furnishes him.
'Giver of splendour, giver of room,' [he says];
splendour is offspring, room is cattle;
with offspring and with cattle he furnishes him.
'Giver of out-breath, giver of in-breath,' [saying this] he steps down,
according to form;
with two [verses];
two-footed is the sacrificer;
for firm standing.

Indra slew Vṛtra.
Slain, [Vṛtra] bound him with sixteen coils.
Having burnt away the coils of evil with Agni, he freed himself from evil by means of the two Vaiśvakarmana [verses].
When he offers with the [verse] addressed to Agni,
having burnt away the coils of evil with Agni, he frees himself from evil by means of the two Vaiśvakarmana [verses].
He should offer with [the verses] one by one if he should desire, 'May he be freed from evil slowly';
he is freed from evil slowly.
Having recited all at once, he should offer with the last if he should desire, 'May he be freed from evil swiftly';
he is freed swiftly from evil.
There are sixteen [verses];
man is of sixteen parts;
as great as his self is, so much of it does he free from evil.
Moreover, he had bound him with sixteen coils.
He offers with two different hymns,
for the different potency of the two hymns.
He places firewood [on the fire];
he sets apart a share for him;
just as one prepares provisions for someone who is coming, so is this hospitality made for Agni.
He places three [pieces of firewood];
for threefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, for him he prepares hospitality.
They are made of udumbara wood;
vigour is the udumbara;
he places vigour into him.
He anoints [them] with ghee;
this is the dear abode of Agni, namely ghee;
he prepares hospitality for his dear abode.
'Up may all the gods [bear] thee'—
the all-gods are the vital breaths;
by means of the vital breaths he lifts him up;
the all-gods are men;
by means of men he lifts him up.
'O Agni, may they bear [thee] with thoughts'—
for whatever thought he lifts him up, therewith he makes him prosper.
'The five directions'—
he leads him along the directions.
Two [verses] are enclosing,
for the smiting away of the rakṣases.
'The golden-haired, sun-rayed one'—
[it is a verse] addressed to Savitṛ,
for impulsion.
'The gods performing the sacrificial service stood for the gods'—
this is the milking of blessings.
'This measurer'—
this is the measurer of the heavenly world.
'The bull, the ocean, the ruddy, fair-winged'—with this he places a stone on the Āgnīdhra [hearth].
Without foundation, as it were, and without support is this intermediate space;
the Āgnīdhra is the intermediate space.
When he places [the stone] on the Āgnīdhra,
he provides a support.
'Placed in the midst of heaven, the dappled stone'—
the dappled [stone] is food;
he secures food.
With two [verses] he places [it];
two-footed is the sacrificer;
for stability.
With six [verses] they proceed from the Āgnīdhra;
six indeed are the seasons;
with the seasons indeed.
With four up to the tail;
four are the metres;
with the metres indeed.
'Indra'—
he secures indriya-power.
'They have magnified'—
he attains growth.
'The prizes'—
food is the prizes;
he secures food.
With an anuṣṭubh addressed to all the gods they proceed;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
speech belongs to all the gods;
all forms are this [anuṣṭubh];
all forms are made in the piled Agni;
therefore they proceed with this [verse].
'Invoking favour'—
the sacrifice is favour;
he secures the sacrifice.
'By the impulsion of the prize, with the elevation I have elevated [thee]'—
the elevation is the brahman, the depression is the brahman;
yonder sun rising is the elevation, setting it is the depression;
by the brahman he elevates himself, by the brahman he presses down the rival.
'Then may Indra and Agni cast my rivals apart in all directions'—
Indra and Agni are force and valour;
by force and valour he thrusts away the rival.

'Stride ye to the firmament with Agni,' [he says];
this is the striding onto the heavenly world.
'Go forward along the eastern quarter, knowing,' [he says];
he turns towards the world of the gods.
'Shining, illumine all regions,' [he says];
therefore Agni shines upon all directions.
'From the earth I have mounted into the intermediate space, from the intermediate space I have mounted into heaven,' [he says];
these indeed are the steps to these worlds.
'Going to heaven, they looked not back,' [he says];
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
'O Agni, go forward first of those who long for the gods,' [he says];
he places sight both in the gods and in men.
'Desiring to sacrifice, in harmony with the Bhṛgus,' [he says];
he leads him to the heavenly world.
With five [verses] he steps upon [it];
fivefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, having paid homage to him, he steps upon [him].
With 'Night and dawn,' [and] 'O Agni, thousand-eyed,' two linked verses, he offers;
he joins together day and night;
therefore day and night are joined.
He offers [the milk] of a black cow with a white calf;
by means of the day he causes the night to be given to him.
Of various forms indeed are cattle, of various vows;
he formed them in this very gift.
He offers with milk;
recognising this, cattle wait upon him.
He offers with sour milk on the naturally perforated [brick];
yonder indeed is the naturally perforated [brick], vigour is sour milk;
in yonder [world] he places vigour;
therefore from yonder they live upon vigour [flowing] hitherward.
'A fair-winged eagle art thou,' with a gāyatrī [verse] he sets [it] down;
on this [earth] he causes him to step.
'Fill the intermediate space with light,' with a triṣṭubh [verse];
into the intermediate space he causes him to step.
'Invoked, fair-faced in the east,' with a jagatī [verse];
in yonder [world] he causes him to step.
Having made him united with these deities, he establishes him.
With three [verses];
threefold indeed is Agni;
as great as Agni is, to that establishment he leads him.
With '[That] of Savitṛ,' with a verse to Savitṛ, he places [a kindling-stick];
for impulsion.
One made of udumbara wood;
vigour indeed is udumbara;
vigour he attaches to him.
Either the fire-piler milks Agni, or Agni milks the fire-piler.
Kaṇva indeed knew the milking of this higher good;
as they milk a given cow, just so the fire-piler milks Agni with this.
'O Agni, may we pay worship to thee,' [he says];
for prosperity.
One made of vikaṅkata wood;
when Agni was created, his splendour went into the vikaṅkata;
that indeed he wins.
'Kindled, O Agni, shine before us,' [with this verse he places] one made of śamī wood;
for appeasement.
With a virāj [verse];
this indeed is an iron tube with ears.
Somadakṣa Kauśreya asked the sacrificial session-attendees about this: 'Do you know the iron tube with ears?'
'We know that which is in the forest,' [they said].
Cattle indeed are its ears;
he wins cattle.
This vajra, a hundred-slayer, the gods hurled down at the asuras with 'For so much, to thee!';
he hurls down a vajra at his rival.
Lay him low!
'Seven, O Agni, are thy kindling-sticks,' [he says];
by this he gratifies the sets of seven.
He offers with a full ladle;
as for a new-born calf one fastens the teat, just so for him he fastens this portion.
He offers with sour milk in front, with ghee from above;
vigour indeed is sour milk, fiery energy is ghee;
vigour and fiery energy, united, he places in him at the end;
moreover, he causes both of these to be given to him.

Now this Apratiratha [hymn];
by this the gods conquered the asuras without resistance;
that is the Apratiratha's quality of being without resistance;
without resistance he conquers his rival with this.
This indeed is the Gāyatrī with wings of light;
by it he goes to the heavenly world.
It consists of ten verses;
the virāj has ten syllables, food is the virāj;
in the virāj, in food, he is established.
With this Bharadvāja went forth, arming Pratardana;
thence he became the kingdom.
Whom he should desire, 'May he be the kingdom,' him he should go forth arming with this;
he becomes the kingdom.
This is the oblation named 'Displacer of the world';
from post after post he thrusts his rival with this.
He should perform it in battle;
pointed or made of bāndhuka should the fuel be;
if the coals twist back, the army of the enemies twists back.
Now to Agni Vaiśvānara [an oblation] on twelve potsherds [is offered];
Agni Vaiśvānara is the year;
this is the dear body of the year, which is Vaiśvānara's;
when [it is offered] to Agni Vaiśvānara,
he shows hospitality to his dear body.
Vaiśvānara is the nobility, the Maruts are the commonalty;
when the [offerings] to the Maruts are offered after the Vaiśvānara,
he harnesses the commonalty to the nobility.
He recites the offering verse for the Vaiśvānara aloud, he offers the [offerings] to the Maruts silently;
therefore the nobility speaks over the commonalty.
Agni is cattle;
with Agni as their mouth Prajāpati created cattle;
the Maruts are cattle;
when the [offerings] to the Maruts are offered after the Vaiśvānara,
[it is] for the propagation of cattle.
If he should turn towards the east, he would be bewildered by the divine commonalty;
if towards the south, he would belong to the fathers;
if towards the west, he would belong to the rakṣases;
if towards the north, he would belong to men;
straight, firmly established, it must be offered;
for the establishment of the sacrificer.
Now these [offerings] to the Maruts
are on seven potsherds each;
for seven by seven are the Maruts, in sevenfold troops;
having recited troop after troop, he offers;
troop by troop he gratifies the Maruts.
With the troop that is to be recited in the forest, he offers in the middle;
this is the nobility of the Maruts, the others are the commonalty;
he places the nobility in the midst of the commonalty.
If he should desire, 'May I slay the nobility with the commonalty,' he should confuse with the others that troop which is to be recited in the forest;
with the commonalty he slays the nobility.
If he should desire, 'May I slay the commonalty with the nobility,' he should make the puronuvākyā and yājyā in triṣṭubh;
might and vigour are the triṣṭubh;
having taken away might and vigour from the commonalty, he binds it to the nobility.
Whom he should desire, 'May I thrust him from the nobility,' with that troop which is to be recited in the forest he should move the chariot-stand placed on the fire;
this indeed bears the nobility of the rājanya,
the nobility of the Maruts;
by the nobility he thrusts him from the nobility.
'Following the path of Indra, the divine clans, the Maruts,' [he says];
this indeed is the following path of the gods;
by the following path of the gods he makes a following path for the sacrificer.
He kindles him when he piles [the altar];
he inflames [him] with the names of the Maruts.

He offers the shower of wealth (vasordhārā).
Verily, this is offered with the thought, 'May there be a shower of wealth for me.'
Verily, this stream of ghee attends upon the sacrificer in the other world.
He offers continuously,
for the continuity of proper food,
for non-interruption.
For him whom he hates, he should interrupt [the stream];
he cuts off his proper food.
He offers with ghee.
Ghee is brilliance;
the shower of wealth is brilliance;
he places brilliance in brilliance.
Verily, this is offered for the ordering of what is not ordered,
for the obtaining of what is not obtained,
for the overcoming of what is not overcome.
'Out-breath and in-breath,' [he says];
he places out-breath and in-breath in the sacrificer.
'Food and sight,' [he says];
this indeed is the form of food;
by form alone he obtains food.
He offers these twelve [times] twelve.
Twelve are the months, [such is] the year;
by means of the year alone he obtains proper food.
'Agni and water,' [he says];
this indeed is the womb of food;
he obtains food together with its womb.
He offers [formulas] half-dedicated to Indra,
for obtaining the deities.
In that he offers [formulas] half-dedicated to Indra,
therefore Indra has the greatest share among the deities.
He makes Indra the higher one;
he establishes the sacrificer in sensory power.
'Aṃśu and Raśmi,' [he says];
Aṃśu and Raśmi are the mouth of the sacrifice;
he obtains the mouth of the sacrifice.
'Spoons and cups,' [he says];
these indeed are the forms of the sacrifice;
by forms alone he obtains the sacrifice.
'The final bath (avabhṛtha) and the call of Svagā,' [he says];
[it is] for stability.
'Agni and the hot cauldron (gharma),' [he says];
this indeed is Agni's holy radiance (brahmavarcasa);
he obtains just that.
'A two-and-a-half-year-old steer and a two-and-a-half-year-old heifer,' [he says];
so many indeed are the ages of cattle;
he obtains cattle endowed with their ages.
'One and three,' [he says];
one and three are the meter of men;
he obtains the meter of men.
'Four and eight,' [he says];
four and eight are the meter of the gods;
he obtains the meter of the gods.
The even and uneven stomas are a pair,
for procreation.
He forms a pair by increasing by two;
he procreates by increasing by four.
He offers [with numbers] up to thirty-three, saying, 'One and three';
thirty-three are the deities;
he obtains the deities.
Up to forty-eight, saying, 'Four and eight';
the jagatī has forty-eight syllables;
cattle belong to the jagatī;
he obtains cattle.
In this world he thrives through the meter of men, in that [other world] through the meter of the gods;
he wins both worlds.
'Strength and propagation,' [he says];
these are thirteen sacred utterances; the year has thirteen months;
at the end he ascends the year.
'May life-span succeed through the sacrifice,' [he says];
this is the ordering of the sacrifice.
'Ṛc and sāman,' [he says];
the shower of wealth is proper food;
having obtained proper food through the shower of wealth, having become an eater of food,
'Stoma and yajus,' [he says];
stoma and yajus are divine rulership;
at the end he enters into divine rulership.

The gods ran a race for the propagation of strength (vājaprasavya);
Agni won it.
When he offers the vājaprasavya [oblations],
[it is] for the winning of proper food.
He offers of each kind of food;
he obtains all food.
From whatever [food] he does not offer, he is deprived of that.
If he should not offer of something, he should sprinkle it on the kindling-wood;
thereby he obtains it.
He offers with an udumbara spoon;
the udumbara is vigor;
through vigor he obtains proper food.
This is the consecration of Agni;
therefore, one who has piled the fire should not run when it rains;
if he runs, he runs away from proper food.
Now, these are the kingdom-holders (rāṣṭrabhṛt);
he who is piled is the royal power among the fires;
these verily uphold his kingdom;
that is the kingdom-holders' nature as kingdom-holders;
through them he hands over the kingdom to him.
These are six;
six are the seasons;
by means of the seasons he hands over the kingdom to him;
moreover, he is established in the seasons.
The offerings are pairs,
for procreation.
Twelve [are they];
twelve are the months, [such is] the year;
by means of the year he hands over the kingdom to him;
moreover, he is established in the year.
He offers the thirteenth in the front of the chariot;
the chariot is a vajra;
by means of the vajra he conquers the directions;
having conquered the directions, he goes forth in his chariot.
He offers the wind-oblations;
he obtains the wind.
Verily, this [wind] blows upon the fire-piler in that world.
He offers thrice;
three are these worlds;
from these worlds he obtains the wind;
these worlds blow the wind for him.
He offers with hollowed hands;
Vāyu is [taken] by the portion of the hand;
one cannot reach Vāyu with [cut] portions;
all at once he reaches him.
'Thou art the ocean, misty,' [he says];
these are the forms of the wind;
by forms alone he obtains the wind.
The gāyatrī is the out-breath;
the Havirdhāna cart-shed belongs to the gāyatrī.
The triṣṭubh is the circulating breath;
the Āgnīdhra belongs to the triṣṭubh.
The jagatī is the in-breath;
the sadas belongs to the jagatī.
'Set in the middle of heaven, the dappled stone,' [he says];
that dappled stone is breath-of-life (asu);
he places breath-of-life between out-breath and in-breath,
for the separation of out-breath and in-breath.
He places twenty-one [bricks] at the Hotṛ's hearth;
the twenty-one-fold is the support of the stomas;
[it is] for stability.
Eleven at the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin's [hearth];
the triṣṭubh has eleven syllables;
the triṣṭubh is virility;
he obtains virility.
Eight in each of the others;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
he is established in the gāyatrī.
Six in the Mārjālīya;
six are the seasons;
the seasons carried this around to the south;
the seasons are the Fathers;
therefore six are placed here.

Just as the re-establishing of the fire (punarādheya) is, so is the re-piling (punaściti).
He who does not prosper by the establishment of the fires sets up the re-establishing of the fire;
if one should not prosper by the piled fire, he should pile the re-piling;
he does indeed prosper.
The first layer is the self;
the second, offspring;
the third, grandson.
Piling the third fire, he should place these [bricks] in the middle layer,
for continuity.
This fire is called Napātka ('having a grandson');
he eats food who, knowing thus, places these.
He places eight of these;
eight syllables has the gāyatrī;
he establishes himself in the gāyatrī.
Whatever oblation the fire-piler offers on [a hearth] without bricks, that leaks away;
after it leaking away, the sacrifice leaks away;
after the sacrifice, the sacrificer.
When these are placed,
[it is] for the establishing of the oblations.
After them being established, the sacrifice is established;
after the sacrifice, the sacrificer.
And he places eight of them;
eight syllables has the gāyatrī;
Agni belongs to the gāyatrī;
as great as Agni is, so great he piles him.
Having placed these eight, he covers [them] over with loose earth;
eight syllables has the gāyatrī;
the gāyatrī is the brahman;
with the brahman he piles him.
With the gāyatrī metre, having placed eleven [bricks] with the Lokaṃpṛṇā verse, he covers [them] over with loose earth;
eleven syllables has the triṣṭubh;
the triṣṭubh is manly strength;
with manly strength he piles him.
With the triṣṭubh metre, having placed twelve with the Lokaṃpṛṇā verse, he covers [them] over with loose earth;
twelve syllables has the jagatī;
cattle belong to the jagatī;
with cattle he piles him.
With the jagatī metre [there are] three layers, three earth-fillings;
that amounts to six;
six are the seasons;
he establishes himself in the seasons.
Concerning this they say:
'This is the high altar; whatever former piled fire of his there may be, he should sacrifice having settled near to it.'
Just as he adds more to [a fire] already burning, in the same way he places fiery energy upon this prior [fire].
Concerning this they say:
'Who ought to awaken a superior who is fast asleep?'
When he sacrifices,
he strengthens him with the oblation.
When he piles the fire, he makes a house.
Whatever he gains elsewhere, he brings to his house.
Whether he sacrifices with a regular offering, or with an animal sacrifice, or with Soma, whatever former piled fire of his there may be, he should sacrifice having settled near to it;
just as having gained elsewhere one brings it to his house, exactly so is that.

Having borne the [fire] in the pan (ukhya) for a year, when the second year has elapsed, he should offer an eight-pot-sherd [cake] to Agni, an eleven-pot-sherd [cake] to Indra, a twelve-pot-sherd [cake] to the All-Gods, a pap to Bṛhaspati, and a three-pot-sherd [cake] to Viṣṇu; when the third year has elapsed, he should sacrifice with the Abhijit [rite].
In that it is of eight pot-sherds,
eight syllables has the gāyatrī;
the morning pressing belongs to Agni and to the gāyatrī;
thereby he held fast the morning pressing and the gāyatrī metre.
In that it is of eleven pot-sherds,
eleven syllables has the triṣṭubh;
the midday pressing belongs to Indra and to the triṣṭubh;
thereby he held fast the midday pressing and the triṣṭubh metre.
In that it is of twelve pot-sherds,
twelve syllables has the jagatī;
the third pressing belongs to the All-Gods and to the jagatī;
thereby he held fast the third pressing and the jagatī metre.
In that it is to Bṛhaspati,
Bṛhaspati is the brahman;
thereby he held fast the brahman.
In that it is to Viṣṇu,
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
thereby he held fast the sacrifice.
In that he sacrifices with the Abhijit,
[it is] for conquest (abhijiti).
In that he bears the [fire] in the pan for a year,
thereby he wins this world.
In that he piles a second [fire],
thereby he wins the intermediate space.
In that he sacrifices,
thereby he wins that world yonder.
Para Āṭnāra, Kakṣīvant Auśija, and Trasadasyu Paurukutsa, desiring offspring, piled this [fire];
then they obtained a thousand sons each.
He extends; he goes to that abundance to which they went, who, knowing thus, piles this [fire].

Prajāpati piled this fire in the beginning by means of the seasons, the year: with spring the front part, with summer the right wing, with the rains the left, with autumn the tail, with winter the middle.
With the priesthood (brahman) he piled its front part, with the nobility (kṣatra) the right wing, with the common people the left, with the beasts the tail, with food-desire the middle.
So much indeed is [the world];
as much as there is, that he wins.
They hear of him having piled the fire; he eats food, he shines.
'What is the first layer? What is the loose earth (purīṣa)?' say the theologians.
This [earth] is the first layer, the plants are the loose earth;
the atmosphere is the second, the birds are the loose earth;
the sky is the third, the constellations are the loose earth;
the sacrifice is the fourth, the sacrificial gift (dakṣiṇā) is the loose earth;
the self is the fifth, the offspring is the loose earth.
If he should pile [it] as having three layers, he would cut off the sacrifice, the sacrificial gift, the self, and offspring;
therefore it should be piled as having five layers.
In that there are three layers,
threefold indeed is Agni;
in that there are two,
two-footed is the sacrificer.
Three:
these worlds;
in these worlds indeed he prospers.

In harmony the year with the Āyus,
in harmony the Dawn with the ruddy ones,
in harmony the Sun with Etaśa,
in concord the Aśvins with wonderful deeds,
in harmony Vaiśvānara with the sacrificial food,
with ghee, svāhā!
The wondrous face of the gods has arisen, the eye of Mitra, Varuṇa, and Agni; / he has filled heaven and earth and the atmosphere, the sun, the soul of what moves and stands.
Yoke, O Agni, like a charioteer, your horses that are most invoked by gods; / sit down as the ancient Hotṛ!
For yoke, O god Agni, those horses of yours that are good / and swift, that carry effectively!
You are the vehicle of Agni;
you are Agni's related vehicles;
you are the vehicle of Vāyu;
you are Vāyu's related vehicles;
you are the vehicle of the gods;
you are the gods' divine vehicles;
you are the vehicle of the atmosphere;
you are the atmosphere's related vehicles;
you are the dweller in the atmosphere;
sit in the atmosphere!
Yavas, Āyavas, Ūmas, Evas, the year, the ocean, well-measured;
for the flood, you;
for grace, you;
for truth, you;
for intention, you;
for the prudent one, you;
for heaven, you;
for light, you;
for the shining one, you;
for the Ādityas, you;
for the ṛc, you;
for brightness, you;
for brilliance, you;
for light, you;
for the sun, you;
you are the winner of the east wind;
you are the winner of cloud;
you are the winner of lightning;
you are the winner of thunder;
you are the winner of rain!
With the eastern direction, with the deity Agni, with the gāyatrī metre, I place the head of Agni;
with the gāyatrī metre, with the metre, with Agni's head, I place the head of Agni.
With the southern direction, with the deity Indra, with the triṣṭubh metre, I place the side of Agni;
with the triṣṭubh metre, with the metre, with Agni's side, I place the side of Agni.
With the western direction, with the deity Savitṛ, with the jagatī metre, I place the tail of Agni;
with the jagatī metre, with the metre, with Agni's tail, I place the tail of Agni.
With the northern direction, with the deity Mitrā-Varuṇau, with the anuṣṭubh metre, I place the side of Agni;
with the anuṣṭubh metre, with the metre, with Agni's side, I place the side of Agni.
With the upward direction, with the deity Bṛhaspati, with the paṅkti metre, I place the back of Agni;
with the paṅkti metre, with the metre, with Agni's back, he places the back of Agni.
The Gatherer (Saṃyat) and the Wise (Pracetas), of Agni, of Soma, of Sūrya—
these are their overlords;
whom we hate and who hates us, him I place in their jaws!
The Fierce (Ugrā) and the Terrible (Bhīmā), of the Fathers, of Yama, of Indra—
these are their overlords;
whom we hate and who hates us, him I place in their jaws!
The Firm (Dhruvā) and the Earth (Pṛthivī), of Savitṛ, of the Maruts, of Varuṇa—
these are their overlords;
whom we hate and who hates us, him I place in their jaws!
The Restrainer (Yantrī) and the Controller (Yamanī), of Mitrā-Varuṇau, of Mitra, of Dhātṛ—
these are their overlords;
whom we hate and who hates us, him I place in their jaws!
The Eastward (Prācī) and the Westward (Pratīcī), of the Vasus, of the Rudras, of the Ādityas—
these are their overlords;
whom we hate and who hates us, him I place in their jaws!

"In harmony the year with the Āyus":
the year is the abda;
the months are the Āyus, the dawn the ruddy ones, the sun Etaśa, these [two] the Aśvins;
the year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
cattle are the sacrificial food;
cattle are ghee.
Along with the year cattle are propagated;
from the year he propagates cattle for him.
He offers in a clump of darbha grass.
Whatever un-afflicted [part], whatever vigor this [earth] has, that rises up here;
in whatever is her un-afflicted [part], whatever is her vigor, in that he offers.
He who has piled the fire does not die, for whom they offer thus.
With "The wondrous face of the gods has arisen", he offers on the human head.
Mitra is the day, Varuṇa the night;
so much indeed is [the world];
as much as there is, that he wins.
He offers with a solar verse;
therefore, among animals, man alone [stands] facing the sun; the others are bent downward.
In that he offers twice with the same verse,
therefore man, being symmetrical, has two legs.
Whoever indeed does not yoke the fire when the yoking has come, does not yoke among those who yoke.
With "Yoke, O Agni, those most invoked by gods" [and] "For yoke, O Agni, those of yours":
he yokes the fire itself when the yoking has come;
he yokes among those who yoke, who knows thus.
He yokes with two [verses];
not single [is] a pair.
Whoever indeed does not unyoke the fire when the unyoking has come, does not unyoke among those who unyoke.
With "Like a mother her son, the earth [holds] the one belonging to the loose earth":
he unyokes the fire itself when the unyoking has come;
he unyokes among those who unyoke, who knows thus.
Now these are the Saṃyānī [bricks];
these deities belong to the heavenly world;
by them indeed he goes to the heavenly world.
This [fire] is the gāyatrī with wings of light;
by it indeed he goes to the heavenly world.
Just as one travels on together by boat from water to water, even so by these the fire-piler travels on together yonder according to his world;
that is the travelling-power of the Saṃyānī [bricks].
Now these are the names of the seasons;
he wins the seasons themselves.
"Sumeka" [it says];
the year is indeed sumeka;
he wins the year itself.
Now these are the Ādityeṣṭakās [bricks of the Ādityas];
the Ādityas take away the fiery energy of him who, having piled the fire, does not seem to shine.
When these are laid down,
just as one places more upon a blazing fire, even so he places fiery energy upon it in proper sequence;
the Ādityas do not take away his fiery energy;
as that [sun] is among the gods, so he becomes among men.
Now these are the Ghṛteṣṭakās [ghee-bricks];
Agni is fiery energy, ghee is fiery energy;
in fiery energy he places fiery energy.
Now these are the Vṛṣṭisanis [rain-winning bricks];
by these he wins rain itself.
By these it rains for the fire-piler yonder according to his desire;
that is the rain-winning nature of the Vṛṣṭisanis.
Now these are the body (ātman);
Śaṅkha Kauṣya said this to his son who had piled the fire: "Did you pile the fire?"
"I piled it indeed!"
"Then how is it that you did not go with your whole body (sarvātman)?"
"For that indeed we certainly did not know!"
When these are laid down,
he piles the fire with its body (ātman);
they are pierced again;
he unites breath upon breath itself.
Now this is the sixth layer;
he who, having piled the fire, should not seem to be firmly established, he should lay down this sixth layer;
six are the seasons, the year [is sixfold];
he becomes firmly established in the seasons, in the year;
these deities belong to the heavenly world;
by them indeed he goes to the heavenly world.
These deities are suitable for sorcery (abhicāra);
whomever he hates, of him he should say: "I place that one in your jaws!";
he cuts him off from these deities;
quickly he perishes.

The fire is to be piled according to its proper form.
For he who piles the fire according to its proper form, for him nothing deformed is born.
With the gāyatrī he should touch the first layer when piled;
with the triṣṭubh the second;
with the jagatī the third;
with the anuṣṭubh the fourth;
with the paṅkti the fifth.
He piles the fire according to its proper form; nothing deformed is born to him.
The first layer is the out-breath, the second the circulating breath, the third the in-breath;
with the out-breath and the in-breath he enkindles it.
This is the division of the sacrificial food (iḍā);
cattle are the sacrificial food;
with cattle he piles it.
The fire is to be piled having announced it to Prajāpati.
He who piles the fire without having announced it to Prajāpati incurs misfortune.
Two horses should stand on either side: a brown one on the north, a white one on the south.
Touching them, he should lay down each brick.
This is the form of Prajāpati;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati.
Having announced it to Prajāpati he piles the fire; he does not incur misfortune.
He gives to the Brahman priest a golden vessel filled with honey;
he becomes sweet.
[It is] of a hundred measures;
man has a hundred years of life, a hundredfold vigor;
life and vigor he secures.
With the [verse] containing the word 'wondrous' (citravat)—'The wondrous face of the gods has arisen'—he looks at it;
wondrous indeed he becomes.
At midday he causes the horse to smell [the fire];
that [sun] is the Āditya;
this Indra is Prajāpati;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati;
manifestly he prospers him.
He who knows the obtaining of the fire obtains that wish for which wish the fire is piled.
About to set forth, he should attend upon the piled fire with: 'By consecration (dīkṣā) I have obtained you; by austerity (tapas) I have obtained you; by soma-pressing (sutyā) I have obtained you; by the purificatory bath (avabhṛtha) I have obtained you; by the barren cow (vaśā) I have obtained you.'
That amounts to five;
five are the seasons;
the seasons are the year;
the year is Agni Vaiśvānara;
he obtains that wish for which wish the fire is piled.
'There is only one brick,' say the theologians;
'Speech is the one brick';
'The brāhmaṇa is the one brick';
'These worlds are [the bricks].'
For this the Śarkarākṣyas indeed used to say:
'We know only three bricks as the fire to be piled: these worlds.'
The fire is to be piled endowed with truth.
With 'bhūḥ' he should touch the first naturally perforated [brick];
with 'bhuvaḥ' the second;
with 'svar' the third.
This is the truth of speech;
endowed with truth he piles the fire.
'Have you piled the fire endowed with truth or lacking truth?' say the theologians.
He indeed piles it endowed with truth who piles it thus.
'For which deity is the fire piled?' say the theologians.
'For Prajāpati alone.'
'What is his oblation?'
'The self alone.'

They build the hall with beams pointing east.
The eastern direction is the direction of the gods;
he turns toward the world of the gods.
Yonder sun is in the east;
he ascends toward yonder sun.
They sacrifice for him in an enclosed [place];
having enclosed the world of the gods, they consecrate him in it.
He indeed departs from this world who goes to yonder world;
yonder world is, as it were, concealed from here.
He should make light-holes in the corners;
thereby he does not depart from this world.
He offers an eleven-potsherd cake to Agni and Viṣṇu.
Agni is all the deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
Having taken hold of both the deities and the sacrifice, he enters upon the consecration.
Agni is a purifier (pavitra), Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
purified by the purifier, he takes hold of the sacrifice.
Because it is on eight potsherds,
thereby it belongs to Agni;
because it is on three potsherds,
thereby it belongs to Viṣṇu;
thereby he does not depart from either.
He offers an eleven-potsherd cake to Agni and Viṣṇu.
Agni is the lowest of the gods, Viṣṇu the highest;
just as one binds the two ends with a tie, even so, having grasped them from both sides, he places the deities in the middle.
There should be a cake for one desiring splendour of sacred knowledge (brahmavarcasa);
the cake is the head of the sacrifice;
the cake is splendour of sacred knowledge;
he becomes possessed of splendour of sacred knowledge.
There should be a caru cooked in ghee for one desiring offspring or desiring cattle.
This is a pair: ghee and rice-grains;
ghee is the milk of the cow, rice-grains of the draft-ox;
he places a pair at the mouth of the sacrifice
for procreation.
From whatever form this caru [is],
for, vibrating as it were, the consecrated one goes to yonder world,
for he is consecrated toward yonder world;
that there is a caru
is for prosperity.
He anoints [his eyes];
he anoints [his body];
he bathes;
he puts on a garment;
these, indeed, are the bodies of man;
having become complete in body, he enters upon the consecration.
Man is defective within, impure;
that he eats
is for purity, for freedom from defects.
In this way, indeed, [one is] in yonder world as one being consecrated;
as he thinks, "Thus it will not burn me," so should he fast;
moreover, in such a way that, being consecrated, he may fast from lesser [food], so should he eat.
He shaves his hair and beard, clips his nails, cleans his teeth;
this in man is dead, impure;
having struck away the impure, he approaches the sacrificial substance.
He bathes.
The Aṅgirases, having placed the consecration in the waters, went to the heavenly world;
that he bathes,
thereby he takes hold of that consecration.
He should bathe up to the neck,
for having placed it neck-deep, they went;
with whatever amount of water he bathes, by that he takes hold of that consecration.
He should not bathe in flowing [waters];
if he should bathe in flowing [waters], dominion (kṣatra) would go to renown.
He should not bathe in well-[waters];
if he should bathe in well-[waters], the common people (viś) would go to renown.
He should bathe in standing [waters];
these among waters are consecrated, rich in cattle, which are standing;
hairy, continuous; cattle are hairy;
he bestows renown upon the sacrificer.
He should bathe at a sacred bathing-place (tīrtha);
he becomes a sacred bathing-place of his equals.

The kindler of directions through food-desire, finding heaven for us, sweet, from here may the sweet one protect us;
may Agni the god, hard to cross, undeceived, quicken this holy power (brahman), may he protect us.
May the Rathaṃtara protect us with the sāmans, the Gāyatrī, all-formed among the metres;
may the Trivṛt stoma with its placement among days, the ocean, the wind, fill this strength (ojas).
The fierce, the overcoming of directions, conferring vigour, clean, pure in the day of strength;
through the overlordships of Indra may it fill us from here; may it support this great dominion on all sides.
The Bṛhat sāman, bearing dominion, of increased virility, the Triṣṭubh, strength, adorned, of fierce heroes;
O Indra, with the fifteenfold stoma, with the wind, with the ocean, protect this strength.
The eastern among directions together with food-desire for us, all the gods, finding heaven through the rainy season among days;
may this dominion be hard to cross, strength unassailable, thousandfold, mighty.
With the Vairūpa sāman may I be able to do that; with the Jagatī I introduce him into the clans;
all the gods with the seventeenfold [stoma] into the middle, this dominion, the billow, the fierce wind.
The restrainer of directions has held this dominion, the womb, food-desire, possessed of Mitra, this strength;
Mitra and Varuṇa, moving swiftly with autumn among days, may this strength be of undeceived dominion.
Upon the Vairāja sāman is my thought, gathered by the Anuṣṭubh, hard-to-overcome might;
this dominion, rich in friends, giving moisture, strength, O Mitra and Varuṇa, protect with overlordships.
The sovereign among directions, together with the sāman, mighty, may the winter season protect us through its placement;
the Bṛhatī, wishing for help, the Śakvarī like a wind, may she who faces ghee favour this sacrifice.
Possessed of light, easily milked, rich in milk, may she who faces ghee favour this sacrifice;
thou art our herdsman, helper, and restrainer; O Bṛhaspati, yoke restraining speech.
The upward among directions, the delight, the food-desire of plants, with the year, may Savitṛ [protect] us among days;
the Revat sāman and the Atichandas metre; be thou without enemies, easy to invoke for us.
In the thirty-threefold stoma, O wife of the world, in the wind shining forth, sing praise unto us;
rich in ghee under the overlordship of Savitṛ, rich in milk, may the delight, the food-desire, be ours.
"The golden germ..."
"He who breathes..."
The virāj of directions, wife of Viṣṇu, not terrible, ruling over this might, who is the recipient of desire;
all-pervading, invigorating, well-become, may Aditi be kindly to us in her lap.
The prop of heaven, supporter of the earth, ruling over this might, the wife of Viṣṇu;
may Bṛhaspati, Mātariśvan, and Vāyu, the blowing winds, sing praise to us.
"Vaiśvānara for our help..."
"Questioned in heaven..."
"Afterwards indeed, O Anumati, thou..."
"What name of thine..."

The consecrated one is an oblation.
An oblation not smeared with ghee does not attain sacrificial fitness.
When he anoints him,
he sprinkles ghee upon him
for sacrificial fitness.
Ghee belongs to the gods; clarified butter [belongs] to men; half-churned butter to the gandharvas; butter melted by itself to the sun.
Fresh butter is neither among the gods nor among men.
The consecrated one is neither among the gods nor among men,
having fallen from this world [and] not having reached yonder [world].
When he anoints himself with fresh butter,
[it is] for proper fulfillment.
He puts on a garment.
The hem belongs to Agni; the waistband to the ancestors; the fringe of the border to the plants; the wind-protector to Vāyu; the warps and the woofs to the All-gods; the eyelets to the nakṣatras.
The garment belongs to all the gods; the consecrated one belongs to all the gods.
He equips him with all the deities.
He applies collyrium.
When Indra had slain Vṛtra, the demons pursued him;
he blocked them off by means of the collyrium-mountain.
The demons pursue him who is consecrated,
for so it happens.
When he applies collyrium,
[it is] to block off the demons.
Indra slew Vṛtra;
his eye flew away;
it entered Mount Trikakubh.
When he applies collyrium,
[it is] to win that very eye.
He applies [it] twice to the right [eye], once to the left.
The sacrifice is threefold;
he undertakes the sacrifice from the very beginning.
He applies [it] thrice to the right, twice to the left.
The sacrifice is fivefold;
he wins the sacrifice.
He applies [it] first to the right [eye],
for men apply [it] first to the left;
[this is] for differentiation.
He applies [it] with a yajus;
for men apply [it] without a yajus;
[this is] for differentiation.
He applies [it] moving outward;
he does not run it back;
for men apply [it] back and forth;
[this is] for differentiation.
He applies [it] with a reed-stalk;
for men apply [it] with a porcupine quill;
[this is] for differentiation.
It has a tuft;
that which has a tuft belongs to Mitra; that which is withered belongs to Varuṇa; the consecrated one belongs to Mitra;
[it is] for proper fulfillment.
Deficient, as it were, defective, as it were, is the eye;
when he applies collyrium,
he fills that very [defect];
[it is] for defectlessness.
He purifies [him].
Indra slew Vṛtra;
he slew him right upon the waters.
Those of them that were fit for sacrifice, pure,
they stepped forth;
they became the plants.
The brilliance that was theirs, that is the darbha grass;
therefore it is a purifier;
therefore with darbha blades they purify the waters.
When he purifies [him] with bunches of darbha grass,
he purifies him with the brilliance of the waters and the plants.
He purifies [him] with two [stalks].
Two are truth and untruth;
with truth alone he purifies his untruth.
Two are the in-breath and the out-breath.
He purifies [him] with three [stalks];
for the gods are thrice-truthful;
moreover, three are the breaths: the in-breath, the circulating breath, and the out-breath;
these he places in the sacrificer.
He purifies [him] with five [stalks].
Man is fivefold;
as great as his self is, so much of him he purifies.
He purifies [him] with seven [stalks].
Seven are the metres;
with the metres he purifies him.
He purifies [him] with nine [stalks].
Nine are the breaths;
with the breaths he purifies him.
He purifies [him] with twenty-one [stalks].
Man is twenty-one-fold: ten fingers on the hands, ten toes on the feet, the self is the twenty-first;
as great as his self is, so much of him he purifies.
"May the lord of thought purify you," [he says];
the lord of thought is holy power (brahman);
by holy power he purifies him.
"May the lord of speech purify you," [he says];
for he is the purifier.
"May the divine Savitṛ purify you," [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ, he purifies him with the deities.
"With an unbroken purifier," [he says];
without a flaw he purifies him.
"With the rays of the sun," [he says];
with the very body of the sun he purifies him.
"With the purifier belonging to thee, the lord of the purifier," [he says];
the lord of the purifier is the sacrifice;
for the sacrifice he purifies this.
"May I be able to achieve that for which I purify [thee]," [he says];
"May I be able to achieve the sacrifice," is what he thereby says.
He purifies him from front to back.
From front to back is the breath of man;
when the breath goes crosswise, then he perishes.
If he were to purify [him] crosswise, he would make his breath go crosswise; he would perish.
He makes his breath run along his body.
He purifies [him] outside;
having purified him in the human world, he leads him purified to the world of the gods.

To the Svarga world indeed they go by that which is the introductory [sacrifice] (prāyaṇīya).
That is the introductory nature of the introductory sacrifice.
He is cut off from this world who goes to that world,
when the concluding [sacrifice] (udayanīya) belongs to Aditi (āditya).
He establishes himself firmly in this very world.
The gods, having slain the Asuras and fleeing from blood-money, confused the directions, thinking: 'Lest another should know this after [us]'.
Regarding that they say:
'The Sādhyas indeed were the gods prior to the gods.
They, having confused the directions, went to the Svarga world,
thinking: "Lest another should know this world after [us]".'
These lower gods sought to know after them;
they did not know [them].
Aditi said: 'I choose a boon. I shall proclaim these directions to you, provided that the sacrifice begins with me and concludes with me.'
Therefore the introductory sacrifice belongs to Aditi, and the concluding sacrifice belongs to Aditi,
for the boon was chosen by her.
'Sacrifice to Pathyā Svasti,' she said; 'by her you will know the eastern direction.'
'Sacrifice to Agni,' she said; 'by him the southern.'
'Sacrifice to Soma,' she said; 'by him the western.'
'Sacrifice to Savitṛ,' she said; 'by him the northern.'
'I alone know these two worlds,' she said; 'this is Aditi; hers is this upward direction.'
Upward indeed by that they went to the Svarga world.
These very deities are sacrificed to in this manner,
for the arrangement of the directions,
for the discernment of the Svarga world.
By Pathyā Svasti the gods knew the eastern direction;
Agni and Soma looked along together;
Savitṛ impelled;
this is Aditi: upon her they extended the sacrifice eastward.
That which is the introductory [sacrifice] is gathered as the opening of the sacrifice.
Speech is Pathyā Svasti.
When he sacrifices to Pathyā Svasti,
he gathers speech itself.
He gathers the out-breath and the in-breath by Agni and Soma,
Savitṛ for impelling.
This is Aditi:
upon her he gathers the opening of the sacrifice.
That which is the introductory sacrifice is offered for order.
The Maruts are the divine clan;
when the divine clan is arranged, the human clan is arranged after [it].
When there is the quarter-verse containing the Maruts in the offering-verse: 'O Maruts, grant well-being for wealth',
he arranges the divine clan itself;
when it is arranged, the human clan is arranged after [it].
He sacrifices to Pathyā Svasti;
therefore yonder sun rises in the east without interruption and sets in the west,
for he moves along Pathyā Svasti.
He sacrifices to Agni;
therefore plants ripen from the south forward as they come,
for plants belong to Agni.
He sacrifices to Soma;
therefore the waters flow mostly westward,
for the waters belong to Soma.
He sacrifices to Savitṛ;
therefore this [wind] blows mostly from the north,
for impelled by Savitṛ he blows.
He sacrifices to Aditi;
therefore she rains from above for the creatures.
He sacrifices to these five deities;
the sacrifice has five parts;
he obtains the sacrifice itself.
He cuts off a sixth [portion] for Agni;
there are six seasons;
he firmly establishes himself in the seasons themselves.
There are ten offering- and invitatory-verses;
the Virāj has ten syllables; food is the Virāj;
he firmly establishes himself in the Virāj, in food.
There are two for the Sviṣṭakṛt, [making] twelve;
the year has twelve months,
for the attainment of the year.
There are seventeen kindling-verses;
the year has seventeen parts;
five seasons, twelve months: this indeed is that year;
from the year itself he extends the opening of the sacrifice.

By the cow for purchasing Soma the third pressing is to be obtained.
Cattle indeed are the third pressing.
Because he purchases with a cow,
by that alone he obtains the third pressing.
He measures [Soma] on a red hide.
Therefore cattle are mostly of red form.
He should not consent to its being picked apart.
He should not be an onlooker of it while being picked apart.
Soma is the sovereign lord of plants.
He tears away what is swallowed by him; he becomes hungry.
Therefore the Soma-seller is hungry.
He purchases near water;
he purchases it possessed of sap.
He purchases with gold;
he purchases it possessed of splendour.
He purchases with a she-goat;
he purchases it possessed of ascetic heat.
He purchases with a milch-cow;
he purchases it possessed of the milk-mixture.
He purchases with a draft-ox;
he obtains the bearer of the sacrifice.
He purchases with a young bull and a young cow;
he purchases a pair for him.
He purchases with a bull;
he purchases it possessed of Indra.
As to that, they say: ‘He bargains with Prajāpati who bargains with a bull.’
Whatever [male] has testicles but is devoid of semen, with that he should purchase;
by that alone he purchases it possessed of Indra,
[and] does not bargain with Prajāpati.
He purchases with ten [articles];
the Virāj has ten syllables;
the Virāj is food;
he becomes established in the Virāj, in food-consumption.

‘You are swift,’ [he says].
Speech indeed is the cow for purchasing Soma;
for by speech one hastens this way and that.
Looking at the cow for purchasing Soma, he offers an oblation.
Speech indeed is the cow for purchasing Soma;
looking at her, he grasps her.
‘Held by the mind,’ [he says];
for by the mind speech is held.
‘Pleasing to Viṣṇu,’ [he says].
Viṣṇu indeed is the sacrifice;
he makes her pleasing to the sacrifice.
‘In the impulse of you, whose strength is true, may I attain the support of the body, svāhā!’ [he says].
He who attains the support of her body flourishes.
Impelled by Savitṛ, he attains the support of her body.
With the svāhā-call he grasps her.
Having announced it to yonder Āditya, Soma is to be purchased:
‘O divine Sūrya, we shall purchase Soma.’
Having announced it to yonder Āditya, he purchases Soma.
‘You are thought,’ [he says].
Speech indeed is the cow for purchasing Soma;
he instructs her;
therefore instructed offspring are born.
‘You are intellect,’ [he says];
for by the mind speech is held.
‘You are contemplation,’ [he says];
for by speech one contemplates this way and that.
‘You are the sacrificial fee,’ [he says];
for she is the sacrificial fee.
‘You are worthy of sacrifice,’ [he says];
for she is worthy of sacrifice.
‘You belong to the ruling class,’ [he says];
for she belongs to the ruling class.
Moreover, whom she favours, he becomes a noble.
‘You are Aditi, facing both ways,’ [he says];
for she is Aditi, facing both ways;
for on both sides she encompasses the sacrifice;
the introductory [sacrifice] belongs to Aditi, the concluding [sacrifice] belongs to Aditi.
‘Become well-turned to the east, well-turned to the west for me,’ [he says].
He makes her well-turned to the east and well-turned to the west.
‘May Mitra bind you by the foot,’ [he says].
If she were bound by the foot, she would belong to the fathers.
Speech indeed is the cow for purchasing Soma;
the snare belongs to Varuṇa;
speech seized by Varuṇa would not speak.
If she were seized by the ear, she would be Vārtraghnī.
If she were unchecked [and] restrained—
‘May Mitra bind you by the foot,’ [he says].
Mitra indeed is the sacred lore;
by sacred lore alone he binds her by the foot.
‘May Pūṣan protect the paths,’ [he says].
This [earth] indeed is Pūṣan;
to her he entrusts her.
‘For Indra, the overseer,’ [he says].
Indra indeed is the mightiest of the gods;
him he makes her overseer.
‘May your mother consent, may your father [consent],’ [he says];
for both her father and mother are those who give consent.
‘Go, O goddess, to the god, to Soma for Indra,’ [he says];
for as a goddess she goes to the god.
‘To Soma for Indra; may Rudra turn you back,’ [he says].
Rudra indeed is the swiftest of the gods;
him he places beyond her.
[‘For the other shoots,’ he says.]
‘Return safely, accompanied by Soma,’ [he says].
He brings well-being for her return.

The consecrated one should not offer an oblation.
The consecrated one indeed is an oblation; Rudra is Agni.
If he were to offer, he would surrender himself, become an oblation, to Rudra.
If he were not to offer, he would exclude a joint of the sacrifice.
If the Soma has been purchased, he should offer in the fire of the consecrated one.
For then the sacrifice has been released;
the oblations go forth.
If it has not been purchased, taking the two churning-sticks and clarified butter, going away and churning fire, he should offer at the end of the sacrificial post with a verse to Viṣṇu.
For the sacrificial post belongs to Viṣṇu;
he approaches it with its own deity.
"I have gone past others, I have not approached others," [he says].
For he goes past others and does not approach others.
"Those whom I have not favored, them I have passed by," [he says].
For those whom he does not favor, he passes by.
"Hitherward from the farther ones have I found you, beyond the nearer ones," [he says].
For he finds it hitherward from the farther ones, beyond the nearer ones.
"Thee, such, O tree, we favor for the worship of the gods," [he says].
For he favors it for the worship of the gods.
"Welcome to Viṣṇu," [he says].
Viṣṇu indeed is the sacrifice;
he makes it welcome to the sacrifice itself.
"May the god Savitṛ anoint thee with honey," [he says].
Ghee indeed is the honey of the gods;
he makes it pure and worthy of sacrifice.
"O plant, protect it," [he says].
He makes, as it were, armor [for it].
"O axe, harm it not," [he says].
The axe indeed is a thunderbolt;
[this is] for non-injury.
The chip that falls away first, that he should bring.
For vigor flies away following this chip of it that flies away;
he erects it possessed of vigor.
That which is bent to the south, he should not cut;
it belongs to the pit.
That which is bent to the north, not that;
it belongs to a pillar.
That which has branches pointing upward, chips pointing upward, that he should cut;
that indeed is fit for a sacrificial post;
he cuts that which is fit for a sacrificial post.
That which has an undried top, many branches, and many leaves, that he should cut;
plants indeed are trees, cattle are plants;
he enriches him with cattle.
That which has a dried top, no branches, and no leaves, that he should cut of him whom he hates;
trees are plants, cattle are plants;
he deprives him of cattle.
That which is bent toward the north-east, that he should cut;
that indeed is inclined toward the sacrificial essence;
he cuts that which is inclined toward the sacrificial essence.
"Graze not the sky, harm not the atmosphere, unite with the earth," [he says].
The sacrificial post indeed is a thunderbolt;
[this is] for the non-injury of these worlds.
He props it up toward the north, toward the east;
that indeed is the world of the gods;
he props it up right toward the world of the gods.
"O tree, grow up with a hundred shoots," [he says, and] offers into the stump;
he places abundance right in the trees;
therefore more grow from this stump.
"May we grow up with a thousand shoots," [he says];
he prays for a blessing.
"Whom this axe, sharpened, has led forward to great fortune," [he says].
For he is led forward to great fortune who is led forward to the sacrifice.
One that is a parasite, that he should cut of him whom he hates;
homeless indeed and seatless is that which is a parasite;
he makes him homeless and seatless.
That which has grown by itself, that he should cut;
that indeed has a home and a seat which has grown by itself;
he makes the sacrificer possessed of a home and possessed of a seat.
He should cut it at ankle-height;
for the ankle is a joint;
[it is then] measured by a joint of the sacrifice.
He should cut it at knee-height;
for the knee is a joint;
[it is then] measured by a joint of the sacrifice.
He should cut it at as much as where an axle would touch;
[it is then] measured by a joint of the sacrifice,
for the non-repulsion of cattle.

It should be cut commensurate with cattle;
[namely] of ten cubits.
Ten indeed are the vital breaths of an animal, the body being the eleventh;
eight are the corners, two the joints, the body being the eleventh;
he cuts it commensurate with cattle.
It should be cut commensurate with the stomas;
[namely] of nine cubits;
it is commensurate with the ninefold [stoma] through splendor.
It should be cut of fifteen cubits;
it is commensurate with the fifteenfold [stoma];
the fifteenfold [stoma] is a thunderbolt;
it is commensurate with the thunderbolt.
It should be cut of seventeen cubits;
it is commensurate with the seventeenfold [stoma];
Prajāpati is seventeenfold;
it is commensurate with Prajāpati.
It should be cut of twenty-one cubits;
it is commensurate with the twenty-onefold [stoma];
yonder sun is twenty-onefold;
it is commensurate with yonder sun.
It should be cut of twenty-seven cubits;
it is commensurate with the twenty-sevenfold [stoma];
these worlds are thrice-nine;
it is commensurate with these worlds.
It should be cut of thirty-three cubits;
it is commensurate with the thirty-threefold [stoma];
thirty-three are the deities;
it is commensurate with the deities.
It should be cut commensurate with the meters.
Of five cubits, it is commensurate with the paṅkti meter.
Of six cubits, it is commensurate with the aticchandas.
Of seven cubits, it is commensurate with the śakvarī.
Of eight cubits, it is commensurate with the gāyatrī.
Of nine cubits, it is commensurate with the bṛhatī.
Of ten cubits, it is commensurate with the virāj.
Of eleven cubits, it is commensurate with the triṣṭubh.
Of twelve cubits, it is commensurate with the jagatī.
Of thirteen cubits, it is commensurate with the Sādhya gods.
Large, unmeasured should it be cut.
From the sacrificial post indeed the gods went to the heavenly world;
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
Whatever [tree] is taller than a man, that he should cut;
this indeed is the measure of the sacrificial post;
as large as the sacrificial post is, that he cuts.
There is a top-ring.
From the top-ring indeed the gods went to the heavenly world;
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
Indra indeed hurled the thunderbolt at Vṛtra.
As much of it as was grasped, that was shattered.
They made that into the top-ring.
In that there is a top-ring,
[it is] for firmness, for non-slackness of the grasp.
It is so much [in size];
for so much of it was grasped.
He should not let it project beyond the sacrificial post.
If he were to let it project, women would have swellings.
He should let it project as much as the topmost joint of a finger;
[it is then] measured by a joint of the sacrifice,
for the non-repulsion of cattle.
What is redundant in the sacrifice becomes redundant for the Sādhya gods;
what projects from the sacrificial post is redundant in the sacrifice;
thereby he gratifies the Sādhya gods.
He should make the top-ring of another tree than that of which the sacrificial post is, for him of whom he should desire: 'May I produce a rival for him.'
Other trees indeed are the rivals of this one;
he causes a rival to mount upon his world;
he deprives him of a world.

"For the earth thee! For the atmosphere thee! For the sky thee!"—with this he sprinkles the sacrificial post.
He sprinkles it for these worlds.
For this world the audumbarī [post] is measured out, for that [world] the sacrificial post;
therefore he sprinkles the sacrificial post pointing away from here, and the audumbarī [pointing] hither from there,
for the keeping apart of these two worlds.
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ, [thee]…"—with this he takes the spade.
Impelled by Savitṛ, he takes it with the deities.
"Here do I cut off the neck of the Rakṣas"—[with this he digs];
the rival is the Rakṣas;
he cuts off the neck of his rival.
"May the worlds, the seats of the fathers, be cleansed!"—[with this he sprinkles the pit];
he cleanses it;
moreover, what cruel [wound] they inflict on it in digging, that he appeases.
He pours in [water] containing barley.
Barley is food; the sacrificial post is splendor;
he places splendor in food.
Barley is food; the sacrificial post is a thunderbolt;
he places the thunderbolt in food.
"I spread thee, a world, the seat of the fathers"—[with this he spreads the barhis in the pit];
if they were to set it up without barhis, it would be a pit-post;
the plants are on the northern half of this [earth];
he sets it up on the northern half of this [earth];
he has made it not a pit-post.
"Thou art of easy approach, going in front of the leaders"—with this he throws down a chip of the sacrificial post.
Rough indeed is it when hewn, unfit for cattle;
he sets it up with its skin.
"May heaven and earth be filled with ghee!"—[with this he besprinkles it];
with ghee he moistens heaven and earth;
therefore plants, [even when] unanointed, shine.
"May god Savitṛ anoint thee with honey!"—[with this he anoints it];
ghee is the honey of the gods;
he makes it fit for offering, sacrificial.
"Make the plants possess good fruit!"—with this he fixes the head-ring;
he causes the plants to bear fruit;
therefore they ripen without being ploughed.
He anoints the agniṣṭhā with ghee;
ghee is splendor, the sacrificer is the agniṣṭhā;
with splendor he makes the sacrificer prosper.
He anoints continuously to the end,
for the continuity of splendor,
for non-interruption.
Against whomever he bears hatred, for him he should interrupt [the anointing];
he deprives him of splendor.
He anoints on all sides;
from all sides he makes him prosper with splendor.
"Thou hast touched the sky with thy tip; thou hast filled the atmosphere with thy middle; thou hast made firm the earth with thy butt"—
the sacrificial post is a thunderbolt;
[this is] for the keeping apart of these worlds.
"Those dwellings of you two…"—with this [verse] addressed to Viṣṇu he brings it down;
the sacrificial post belongs to Viṣṇu;
with its own deity he brings it down.
"Behold the deeds of Viṣṇu…"—with this [verse] addressed to Viṣṇu he arranges it;
the sacrificial post belongs to Viṣṇu;
with its own deity he arranges it.
He should make the agniṣṭhā [face] toward the fire;
fire is splendor, the sacrificer is the agniṣṭhā;
with splendor he makes the sacrificer prosper.
Against whomever he bears hatred, for him he should bend it this way or that way;
he deprives him of splendor.
"Thee, winning the Brahman, winning the Kṣatra…"—
he expresses a blessing;
"Strengthen the Brahman! Strengthen the Kṣatra!"—
he strengthens for him the blessing that was expressed.
"That highest step of Viṣṇu…"—with this [verse] addressed to Viṣṇu he looks up at it;
the sacrificial post belongs to Viṣṇu;
with its own deity he looks up at it.

He should make half of the post-hole on the altar, half outside the altar,
for the obtaining of the measured and the unmeasured.
He should not make the butt visible;
if he were to make the butt visible, it would be a pit-post.
He should not let it go untied around [with the cord];
if he were to let it go untied around, the sacrificer would become hungry, the adhvaryu would become naked.
The cord is vigor;
he ties it around against the middle;
he places vigor in the middle, both in the sacrificer and in offspring;
therefore vigor invigorates offspring from the middle.
He should set it up without a cord for him whom he hates;
the cord is vigor;
he deprives him of vigor.
He should push it upward or downward for him whom he hates;
the cord is vigor;
he deprives him of vigor.
He should tie it around high up, far above, if he should desire, "May it rain!";
it rains from the waters, from the plants, when it rains;
the cord is the plants;
he brings the plants closer to the rain;
it rains quickly.
He should tie it around low down, far below, if he should desire, "May it not rain!";
it rains from the waters, from the plants, when it rains;
the cord is the plants;
he puts the plants further from the rain;
it takes a long time to rain.
"Thou art girded around; may the divine clans gird thee around!"—[with this he ties it];
these divine clans are the plants;
the cord is the plants;
the sacrificial post is the sacrificer;
he turns offspring face to face toward the sacrificer.
"Around this man [pour] wealth!"—
wealth is cattle;
he makes him prosper with cattle.
"Glide over the back of the atmosphere!"—with this he inserts the svaru-chip;
for it has the atmosphere as its deity at this time, being above this world, as it were, and below that one;
with its own deity he inserts it.
The butt belongs to the fathers;
what is above the butt belongs to men;
the region of the cord belongs to the plants;
what is above the cord belongs to the All-gods;
the head-ring belongs to Indra;
what is leftover belongs to the Sādhyas;
in this very manner are the fathers, men, and gods in these worlds;
the sacrificial post belongs to all deities;
with all deities he makes it prosper.
He should make [the post] bent inward from the side of the fire, bent outward on the outer side;
by what is bent inward from the side of the fire he makes room for the sacrificer; by what is bent outward on the outer side, he thrusts away the rival from the sacrifice.
By means of the sacrifice the gods went to the heavenly world;
they desired, "May no other know this world of ours!";
they obstructed the quarters;
because they obstructed (ayopayan) the quarters, that is why the post is called post (yūpa);
having gone to that world, they thirsted;
they knew, "From offerings given from there we have come here";
they gave this [post] back;
thereby they prospered.
This indeed is the warp of the sacrifice;
this is the highest footstep;
just as a web is stretched from a peg, so from here the sacrifice is stretched.
Formerly, pulling it down, they used to offer it [the post itself];
in that he offers the svaru-chip,
[it is] for the ransom of this very [post];
ransomed for him, pleased, rendered auspicious, it attends upon these worlds.
"May thy smoke go to the sky, thy flame to the atmosphere, thy ash to the earth, svāhā!"—
he makes it go to these very worlds.

He recites [a verse] addressed to Savitṛ;
impelled by Savitṛ, he begets it.
He recites [a verse] having one deity;
in a single way he begets it.
If he were to recite [a verse] having two deities, he would generate for him a rival as a second.
He recites [a verse] addressed to Heaven and Earth,
for the comprehension of born offspring.
He should not recite [a verse] containing [the word] "smoke";
for when Indra slew Vṛtra, wherever he had failed, smoke arose from there;
he who is born from failure—
he becomes destructive to his failure.
If [the fire] being churned should not be born, he should recite [a verse] slaying the Rakṣases,
for the warding off of the Rakṣases.
He should recite without breathing out;
for the Rakṣases seek to destroy the sacrifice along the breath;
he makes an obstacle for the Rakṣases.
He should recite a gāyatrī for a brāhmaṇa;
for the brāhmaṇa belongs to the gāyatrī;
a triṣṭubh for a rājanya;
for the rājanya belongs to the triṣṭubh;
a jagatī for a vaiśya;
for the vaiśya belongs to the jagatī;
according to the respective meters.
"At the impulse of god Savitṛ [thee]…"—with this he takes the cord;
impelled by Savitṛ, he takes it with the deities.
"With the noose of the cosmic order, O divine offering, I fasten thee"—
that which is sacrificial [and] fit for sacrifice is the noose of cosmic order;
with that he seizes it.
"Welcome to such-and-such [deity]"—
to whichever deity he seizes [it], for that very [deity] he makes it welcome.
"Overpower the human!"—
the sacrificial post is the thunderbolt, cattle are human;
with the thunderbolt he restrains cattle for him.
Bare and hewn, this [post] is unfit for sacrifice;
when he binds the animal [to it],
he makes it fit for sacrifice, sacrificial.
From the front, cattle approach the sacrificial essence;
deities in this [sacrifice] are the vital breaths;
when they propitiate [it] pointing west from the front,
they propitiate half of the deities.
"From the waters, from the plants, [thee]…"—
from the waters, from the plants, is this animal seized;
from the waters and from the plants he sprinkles it.
"May thy mother consent, may thy father [consent]…"—
for his father and mother are the consenters.
The waters are all deities;
the animal belongs to all deities;
with all the deities he enriches it.
To much that is unfit for sacrifice, unsuited for sacrifice, does this animal go;
the waters are fit for sacrifice, sacrificial;
when he sprinkles with water,
he makes it fit for sacrifice, sacrificial.
"Thou art the drink of the waters"—with this he takes up water;
those portions that are within, those he makes fit for sacrifice.
"Pleasing offering to the gods, full of ghee"—with this he sprinkles down upon the chest;
those portions that are underneath, those he makes fit for sacrifice.
He sprinkles on all sides;
on all sides he enriches it with sacrificial essence.
The head of the sacrifice is the butter-libation; the animal is the self;
when, having poured the butter-libation, he anoints the animal,
he places the head of the sacrifice onto the self.
"May Vāyu unite with thy breath"—
he makes Vāyu unite with his breath.
"Thy limbs with those worthy of sacrifice"—
those worthy of sacrifice are the deities;
he makes his limbs unite with the deities.
"The lord of the sacrifice with the benediction"—
with the benediction he enriches the sacrificer.

He chooses the priests;
the meters are the priests;
he chooses the meters.
When chosen [and] joined by him, the vaṣaṭ-calls are harnessed before them.
He chooses the hotṛ;
thereby he chooses the jagatī among the meters.
He chooses the āgnīdhra;
thereby he chooses the paṅkti among the meters.
He chooses the two adhvaryus;
thereby he chooses the aticchandas among the meters.
Between the hotṛ and the two adhvaryus he chooses the āgnīdhra;
therefore Agni has entered into the midst of the plants.
Since he sacrifices last,
therefore, being churned from above, he is born.
He chooses the maitrāvaruṇa;
thereby he chooses the gāyatrī among the meters.
He chooses the brāhmaṇācchaṃsin;
thereby he chooses the triṣṭubh among the meters.
He chooses the potṛ;
thereby he chooses the uṣṇih among the meters.
He chooses the neṣṭṛ;
thereby he chooses the kakubh among the meters.
Born behind, as it were, is this meter, the anuṣṭubh;
born behind is this office of the hotṛ, namely that of the acchāvāka;
therefore they only summon the acchāvāka, they do not choose him.
Now these Āprī [verses]:
Prajāpati, having created offspring, thought himself emptied out;
he saw these Āprī [verses];
with them he propitiated himself.
The sacrifice is Prajāpati; the sacrificer is the self of the sacrifice.
Since these are performed in the first half of the sacrifice,
he propitiates the sacrificer right from the beginning.
He sacrifices the fore-offerings;
the fore-offerings are the vital breaths;
he fore-sacrifices the vital breaths;
that is the fore-offeringness of the fore-offerings.
He should propitiate without breathing out for him whom he wishes: "May he go to full life";
the fore-offerings are the vital breaths;
he establishes unbroken vital breaths in him;
he goes to full life.
The omentum of the animal is the self; the Āprī [verses] of the sacrificer are the self;
since the last Āprī verse encompasses the omentum,
the self encompasses the self,
for the continuity, for the non-interruption of the sacrifice.
There are eleven fore-offerings, eleven after-offerings, eleven by-offerings;
so many are the deities;
the animal belongs to all deities;
with all the deities he enriches it.
Thirty-three are the deities that drink Soma, thirty-three that do not drink Soma;
the eight Vasus, the eleven Rudras, the twelve Ādityas, the vaṣaṭ-call, and Prajāpati—these are the Soma-drinkers;
the fore-offerings, the after-offerings, [and] the by-offerings—these are the non-Soma-drinkers;
therefore they are performed outside the sacrifice.
With Soma he pleases the gods who drink Soma;
with the animal, those who do not drink Soma;
both of these become pleased with him at the sacrifice.
This indeed Śyāparṇa Sāyakāyana said to Aṣāḍha Kaiśina when he led him away from the Soma:
"If thou shalt lead me away from the Soma, thou shalt lead me away together with both gods, both the Soma-drinkers and the non-Soma-drinkers;
deprived of gods thy sacrifice will be abandoned;
and in whatever direction they shall lead me, from there [enemies] attacking will conquer thee."
Having deliberated: "From here there is no fear for us," they led [him] toward the south and west;
thence the Kuntis, attacking, conquered the Pañcālas.
He who leads one who knows thus away from the Soma, leads him away together with both gods, the Soma-drinkers and the non-Soma-drinkers;
deprived of gods his sacrifice is abandoned;
and in whatever direction they lead him, from there [enemies] attacking conquer him.

The gods and the Asuras did equally at the sacrifice;
whatever the gods did, that the Asuras did.
Then the gods saw this Upāṃśu [cup];
they saw the sacrifice as concluding in this [cup];
in it alone they brought the sacrifice to conclusion.
Eight times they pressed;
thereby they obtained the gāyatrī meter.
Eleven times [they pressed];
thereby [they obtained] the triṣṭubh.
Twelve times [they pressed];
thereby [they obtained] the jagatī.
Three times they drew [it];
thereby they obtained the Soma-pressings.
Those gods, having enclosed as much as the sacrifice was, stepped up to offer.
Those Asuras saw: "The gods are offering."
Having raised the vajra, they rushed at them.
The gods shut them off from these worlds with the Antaryāma [cup].
Then the gods flourished, the Asuras perished.
He for whom they offer the Upāṃśu knowing thus, and who knowing thus offers, shuts off his rival from these worlds; he himself flourishes, his rival perishes.
Now indeed Āruṇa Aupaveśi used to say:
"Why should he sacrifice who does not know that the sacrifice is to be concluded in the Upāṃśu?"
Eight times he presses;
thereby he obtains the gāyatrī meter.
Eleven times [he presses];
thereby [he obtains] the triṣṭubh.
Twelve times [he presses];
thereby [he obtains] the jagatī.
Three times he draws [it];
thereby he obtains the Soma-pressings.
Having concluded the sacrifice in the Upāṃśu, he proceeds with that which is concluded, unharmed, uninjured.
He should press eight [and] eight [times] for one desiring spiritual luster;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
he disposes the gāyatrī at the opening of the sacrifice;
in the gāyatrī, in fiery energy, in his own womb, in the heavenly world, the opening of the sacrifice is extended.
He who knows thus becomes eminent.
He should not say: "Thou art taken with a support (upayāma)."
The earth indeed is the support;
by it he would block the breaths.
They do not stretch out a strainer;
the atmosphere indeed is the strainer;
if he were to enclose the breaths with the atmosphere, he would perish.
This [wind] which purifies, he indeed is breath;
inhaling this [breath], we breathe.
Since he does not say: "Thou art taken with a support", nor do they stretch out a strainer,
therefore this [wind], unenclosed, blows at will this way and that way;
therefore we breathe at will.
The Upāṃśu is breath-purifying;
with the breaths alone he purifies it.
He takes six shoots;
therefore there are six breaths; he purifies them in pairs;
therefore the breaths are in pairs.
He holds down the Upāṃśu [shoot] with the Upāṃśusavana stone;
the Upāṃśusavana stone is breath;
the Upāṃśu is breath;
from breath alone he holds down breath.
The Upāṃśusavana stone is above, the shoots below;
the Upāṃśusavana stone is breath, the shoots are offspring;
in offspring alone he places breath;
therefore breath is supreme.

"Purify thyself for the lord of speech!"—
"Purify thyself for lordship over speech!" is indeed what he says by this.
Therefore by this [speech] we speak at will and we breathe forth.
Prajāpati indeed was this [universe];
speech was his second.
He entered into pairing with her;
she conceived a child;
she stepped away from him;
she created these offspring;
she entered back into Prajāpati alone.
"Purify thyself for the lord of speech!"—
from this very pair he is propagated with offspring and with cattle.
"Purify thyself for the lord of speech!"—
sacred prayer is indeed the lord of speech;
by sacred prayer alone he purifies it.
"By the shoots of the bull, purified by the hands!"—
Indra is indeed the bull; his hands are the rays [or hands];
by them alone he purifies it.
"Thou art the divine strainer of the gods!"—
to the deities alone he assigns it.
"Our honeyed refreshments!"—
for with Soma they purify Soma.
"Whose portion thou art!"—
to the deities alone he assigns it.
"Make our refreshments honeyed!"—
the refreshments are indeed the deities;
for the deities alone he makes it honeyed.
"Thou art self-prepared!"—
he makes breath his own.
"Go through the wide intermediate space!"—
for it has the intermediate space as its deity at that time.
"For all the senses!"—
he places breath in both the gods and men.
"Mind hath prepared thee!"—
for breath is held by mind.
"Hail to thee, the well-formed one, for the sun!"—
Soma is indeed the well-formed one;
in yonder sun are the dear bodies of the gods;
those alone he gratifies.
Moreover, by the utterance of "svāhā" alone he prepares the offerings in it.
He offers it as completely offered;
therefore breath goes outward.
He offers standing upright;
the sacrifice is co-measured with man;
man is continuous through in-breath and out-breath;
for the non-shattering of in-breath and out-breath.
The Adhvaryu does not stumble who knows thus.
He should offer the Upāṃśu from the north, the Antaryāma from the south.
A chariot of the gods indeed is this sacrifice;
its two reins are these: the Upāṃśu and the Antaryāma.
He arranges the reins in front of the divine chariot
for the arrangement of the year, for the discernment of the heavenly world.
In imitation of that, the two reins of a human chariot are arranged.
They are to be offered straight,
for the non-intertwining of the breaths.
He should wipe upwards if he should desire, "May it not rain!";
yonder alone he holds back the rain.
He should wipe downwards if he should desire, "May it rain!";
the offering that rises up from here causes rain to fall from yonder;
with the Soma-offering alone he leads down rain from heaven.
"To the ray-drinking gods thee!"—so he wipes [it] on the enclosing stick;
the ray-drinking gods are indeed the breaths;
those alone he gratifies.
Moreover, this is the Soma-drink of the enclosing sticks.
He wipes on the inside;
therefore the breaths are inside.
He cuts off the sacrifice when he brings it to completion before the pressing.
When he unites the unpressed shoots,
[it is] for the continuity of the sacrifice, for non-interruption.
He unites six shoots;
therefore there are six breaths.
In pairs [he unites them];
therefore the breaths are in pairs.
Being moist, they do not cling together;
therefore the breaths, being moist, do not cling together.
Whoever indeed knows the Soma-drink of Soma is not cut off from the Soma-drink, nor his offspring.
"O Soma, what inviolable, wakeful name is thine, to that Soma, to thee, hail!"—
this indeed is the Soma-drink of Soma;
he thereby gratifies Soma with Soma;
he who knows thus is not cut off from the Soma-drink, nor his offspring.
If he should practice sorcery, he should take [it] with: "On the impulse of the divine Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan I take thee!";
"In the exhilaration of the exhilarating, intoxicating [drink], may it be false, then I will offer thee!"—
he makes it false indeed.
Soma belongs to all deities;
all the deities at that time hope in this one: "He will offer to me, he will offer to me!";
the deities, desiring the oblation, burn him up.
He stands until he is breathless;
he reaches the very end of his breath.
When he speaks the yajus,
he cuts him off from the gods.
He practices sorcery with the Upāṃśu;
the Upāṃśu is breath;
with breath alone he practices sorcery against his breath;
quickly he perishes.

The Dhruva [cup] is life.
It is drawn last;
therefore life is the last among the vital breaths.
It is drawn ninth;
for there are nine vital breaths.
He sets it down solitary in the northern Havirdhāna [cart];
therefore the downward breath, alone among the vital breaths, has half a share.
After the Dhruva, indeed, human beings are propagated; after the other cups, the other creatures.
He sets down the Dhruva on [an un-earthed place] where earth is not strewn; the other cups on where earth is strewn;
therefore man stands firm with flesh, animals with bone.
A prince guards [it];
a noble is indeed the lord of creatures; the Dhruva is life;
he guards life itself for the creatures;
and also he guards royal power.
If he should desire: 'May they expel him who is settled, may he who is expelled become settled,' [saying:] 'Thee, the un-firm, possessing an un-firm dwelling, un-firmest of the un-firm, most immovable of the immovable, I push out from such-and-such a folk,' he should push out the Dhruva.
Firm indeed is he who is settled; un-firm is he who is expelled.
He expels him who is settled; he causes him who is expelled to become settled; quickly they change places.
If he were to do so, he would dislodge the life of creatures.
He should pass grass over and above with this mantra;
thereby he causes the expelled to become settled, [and] does not dislodge the life of creatures.
The altar is indeed the realm of the gods;
as great as is the altar, so great is the earth;
by means of the realm of the gods he drives him away from the realm of men.
He should draw [it] full for him of whom he should desire: 'May he go to the full span of life.'
He draws full life for him;
he goes to the full span of life.
He should draw a little for him of whom he should desire: 'May he go to a short span of life.'
He makes his life short; quickly he perishes.
He should draw above the half for him of whom he should desire: 'May he go to higher life.'
He brings him to higher life.
According to the characteristic form he grasps life for him.
He draws [it] enclosed on both sides with Vaiśvānara [verses];
therefore the vital breaths are enclosed on both sides with Vaiśvānara.
[With the verse:] 'Head of heaven, the messenger of the earth,'
he places life both in the gods and in men.
The sacrifice is commensurate with man; man is continuous with life.
When this [cup], drawn in the morning, lies until the third pressing,
by means of life he makes the sacrifice continuous.
In the morning indeed these two are drawn, in the world of the Gāyatrī: the Āgrāyaṇa and the Dhruva.
They two lie until the third pressing from the world of the Jagatī.
Therefore the Gāyatrī, not having lost its potency, returns at the later pressing;
that is its allotted portion.
The Gāyatrī is breath; the Dhruva is life.
Because this, placed in the morning, lies until the third pressing,
therefore as great as is life, so great is breath.
The Dhruva is life, Agni is Rudra;
it should not be offered alone;
he would burn the life of creatures; they would be liable to die.
He pours [it] into the Hotṛ's cup.
The Hotṛ belongs to All the Gods; these creatures belong to All the Gods;
he places life in the creatures themselves.
Indeed, he makes it un-firm when he pours [it] out;
consequent upon its un-firmness, things do not succeed for the creatures.
[Saying:] 'May the divine clans be strengthened, may men prosper, may the heavenly things be in heaven, the birds in the intermediate space, the earthly things on earth,' he pours out the Dhruva.
According to their proper place he strengthens the creatures.
When summoned, he should pour it out at the Uktha;
by means of what has been chanted he continues the recitation.
If he should pour it out when summoned, he would make the Uktha redundant;
if when recited, he would make the cup redundant.
At the middle concluding verse, at that he pours it out.
These are indeed the limbs of the sacrifice, namely the Ukthamadas; the Dhruva is life.
One half of a man's self is the limbs, one half is life;
having joined the ends on both sides, he places life in the middle.
'From what reality,' the knowers of sacred speech ask, 'do the rushing waters not dwindle, do not dry up?'
Awaiting this very Soma-drink, which is the Dhruva;
for this pushes them away.
Sitting facing west, he pours [it] out;
along the current indeed he lets them flow.
He should not urinate halfway through;
he should sit through the Agniṣṭoma.
The Dhruva is life;
he would cause life to flow away.
As great indeed is the Agniṣṭoma, so great is the sacrifice.
When he sits through the Agniṣṭoma,
he goes within his breath;
he goes to the full span of life.
The Sadas is the belly of this sacrifice;
thither they run, slipping out.
The sacrifice is Prajāpati;
when he sits through the Agniṣṭoma,
he does not empty Prajāpati's belly; he does not become hungry.

The gods, having slain the Asuras and fleeing from blood-money, confused the seasons, the year.
The gods sought its ordering.
They ordered it by means of the seasonal cups.
That the seasonal cups are drawn
is for the ordering of the year,
for the discernment of the heavenly world.
Both draw together first;
for both of these proceed together to order the year.
Both [draw] together last;
having ordered the year, both are unyoked together.
The two season-vessels have mouths on both sides;
for the seasons have mouths on both sides;
for we do not know from which side is the mouth of the seasons.
They should not cross each other.
If they should cross each other, season would become aggressive towards season.
Because they pass each other,
therefore they say: "Like two horses, the seasons have sniffed each other."
Twelve of these are drawn;
twelve months are a year,
for the ordering of the year.
The year is the heavenly world;
these are its steps, its crossings,
that the seasonal cups [are].
He ascends an ascent by means of these,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
Six are of one kind, six of the other.
That sun is the season;
therefore it goes north for six months, south for six.
Six with "Through the season";
six indeed are the seasons;
he firmly establishes himself in the seasons.
Four with "Through the seasons";
four-footed are cattle;
cattle are propagated along the year;
from the year he propagates cattle for him.
Twice again with "Through the season";
two-footed is the sacrificer;
he firmly establishes himself above the cattle.
These are the Soma-drinkers, Indra's kin among the Soma-drinkers,
that the seasonal cups [are].
For he says: "Let him drink Soma through the season,"
not "The season [shall drink]";
they do not offer to the season.
These are their own two vessels, by which they offer to them;
therefore a kinsman brings tribute in his own vessel;
they bring tribute to him who knows thus.
They drink alternating;
for freshness.
Therefore it rains season by season.
Having ordered the year by this, having discerned the heavenly world, the gods placed this light of the sacrifice above.
That the Aindrāgna [cup] is drawn,
he places light at the opening of the sacrifice,
for the ordering of the year,
for the discernment of the heavenly world.
Therefore the light traverses above and over the year.
He draws the Aindrāgna [cup] with the season-vessel;
Indra-Agni are the nobility, the seasons are the peasantry;
he inserts the nobility into the peasantry.
The All-Gods, having their share in the third pressing, desired the morning pressing.
Prajāpati saw that they were to be gratified in this cup.
That the Vaiśvadeva [cup] is drawn at the morning pressing,
he gratifies the All-Gods at the morning pressing.
Gratified, pleased, they release the two later pressings for him.
He draws the Vaiśvadeva [cup] with the Śukra-vessel.
That sun is the Śukra, these creatures belong to the All-Gods;
therefore it faces all creatures towards the west;
for they think: "Towards me, towards me!"

At the midday pressing, Indra slew Vṛtra with the Maruts by virility.
The chant is related to the Maruts, the recitation is related to the Maruts, the cups are related to the Maruts;
he enriches the sacrificer with virility from every side;
he slays Vṛtra from every side.
The first Marutvatīya [cup] is the aiming [of the weapon];
the second is the drawing back;
with the third he releases the discharge.
With the first Marutvatīya [cup] he raises the thunderbolt;
with the second he hurls it;
with the third he lays [the foe] low.
For whomever he might hate, he should draw [the cups] not dedicated to the Maruts for him;
the Maruts are virility;
with virility he causes him to fail.
Indra, about to slay Vṛtra, gave a gift to the gods, the sacrificial fees;
with the Marutvatīya [cups] he slew Vṛtra.
Therefore one should not give when [the hymn] to Marutvat has been recited;
for Vṛtra has then been slain;
a victory has been won.
Nor should he accept;
he displaces established cattle, he becomes cattleless.
Indra, having slain Vṛtra, became Mahendra (the Great Indra);
he looked down on the other gods;
he took out this cup as his choice share, the Mahendrīya [cup].
Therefore a king, having won a battle, drives off his choice share.
The gods said to him: "You have said everything; let there be a share for us here too."
[He said:] "How should there be a share for you?"
"Let him sacrifice with the Virāj for this cup."
The Virāj is the gods' vessel;
that he sacrifices with the Virāj,
he gratifies the deities with the gods' vessel.
Therefore when a king wins a battle, [a share is given] there even to the charioteers and village heads.
The Trivṛt [stoma] has its domain through the Gāyatrī, the Pañcadaśa through the Triṣṭubh, the Ekavim̐śa through the Jagatī.
Now this Virāj
is the very domain where the Saptadaśa [is].
That he sacrifices with the Virāj,
he makes the Saptadaśa possess a domain.
For whomever he might hate, he should sacrifice with the Triṣṭubh for him;
the Triṣṭubh is force, virility;
by force and virility he cuts him off from his domain;
he, deprived of domain, perishes.
The gods, having slain the Asuras and fleeing from blood-money, entered the sacrifice from the middle.
They entered this verse, the Virāj.
That he sacrifices with the Virāj
is for the propitiation of the deities.
The Ādityas, going to the heavenly world, pushed the Aṅgirases back into this world together with the sacrifice.
Those Aṅgirases said to the Ādityas: "For how many of you shall we convey the oblation?"
"For thirty-three," they said:
"ten Vasus, Indra the eleventh;
ten Rudras, Indra the eleventh;
ten Ādityas, Indra the eleventh;
these are thirty deities, thirty;
the vaṣaṭ-call is of two syllables;
speech is of one syllable;"
thereby it belongs to Indra.
His deities are accommodated in the oblation;
his oblation goes according to the deity.
They choose him for the office of priest who knows thus;
for as many as the deities are, so many he causes the oblation to reach.

From the midday pressing, indeed, the gods went to the heavenly world.
The sacrificial gifts are their steps and bridge-crossings.
When he gives sacrificial gifts,
having constructed a bridge, he goes to the heavenly world.
As many living creatures as he gives here, so many are the vital breaths in yonder world;
therefore much is to be given.
Therefore here they praise what is given generously;
he constructs a greater bridge.
At this time indeed the deities bend down closest [to the earth];
therefore at this time he [the sun] burns most intensely, since he climbs to the heavenly world from the closest distance.
He offers with two verses to Sūrya,
for the attainment of the heavenly world,
with two [verses];
the sacrificer is two-footed;
he makes him step upon this [earth].
When he offers in the Āgnīdhra [fire] with a verse to Agni,
he makes him step in the intermediate space.
When he gives,
he makes him reach the heavenly world.
‘Go to the sky, go to the light,’ [he says],
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
‘I have approached your form with form, [your] age with age,’ [he says];
this indeed is the form of cattle, namely ghee, namely gold;
with form indeed he approaches their form, with age, age.
‘May Tutha Viśvavedas distribute you,’ [he says];
Tutha Viśvavedas is indeed truth;
with truth, with the brahman, he distributes them.
‘This, O Agni, is your gift that comes, brought by Soma,’ [he says];
cattle are connected with Agni;
they are given in the Soma [sacrifice];
‘Lead it by the path of Mitra,’ [he says];
by the path of Mitra indeed he leads them.
He leads [them] between the Āgnīdhra [shed] and the Sadas [shed];
by this path indeed the gods went to the heavenly world;
he causes them to reach that very [path].
From the south, indeed, the Rakṣases conquered the sacrifice of the gods;
they drove them away again from the Āgnīdhra [fire].
When they lead the sacrificial gifts south from the Āgnīdhra,
[it is] for the winning of the sacrifice,
for the smiting away of the Rakṣases.
He offers in the Āgnīdhra [fire] with a verse to Agni;
cattle are connected with Agni,
for the redemption of the cattle.
He should offer with a second verse to Varuṇa if he is about to give a horse, or a cart, or a chariot;
for horses belong to Varuṇa, trees belong to Varuṇa;
Agni is all the deities,
for the propitiation of the deities.
He gives a cushion;
this is indeed the form of the metres;
by means of the form indeed he secures the metres.
He gives gold;
endowed with golden radiance, he makes them go to the heavenly world.
‘Given by us, full of sweetness, go to the gods,’ [he says];
he makes them go to the heavenly world.
‘Enter into the giver,’ [he says];
therefore in yonder world they are prone to return [to him].
‘Go forth by the path of ṛta, O sacrificial gifts of silver!’ [he says];
that which is worthy of sacrifice, fit for offering, that is the path of ṛta;
by that indeed he leads them.
‘May I obtain a brāhmaṇa, prosperous, having [distinguished] fathers, having a patrilineage,’ [he says];
he who is learned in the Veda and of ṛṣi descent, he has [distinguished] fathers and a patrilineage;
‘May I find him,’ thus in truth he says this;
for he is one to whom sacrificial gifts are well placed.
That [gift] which he gives to a non-brāhmaṇa spreads out as trees;
that which he gives to the Kaṇvas and Kaśyapas spreads out as fog;
that which he gives to a brāhmaṇa who is not learned in the Veda spreads out as mirages;
that which he gives to one learned in the Veda and of ṛṣi descent enriches him in yonder world, and in this world it serves for his enjoyment again;
therefore he alone is to be sought.
‘Look abroad at the light, look abroad at the intermediate space,’ [he says, and] looks at the Sadas.
Soma-drinkers indeed are these brāhmaṇas;
their eyes are full of fiery energy.
They are hostile to the giver;
they are capable of repelling him;
he pays homage to them,
to prevent repelling.
He gives the gold of Atri.
Svarbhānu the Asura pierced the sun with darkness;
Atri was the first to find him.
That he gives the gold of Atri
is for the attainment of the heavenly world.
If he should not find a descendant of Atri, he should give it to one who is of ṛṣi descent and united [in study];
thereby indeed he prospers.
The Asuras indeed led sacrificial gifts to the gods;
they repelled them;
they said, ‘This one has not been affectionate.’
They led another to them;
they repelled it;
they said, ‘This one has gone to manly valor.’
They led another to them;
they repelled it;
they said, ‘This one has run away.’
Concerning these, indeed, the ṛṣi recited:
‘The black drop stood down upon the Aṃśumatī, speeding with ten thousands; / Indra aided him who was blowing with might; he held back the slaughtering, insolent bands.’
She, having become a she-hyena, entered the Asuras;
she burned them out.
Therefore a sacrificial gift that has been repelled must not be accepted;
he must not let it mingle among the cattle.
Having become a she-hyena, she enters him;
she burns him out.

The Ādityas, having slain the Asuras and fleeing from blood-money, entered the gods.
They entered the [gods] with two deities.
For these are the most united among the gods;
therefore the Āditya [cup] is drawn from the [cups] with two deities.
The Ādityas indeed were born from the remnant [of the oblation];
therefore this remnant Soma is drawn.
The Ādityas indeed have the remnant as their share;
therefore this remnant Soma is drawn.
He draws [it] behind an enclosure.
Agni indeed is Rudra; the Āditya [cup] is livestock,
for the protection of livestock.
The gods indeed excluded Rudra from the sacrifice.
They assigned together: “This is your share in the mother”; bringing it thus far to the end of the Āditya [cup], in connection with this they shut [him] out from the sacrifice.
When he draws the Āditya [cup] behind an enclosure,
he shuts Rudra out from the sacrifice.
Livestock indeed are the Ādityas.
Since this Soma is drawn abundantly,
therefore livestock, being eaten, are not exhausted.
When there are many inside the altar, then he should draw [it];
he secures them as proper food.
When his rival is inside the altar, then he should draw [it];
this indeed is the sacrificer's own world, namely the altar;
livestock are the Ādityas;
in his own world he strips off the livestock of the rival.
The introductory verses are Bṛhatī verses;
livestock indeed are the Bṛhatīs;
livestock is the Āditya [cup];
he places livestock in livestock.
He draws Soma first, then curds, then Soma.
Curds indeed are nourishment, the Āditya [cup] is livestock;
he places nourishment right in the middle among livestock.
It has boiled milk as coagulant;
therefore the raw [cow] yields the cooked [milk].
If he should desire: “May it rain,” he should mix [it] from above with curds, saying: “With that heavenly rain I mix you.”
Curds indeed are livestock, the Āditya [cup] is livestock;
it rains for livestock indeed whenever it rains;
he brings down rain for livestock.
He stirs it with the Upāṃśusavana stone;
breath indeed is the Upāṃśusavana stone, the Āditya [cup] is livestock;
he places breath in livestock.
“O Vivasvat, O Āditya, this is your Soma-drink!”
That Upāṃśusavana stone is indeed he;
he enriches him directly with the Soma-drink.
If a drop falls down from what is lifted up, it rains immediately.
Having drawn it, he covers it,
for the holding of embryos, against falling down.
He moves it forward at the tenth chant;
therefore embryos, being deposited, are born in the tenth month.
Looking elsewhere, he offers;
Agni indeed is Rudra, the Āditya [cup] is livestock,
for the protection of livestock.
If he were to look, a supervising brahmin might seize the livestock;
he becomes a non-superviser of the livestock being given away.

With the Antaryāma vessel he draws the Sāvitra [cup].
Livestock indeed is the Antaryāma [cup];
impelled by Savitṛ, livestock are born;
impelled by Savitṛ he generates them.
He offers it unset;
therefore livestock are born in the field unset.
The gods indeed could not lift the third pressing.
At that time Savitṛ was at the morning pressing.
The gods said to him: “Let us lift this with you.”
“No,” he said, “I am the chief; I will not retreat backward.”
“Be the chief here!” they said, “Be the chief here, and then let us lift [it] with you.”
They lifted it with Savitṛ.
That the Sāvitra [cup] is drawn
is for the lifting of the third pressing.
With “Unto thee, divine Savitṛ,” he is chief at the morning pressing.
Savitṛ indeed encompassed that sacrifice all around;
he who knows thus encompasses the rival all around.
With the Antaryāma vessel he draws the Sāvitra [cup] from the Āgrāyaṇa [cup] at the third pressing.
In the morning indeed this is drawn in the domain of the Gāyatrī, namely the Āgrāyaṇa;
he distributes it at the third pressing in the domain of the Jagatī;
therefore the Sāvitra [cup] is drawn from the Āgrāyaṇa;
for Savitṛ among the gods belongs to the Gāyatrī.
Upon the residue of the Sāvitra [cup] he draws supplementary [Soma] for the All-gods.
This is indeed the same cup,
for continuity.
These offspring belong to the All-gods;
impelled by Savitṛ, offspring are born;
impelled by Savitṛ he generates them.
“Thou art good refuge, having a good resting place,” [he says];
for he draws supplementary Soma upon Soma.
And he makes the upper a good refuge for the lower, and the lower a good resting place for the upper.
“Homage to the Bṛhadukṣa,” [he says];
for Bṛhat is of the gods, homage of the fathers.
The fathers indeed have recitation as their share, not chant as their share;
wherever he recites the recitation for them, there he draws the cup for them.

Indra indeed slew Vṛtra.
He broke open his head;
that became the droṇakalaśa trough.
The Soma that flowed together from that, that became the Hāriyojana [cup].
Indra indeed, having slain Vṛtra, poured together the Soma from his lung and heart;
that became the Hāriyojana [cup].
He thought: “If I offer this unboiled and without a second, the Asuras will arise after [me]; if I do not offer it, they will arise after [me].”
The Soma was finished; the enclosing-sticks had been thrown [into the fire].
Then he arose to offer.
Agni said to him: “You shall not offer this unboiled and without a second in me.”
Into it he threw parched grains;
he offered it boiled and having a second.
That he throws in parched grains,
is just for becoming boiled, for having a second.
Those cattle which were in Vṛtra, these are they;
he thus secures livestock that yield desires;
for he fulfills every desire of these.
The Ṛgveda and Sāmaveda are indeed the two bay steeds of Indra;
this is the portion of the two of them, namely the Hāriyojana [cup].
The enclosing-sticks are the stalls;
that he offers the Hāriyojana [cup] when the enclosing-sticks have been thrown [into the fire],
he covers the fodder for the two without a stall.
Prajāpati indeed is the Āgrāyaṇa [cup];
from the Āgrāyaṇa this Soma is left over.
Whatever is left over of the sacrifice, that is left over for Prajāpati.
That he offers this surplus Soma,
therefore this Prajāpati is surplus;
he purifies towards creatures.
With the droṇakalaśa trough the Unnetṛ offers the Hāriyojana [cup].
Surplus indeed is this among vessels, namely the droṇakalaśa trough;
surplus is this [priest] among priests, namely the Unnetṛ;
surplus is this [Soma] among Somas, namely the Hāriyojana [cup];
by means of the surplus he obtains the surplus.
They ask for an invitation from the Unnetṛ,
for the securing of whatever Soma-drink is there.
Those cattle which were in Vṛtra, these are they.
If he were to crush [the parched grains], destructive livestock, few in number, would attend upon him, consuming [him],
for he hurts [them].
If he were not to crush [them], non-destructive livestock, many in number, would attend upon him without consuming [him],
for he does not hurt [them].
He should squeeze [them] as it were;
he does both;
non-destructive livestock, many in number, attend upon him, consuming [for him].
He should suck out as it were,
for the securing of whatever Soma-drink is there.
He scatters [them] on the high altar;
the high altar is livestock; the Hāriyojana grains are livestock;
he establishes livestock in livestock.

The one for Agni, the one for Sarasvatī, the one for Soma, the one for Bṛhaspati: one should seize these together if desiring spiritual lustre, desiring offspring, desiring cattle, desiring the office of purohita, desiring the office of purohita.
In that there is one for Agni,
for the brāhmaṇa belongs to Agni.
In that there is one for Sarasvatī,
for Sarasvatī is speech,
for he speaks speech.
In that there is one for Soma,
for he drinks Soma.
In that there is one for Bṛhaspati,
for Bṛhaspati is the brāhmaṇa;
having made the brāhmaṇa four-footed, he establishes it;
he becomes endowed with spiritual lustre.
Then the one for Pūṣan:
Pūṣan is cattle;
he yokes cattle after the brāhmaṇa.
Then the one for Indra and Agni,
then the one for Indra:
Indra and Agni are royal power, Indra is royal power;
having brought royal power to completion, he establishes it;
and with royal power he pushes cattle toward the brāhmaṇa.
The one for Agni is in the middle, those for Indra on both sides.
The brāhmaṇa belongs to Agni, the rājanya to Indra;
with the brāhmaṇa, royal power glides apart in the middle.
He attains the office of purohita who knows thus.
He makes Indra and Agni the greatest share-holders of the cattle.
Agni's is that for Agni, Indra's is that for Indra, that for Indra and Agni is for both together;
therefore the brāhmaṇa and the rājanya are the greatest share-holders among creatures.
Then the one for the Viśve Devas,
then the one for the Maruts:
the Viśve Devas are the commonalty, the Maruts are the commonalty;
having brought the commonalty to completion, he yokes it after royal power;
having made royal power head the brāhmaṇa, he yokes the commonalty after it.
The one for Savitṛ is for impulsion;
the one for Varuṇa is for freedom from Varuṇa.
Properly he unites the brāhmaṇa, properly royal power, properly the commonalty.
Where the properly arranged set of eleven is seized, there it is in order for creatures:
the brāhmaṇa becomes a brāhmaṇa, the rājanya a rājanya, the vaiśya a vaiśya.
Following this disordered [arrangement] of the set of eleven, indeed, it is not in order for creatures.
This indeed is the set of eleven;
this the Kāpeyas know;
this he should seize as the final one of the Atirātra.
This other is sacrificial-session-like, which creatures follow falling into trouble.
This is the division of days;
these days have these deities.
Thus ends the twenty-ninth sthānaka, named Dīrghajihvī, in the Madhyamikā of the Caraka recension in the glorious Kāṭhaka Yajurveda.

One who desires, "May it be arranged for offspring according to prior order," should draw [cups] with Aindravāyava at the head.
Following the proper arrangement of the sacrifice, it is arranged for offspring;
following an improper arrangement of the sacrifice, it is not arranged.
He arranges for offspring according to prior order;
the younger does not surpass the older.
For one who is ill, he should draw [cups] with Aindravāyava at the head.
He whose [body] is ill is deprived of breath;
the Aindravāyava is breath;
he prospers him with breath itself.
Those among whose consecrated ones one dies should draw [cups] with Maitrāvaruṇa at the head.
Those among whose consecrated ones one dies are deprived of out-breath and in-breath;
Mitrā-Varuṇā are out-breath and in-breath;
they encompass out-breath and in-breath at the mouth;
therefore out-breath and in-breath are at the mouth.
A late-born [son] should draw [cups] with Āśvina at the head.
The Aśvins were late-born among the gods;
they attained the top;
the Aśvins are his deities, who is late-born;
he takes hold of them;
they lead him to the top.
One who has attained prosperity should draw [cups] with Śukra at the head.
Yonder sun is Śukra;
this is the end;
having reached the end of prosperity, a man turns back;
from the end he seizes the end;
he does not become worse than that, such as he is when sacrificing.
One practicing sorcery should draw [cups] with Manthin at the head.
An afflicted vessel is this vessel of Manthin;
he causes him to be seized by death itself.
One against whom sorcery is practiced should draw [cups] with Ukthya at the head.
The sensory power and virility of all vessels is the vessel of Ukthya;
with all sensory power he overpowers him.
"May we not go from thee to desolate fields; do not kick away [the pail] with milk; do not burn us! / May we not suffer for sin committed by another; / O Sarasvatī, lead us to better [fortune]!"
so [he says].
Desolate fields belong to death;
thereby he does not go to the fields of death.
One whose father and grandfather were virtuous, yet he does not attain that, should draw [cups] with Āgrāyaṇa at the head.
He whose father and grandfather are virtuous, yet he does not attain that, is deprived of speech and sensory power.
The breast of the sacrifice, as it were, speech as it were, is the Āgrāyaṇa;
he prospers him with speech, with sensory power.
For one who is ill, he should draw full cups.
Pain reaches the breaths of him who is ill;
the cups are breaths;
he frees his breaths from pain.
When Parjanya does not rain, he should draw full cups.
Pain reaches the breaths of creatures when Parjanya does not rain;
the cups are breaths;
he frees the breaths of creatures from pain;
it rains suddenly.

"O Lord of the field, yield to us a sweet wave like a milk-cow milk, / dripping with honey, like clarified butter well-purified; may the lords of truth be gracious to us!"
"Thou art taken with a support; / for Aditi of four udders, thee!"
"Stir up from the ocean, O Maruts; do ye, rich in moisture, cause rain to fall! / Your milk-cows, O wondrous ones, fail not; the chariots have turned in pursuit of those who journey in beauty."
"Thou art taken with a support; / for Aditi of four udders, thee!"
"The great mother of those of good vows, the wife of truth, we would invoke for aid, / of mighty rule, unaging, widespread, affording good shelter, Aditi of good guidance."
"Thou art taken with a support; / for Aditi of four udders, thee!"
"This is thy womb; / for Aditi of four udders, thee!"
Rain departed from the gods;
it settled in these worlds;
the gods resolved to seek it;
they saw this cup;
they drew it;
thereby they brought down rain from these worlds.
One who desires rain should draw this [cup]:
"O Lord of the field, a sweet wave..." [he says];
yonder Lord of the field rains from up there;
"Stir up from the ocean, O Maruts..." [he says];
the Maruts bring the released rain;
"The great mother of those of good vows..." [he says];
this [earth] is Aditi; on her it rains;
these deities rule over rain;
them he has recourse to;
he brings down rain from these worlds.
The vessel has four teats;
four are the quarters;
from the quarters he brings down rain.
Whichever teat it first enters, from that quarter it rains.
Even while the vessel is being made, it rains.
With an earthen [vessel] he draws;
with a wooden one he offers;
for an earthen [vessel] has not attained the oblation.

With 'Thou art the strainer of the Vasu, having a hundred streams; thou art the strainer of the Vasus, having a thousand streams',
the strainer is the share of the Vasus;
he makes it for them.
Prajāpati created offspring.
His two cooking pots collapsed.
He saw these two cooking pots;
with them he took [it].
When these two cooking pots are [used],
he puts back the two cooking pots of Prajāpati.
With 'Free from consumption, I unite you with offspring',
he makes them free from consumption.
With 'Becoming abundant through increase of wealth',
he makes them attain abundance.
With 'Swelling, full of honey, full of ghee',
he makes the oblation full of honey, full of ghee for the gods.
With 'Living, may we sit near you living',
he utters a blessing.
With 'Thou art the heat (gharma) of Mātariśvan',
the heat of Mātariśvan is the mid-space.
With 'Thou art heaven, thou art earth',
he made it heaven and earth.
With 'All-sustaining by the highest abode, thou art uncrooked; do not be crooked',
he firms it.
He covers [it] with the strainer;
he unites the milk of the plants and of the beasts.
With 'She is all-life, she is all-embracing, she is all-sustaining',
that [heaven] is all-life, the mid-space is all-embracing, this [earth] is all-sustaining;
he causes these worlds to yield in proper order;
these worlds, yielded, milk for him in proper order.
He consecrates three with a yajus;
three are these worlds;
he places sap in these worlds;
therefore creatures live upon these worlds;
he becomes one upon whom others live, who knows thus.
With 'Offered is the drop, offered is the droplet', he consecrates [it],
against spilling.
With 'To Agni, the high firmament, svāhā',
this [earth] is Agni, the high firmament;
much of this [milk] spills while being milked;
he establishes it on this [earth],
against spilling.
He should not milk into a wooden vessel;
a wooden vessel contains fire;
he would sacrifice with stale oblation.
And as to that, the Dārteyas used to say: 'The cake is the mouth of the oblation; not from this is there the staleness of the milk-oblation; it should indeed be milked.'
A Śūdra should not milk;
he is born from the non-existent;
it would be non-existent.
Whatever indeed passes beyond the strainer, that is oblation.
A Śūdra should not milk the Agnihotra;
for they do not purify that.
With 'Mix yourselves, O righteous ones', he pours back,
for cookedness;
for the gods desire what is cooked.
With 'Thee, Indra's share, I curdle with Soma',
he makes it Soma;
Soma indeed is the mystic sānnāyya of the gods;
his Soma-drinking is continuous, who, knowing thus, sacrifices with the sānnāyya.
With 'Thou art unconquered for Viṣṇu',
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he makes it unconquered for the sacrifice.
He should not cover [it] with an earthen vessel;
if he were to cover it with an earthen vessel, the oblation would have the ancestors as its deity.
He covers it with a wooden vessel;
a wooden vessel contains fire;
Agni guards the oblation.
He should make it watery;
water smites demons;
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
With 'O Viṣṇu, guard the oblation',
Viṣṇu guards the oblation.
With 'O waters, be awake',
the waters are the guardians of the sacrifice;
he commits it to them.

With 'At the impulse of the god Savitṛ',
impelled by Savitṛ he pours it together for the deities.
With 'Pleasing to such-and-such',
for whichever deity he pours it together, he makes it the oblation;
he also makes it pleasing to that [deity].
He pours it together over the purifier;
he makes it the oblation.
He pours the waters on.
The waters are ambrosia;
he makes the oblation ambrosia.
With 'United are the waters with the plants, united the plants with the sap',
these plants invigorate one another.
With 'What of you is rich, [belonging] to the rich; what of you is fit for oblation, [belonging] to that fit for oblation; what of you is living, [belonging] to the living',
the waters are rich, the plants full of honey, cattle are living;
having united the waters, plants, and cattle into one for him, he makes them full of honey.
With 'For thee that generates', he mixes [it];
he creates a pair.
With 'Thou art the head of Makha',
Makha is the sacrifice;
he makes the head of the sacrifice.
With 'Thou art the cauldron, all life, spread wide, may the lord of the sacrifice spread wide',
he spreads it.
With 'Burnt up is the demon',
he smites away the demons from the mouth of the sacrifice.
This cauldron is indeed set on the fire every half-month, namely the puroḍāśa;
being unappeased, it would be capable of burning up the cattle of the sacrificer.
When he carries the fire around it,
he makes it an animal,
for appeasement, to prevent burning up.
He carries [it] around thrice;
three are these worlds;
from these worlds he appeases it.
When the puroḍāśa was placed on the fire, the demons wished to kill it;
Agni, the demon-killer named Naka, was in heaven;
he smote away the demons from it.
With 'May the god Savitṛ cook thee upon the highest firmament',
[it is] for the smiting away of the demons.
With 'May Agni not injure thy body',
[it is] for non-injury;
he causes it to take a crust.
Therefore the brain is covered around.
The potsherds that belong to the head are these potsherds;
the puroḍāśa is the brain.
If he should not cover it over, the brain would be exposed;
when he covers it over,
therefore the brain is hidden.
He covers it over with ashes;
therefore the bone is covered with flesh.
He covers it over with the veda;
therefore the head is covered with hair.
The gods did not find him on whom they could wipe off the cruel [part] of the sacrifice.
Agni said: 'I will generate for you him on whom ye will wipe off the cruel [part] of the sacrifice.'
He cast [a drop] with a burning coal onto the waters;
thence Ekata was born;
a second;
thence Dvita;
a third;
thence Trita.
That he fashioned [them] from himself, that is the selfhood of those belonging to the self;
that he fashioned [them] from the waters, that is the water-nature of those belonging to water.
He pours [it] out inside the vedi;
he obtains that very [blessing].
He sprinkles it with a burning brand,
for cookedness;
for the gods desire what is cooked.
They went, wiping [the guilt] off one after another;
they wiped it off on the one upon whom the sun rises [asleep];
the one upon whom the sun rises, on the one upon whom the sun sets [asleep];
the one upon whom the sun sets, on the one with deformed nails;
the one with deformed nails, on the one with black teeth;
the one with black teeth, on the one whose younger brother married first;
the one whose younger brother married first, on the younger brother who married before his elder;
the younger brother who married before his elder, on the husband of an elder sister whose younger is married;
the husband of an elder sister whose younger is married, on the slayer of a man;
the slayer of a man, on the slayer of a brahmin;
the slayer of a brahmin, on the slayer of an embryo;
guilt does not pass beyond the slayer of an embryo.

With 'At the impulse of the god Savitṛ', he takes up the wooden sword [sphya];
impelled by Savitṛ he takes it up along with the deities.
With 'Thou art Indra's right arm, thousand-spiked, hundred-edged',
the wooden sword is indeed a thunderbolt;
he sharpens the thunderbolt itself,
being about to strike his rival. With 'O earth, divine place of sacrifice, let me not injure the root of thy plant',
[it is] for the non-injury of the plants.
With 'Go to the pen, the cow-stall',
the metres are indeed the pen, the cow-stall;
he makes the metres a pen, a cow-stall for him.
With 'May thy heaven rain',
he brings down rain itself.
With 'Bind, O god Savitṛ, in the furthest earth with a hundred fetters',
a man has two rivals: he whom he hates, and he who hates him;
both of these he binds for him in the furthest earth with a hundred fetters.
With 'May not thy drop leap to heaven',
the drop is whatever sap she has;
these creatures live upon it;
he restrains it upon her,
so that it should not leap away.
With 'Let not Araru fly to heaven',
Araru is indeed the rival;
he thrusts the rival away from the heavenly world.
There was an Asura named Araru;
he feared, 'The gods will overcome us by the sacrifice.'
He smeared the earth with poison, making it impure.
Indra slew Vṛtra;
his blood ran along this [earth];
it became impure.
When he digs up,
whatever of it is impure, unsuited for sacrifice, that he smites away.
There was an Asura named Araru;
he feared, 'The gods will thrust me out from the earth.'
He lay creeping along the earth.
Indra noticed him.
With 'Drive away Araru, the non-sacrificial place, from the earth, the sacrificial place', he smote him from the earth.
He flew to heaven.
With 'Let not Araru fly to heaven', he thrust him back from heaven.
Then the gods prospered, the Asuras were vanquished.
For whomsoever knowing thus the stambayajus is carried, and who, knowing thus, carries it, he excludes the rival from these worlds; he prospers in himself, his rival is vanquished.
This earth belonged to the Asuras;
the gods said, 'Give us of this.'
They said, 'Speak for yourselves.'
Agni conquered the eastern quarter;
the Vasus the southern, the Rudras the western, the Ādityas the northern.
The gods found this [earth] from the Asuras.
Then the gods prospered, the Asuras were vanquished.
He who, knowing thus, encloses the altar with these deities, wins this [earth] of his rival; he prospers in himself, his rival is vanquished.
What was pure and what was impure went apart from the earth;
the pure went eastward and northward;
the impure westward and southward.
He should make the altar sloping eastward and northward;
he makes it pure, fit for sacrifice;
and also for the separation of the pure and the impure.
He raises the two arms eastward;
he encloses the Āhavanīya fire.
The hips [of the altar] are westward;
he encloses the Gārhapatya fire.
The demons indeed crawl along the root;
he should not cut it with the fingernail.
If he should cut it with the fingernail, he would become one with deformed nails;
he cuts it with the wooden sword.
The wooden sword is indeed a thunderbolt;
with the thunderbolt he slays the demons.

Prajāpati was both ghee and honey.
From where the honey was, from there he created creatures;
therefore, there is as it were generation from honey;
therefore, they do not perform rites with honey,
for that has lost its freshness.
They perform rites with ghee;
for this sacrificial butter [ājya] belonging to Prajāpati has not lost its freshness;
fresh among the gods is Prajāpati.
He sets it on the Gārhapatya fire;
the wife looks at it;
this is indeed the wife's taking hold of the sacrifice;
and he places a pair at the opening of the sacrifice
for generation.
What the wife does at the sacrifice, that is a pair.
In that the wife looks at it,
he makes a pair.
That which the wife looks at is impure, unsuited for sacrifice;
he sets it on the Āhavanīya fire;
he makes it pure, fit for sacrifice.
The gods and the Asuras did indeed perform equally at the sacrifice;
whatever the gods did, that the Asuras did.
Indra saw this glance [avakāśa];
with it he looked at it;
with it he overcame the Asuras.
Then the gods prospered, the Asuras were vanquished.
He who, knowing thus, looks at the sacrificial butter with this, overcomes his rival;
he prospers in himself, his rival is vanquished.
With 'Burnt up is the demon',
he smites away the demons from the opening of the sacrifice.
With 'Ye are unwhetted, destroying the rival',
he makes them unwhetted.
He cleanses the ladles;
he purifies them.
He cleanses the dipping-spoon [sruva] first;
he sharpens their male first;
then the Juhū,
then the Upabhṛt,
then the Dhruvā.
Yonder [heaven] is the Juhū, the intermediate space the Upabhṛt, this [earth] the Dhruvā;
he cleanses these worlds in order of precedence.
The Juhū belongs to the deity of the sacrificer, the Upabhṛt to the deity of the rival.
Scooping four times into the Juhū, he takes more sacrificial butter;
scooping eight times into the Upabhṛt, less;
he makes his rival an underling to him.

"The Atirātra is the highest of sacrificial rites," the theologians say; "why do they undertake it first?"
In that they undertake the Atirātra, they undertake the Agniṣṭoma first, then the Ukthya, then the Ṣoḍaśin, then the Atirātra.
Having undertaken the sacrificial rites according to precedence, having taken hold of them, having encompassed them, they sit drinking soma.
They undertake the Jyotiṣṭoma first.
The Jyotiṣṭoma is indeed the mouth of the stomas;
from the mouth itself they thus employ the stomas.
The Jyotiṣṭoma attains to the virāj: the Go is redundant by one, the Āyus is deficient by one;
being praised together, they attain to the virāj.
Two verses remain over.
The virāj is the heavenly world;
the sacrificers are two-footed;
in the heavenly world, in the virāj, the two-footed sacrificers become established.
The Rathantara is the pṛṣṭha.
This earth is the Rathantara;
having taken hold of this very [earth], they sit.
"The Rathantara is by day, the Rathantara is by night," the theologians say; "how is that not monotonous?"
The Saubhara is the Brahma-sāman at the third pressing; it is great [Bṛhat].
He places it in the middle,
for the separation of the Rathantara;
thereby it is not monotonous.
From the Atirātra they undertake the twenty-fourth day.
Twenty-four are the half-months of the year;
the year is reached half-month by half-month;
half-month by half-month they thus reach the year.
Its stotriya verses are three hundred and sixty;
three hundred and sixty indeed are the days of the year;
the year is reached day by day;
day by day they thus reach the year.
It is an Ukthya;
ukthas are cattle;
they win cattle.
The Bṛhat is the pṛṣṭha;
the heavenly world is the Bṛhat;
they reach the heavenly world by it.
The sāman called Trayastriṃśa is at the midday Pavamāna;
thirty-three are the gods;
thereby they reach the gods.
For this indeed they undertake the year;
they reached the gods,
they reached the heavenly world,
they won cattle;
they reached the year half-month by half-month,
they reached [it] day by day.
On that twenty-fourth day, having encompassed all the deities, they sit drinking soma.
Those who undertake the year turned away from here do not attain it safely;
and those who undertake it turned hither from over there, they cross over it safely.
In that [they do] thus, they undertake it turned hither from over there.

They undertake the Jyotiṣṭoma first;
by it they become established in this world.
They undertake the Goṣṭoma second;
by it they become established in the intermediate space.
They undertake the Āyuṣṭoma last;
by it they become established in that world.
This world is indeed light, the intermediate space is the cow, and yonder [world] is life.
In that they undertake these stomas,
the sacrificial session performers go establishing themselves in these worlds.
The Jyotiṣṭoma attains to the virāj: the Go is redundant by one, the Āyus is deficient by one.
When chanted, they attain to the virāj.
The virāj is vigor.
In that they go by these stomas,
the session performers go securing vigor.
They do not incur the affliction of hunger;
they do not become hungry,
for the session performers are, as it were, pressed hard by hunger.
The Agniṣṭomas are on both sides;
they are the rims.
The Ukthyas are in the middle;
they are the navel.
This is the moving chariot-wheel of the gods.
In that they go by this six-day period,
they mount the chariot-wheel of the gods
for safety.
They attain well-being.
They go with light on both sides.
The heavenly world is indeed light;
the session performers thus go establishing themselves on both sides in the heavenly world.
They go by a six-day period;
the seasons are indeed six;
they go establishing themselves in the seasons.
There are two six-day periods;
they amount to twelve days.
Ten indeed are the vital breaths in man, the breasts are eleventh and twelfth;
thus they turn around man.
There are three six-day periods;
they amount to eighteen days:
nine and nine;
nine are the vital breaths;
thus they turn around the vital breaths.
There are four six-day periods;
they amount to twenty-four days.
Twenty-four are the half-months of the year;
half-month by half-month the year is reached;
thus they turn around the year.
There are five six-day periods;
they amount to thirty days.
Ten are the fingers of the hand, ten of the foot, ten are the vital breaths: that makes thirty.
The virāj has thirty syllables; man is connected with the virāj;
thus they turn around man.
"The year is unestablished," they indeed say, "broader than an established place."
This much is the brāhmaṇa of the year as of a month;
month by month they go establishing themselves, who know thus.

Now these Parasāmans:
Of these Parasāmans, that which is the first is the last; that which is the last is the first; that which is in the middle remains in the middle.
Thereby, by whatever path these go, by that they return from yonder,
for the continuity, for the recognition of these worlds.
After the Viśvajit they undertake descending stomas.
Concerning this they say: "They lose what has been conquered, they undertake descending stomas that are already accomplished, they turn back from the heavenly world, and they stretch out the sacrifice facing backward."
They go in that way for five months, and of the sixth month three abhiplavas, and the two days: the Go and the Āyus.
They undertake the pṛṣṭhas: "The pṛṣṭhas are indeed the yield of the sacrifice; let us milk the sacrifice, both cattle and immortality, and let us stretch out the sacrifice facing forward."
The seasons are indeed the pṛṣṭhas.
Thereby they go establishing themselves in the seasons.
The heavenly world indeed is the pṛṣṭhas.
Thereby they go establishing themselves in the heavenly world.
Divine dominion indeed is the stomas of the Āyus.
Thereby they go ascending to divine dominion.
They undertake the chandomas.
The chandomas are indeed cattle.
They obtain cattle.
They undertake the tenth day, generative, belonging to Prajāpati, a foundation.
They obtain generation, offspring, [and] a foundation.
They undertake the Mahāvrata day.
Concerning this they say: "This day, which is the Mahāvrata day, is the solstice; it is to be undertaken at the solstice."
Then they say: "This day, which is the Mahāvrata day, belongs to Prajāpati; it is to be undertaken in the latter half of the year.
He who is born after six months, or who is born after seven months, perishes; by him one enjoys no benefit.
In a year, indeed, semen that has been deposited is brought forth.
So, when they undertake the Mahāvrata day in the latter half of the year,
they bring forth in a year this semen that has been deposited.
According to the seasons they arrange the seasons;
in proper sequence they bring forth offspring.
They undertake the twenty-fivefold stoma, the stoma of man.
Ten are the fingers of the hand, ten of the feet, two arms, two thighs, the trunk is the twenty-fifth.
When they undertake this stoma,
from their own stoma the session performers bring forth their own self,
for prosperity.
Concerning this they say: "This day which is the Mahāvrata belongs to Prajāpati; on it all cups should be drawn, for Prajāpati has pervaded, as it were, everything."
One cup should be drawn,
for Prajāpati is one.
Three should be drawn;
three are these worlds;
in these worlds they prosper.
Five should be drawn;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
they obtain the sacrifice.
Seven should be drawn;
seven are the domestic animals;
they obtain them.
Nine should be drawn;
nine are the breaths;
they establish themselves in the breaths.
Fifteen should be drawn;
fifteen are the nights of a half-month; by half-months the year is obtained;
by half-months they obtain the year.
Seventeen should be drawn;
Prajāpati is seventeenfold;
they obtain Prajāpati.
Twenty-one should be drawn;
yonder sun is twenty-first;
they obtain yonder sun.
Twenty-seven should be drawn;
thrice-nine are these worlds;
in these worlds they prosper.
Thirty-three should be drawn;
thirty-three are the deities;
they establish themselves in the deities.
Thus ends the thirty-third sthānaka, named Sattrāṇi, in the Orimikā in the Caraka recension of the glorious Kaṭha Yajurveda.

Having mounted a chair, the Udgātṛ sings the laud with the Mahāvrata;
having mounted a swing, the Hotṛ recites the Mahaduktha in response.
Standing upon a standing-board, the two Adhvaryus utter the response;
the others sit on grass cushions.
The intermediate space is indeed food;
from yonder indeed it is granted;
in this [earth] it is brought forth;
therefore it is born for the intermediate space,
in that they perform [the rite] with the Mahāvrata seated in the intermediate space,
for the attainment of food and proper nourishment.
Moreover, in the presence of the gods, they conquer the high assembly;
they proceed stepping onto the heavenly world.
There are a praiser and a blamer.
The one praises the session performers, the other blames; he who praises,
whatever of theirs is well-lauded and well-recited, that he praises;
then he who blames, whatever of theirs is ill-lauded and ill-recited, that he strikes away.
A Śūdra and an Ārya pull at a hide.
The gods and the Asuras indeed contended for the sun;
the gods won it.
He causes the Āryan class to win;
he causes his own self to win.
The Ārya should be within the altar ground, the Śūdra outside the altar ground.
The hide should be white and round,
the form of the sun.
Drums sound in all the corners;
the speech that is in the quarters, that they secure thereby.
There is an earth-drum;
the speech that is in this [earth], that they secure thereby.
Lutes sound;
the speech that is in cattle, that they secure thereby.
Reed-lutes sound;
the speech that is in plants, that they secure thereby.
Flutes of reed sound;
the speech that is in trees, that they secure thereby.
The harp has a hundred strings;
man has a life-span of a hundred years, has a hundredfold virility;
he secures life-span and virility indeed.
A student of the Veda and a courtesan revile each other;
for in the world all voices sound.
They enact a copulation;
indeed, as those bringing forth offspring, these [performers] sit for a year's session;
their generative capacity is covered over by the year itself;
in that they enact a copulation,
[it is] for the attainment of the generative capacity of the year itself.
Women bearing water-jars approach,
for success.
Singing "idam madhuram," clad in armor, they go around;
they magnify the Mahāvrata itself,
and also for the possession of Indra.
The splendor of the sacred power is of one kind, that of the ruling power of another;
in that he is consecrated on a black antelope skin, that is the splendor of the sacred power;
in that, clad in armor, he has a strung bow, that is the splendor of the ruling power;
so both take place,
for the securing of both splendors.

Prajāpati indeed measured out the Twelve-day rite from his own self.
Prajāpati is the year.
He took three nights from each of four months;
that became the consecration.
He took three nights from each of the next four [months];
those became the Upasads.
He took three nights from each of the next four [months];
that became the pressing.
Therefore they say: "As great as the year is, so great is the Twelve-day rite,"
for it was fashioned from the entire year.
He created the first day, and the Ehīḍa sāman, this world, fire, light;
and he created the second day, and the Ūrdhveḍa sāman, the world of the intermediate space, wind, light;
and he created the third day, and the Aiḍa sāman with iḍās, yonder world, the sun, light;
and he created the fourth day, and the Paristubdheḍa sāman, and the world in which brilliance is the light;
and he created the fifth day, and the Ādhyardheḍa sāman, and the world in which truth is the light;
and he created the sixth day, and the Hoiḍa sāman, and the world in which the sacred power is the light;
and he created the seventh day, and the Punarnitunnā iḍā, and the world in which austerity is the light;
and he created the eighth day, and the Viṣvagaiḍa sāman, and the world in which cosmic order is the light;
and he created the ninth day, and the Hoiḍa sāman, and the world in which the sacred power is the light;
and he created the tenth day, and the Dvīḍa sāman, and the world in which immortality is the light.
The in-breath is the introductory [day], the out-breath the concluding [day];
therefore, as much as is done on the introductory [day], so much is done on the concluding [day];
for as great as the in-breath is, so great is the out-breath.
These worlds, these sāmans, these lights, these splendors he secures, who knowing thus sacrifices with the Twelve-day rite.

Fourfold indeed are those sets of three [nights].
By those that are the first, he purifies himself;
by those that are the second, he becomes pure, fit for sacrifice;
by those that are the third, he cleanses his limbs;
then by those that are the fourth, whatever stain he has within or without, that he shakes off.
Man indeed they eat, who officiate at the Twelve-day rite.
He who eats [the portion] of the animal sacrifice, eats meat;
he who [drinks] the whey, [drinks] blood;
he who [eats] the parched grains, [eats] bones;
he who [eats] the clarified butter, [eats] marrow;
he who [eats] the roasted grains, [eats] hair;
he who [eats] the sacrificial cake, [eats] the brain;
he who consumes the King [Soma], [consumes] sweat;
he who [eats] the groats, [eats] the feces.
He indeed eats the sacrificial session-gift who speaks untruth.
His sacrificial session-gift becomes made savory, his sacrificial session-gift is not devoured, who knows thus.

He is initiated for twelve [days].
Then indeed he is born;
for that is truly born which is born from austerity,
not that which is from a woman. He is initiated for twelve [days].
Twelve indeed are the impure things in man:
hair, skin, blood, meat, bone, marrow, serum, spleen, tears, rheum, sweat, and what he urinates.
These twelve indeed are the impure things in man.
In that he is initiated for twelve [days],
he strikes them away.
He is initiated for twelve [days];
there are twelve Upasads, and twelve [days] after being pressed; these amount to thirty-six.
The Bṛhatī has thirty-six syllables;
cattle belong to the Bṛhatī;
in the Bṛhatī he thus reaches cattle;
having reached cattle in the Bṛhatī, he secures them.
Then the four syllables that step out, that is the four-footed Anuṣṭubh.
Speech is the Anuṣṭubh;
in speech he thus reaches cattle;
having reached cattle in speech, he secures them.
Therefore cattle recognize their name by speech;
by speech they exist, accomplished.

When Indra had slain Vṛtra, his virile power and vigor departed.
It entered into all this: the waters, the plants, the trees.
With that the gods exerted themselves.
They brought this together, which is the Sānnāyya.
Curd and milk indeed are a pair.
What was mixed, appearing like scum, like whey, that was the embryo itself.
From heat (tapas) indeed offspring are born;
it attains heat-nature when it attains cooked-nature;
from that indeed one is propagated.
Because he created offspring with the curd-mixture for the Viśve Devas, therefore these offspring belong to the Viśve Devas.
The drops indeed are the Viśve Devas;
them indeed he sacrifices to in this.
For offspring live off what is given from yonder.
This indeed is the womb of offspring.
The Maruts desired it;
they were emitted seized by distress.
Because it is for the Svatavants,
[it is] for independence, for expiation.
The Maruts indeed are cattle;
this is for the propagation of cattle.
It is on seven potsherds,
for the Maruts are seven,
for cutting off.
[It is] for the Maruts;
moreover, he thereby secures village food.
Created by the Vaiśvadeva, they dispersed in different directions;
he encompassed them with [the cake] for Heaven and Earth,
just for the encompassing of the created offspring.
Because it is on one potsherd,
thereby it belongs to Prajāpati;
he thereby firmly establishes the deities toward Prajāpati.
The one-potsherd [cake] indeed is the sacrificer;
the Āhavanīya is the heavenly world;
he offers it as a complete oblation;
he causes him, having become an oblation, to go to the heavenly world.
The one-potsherd [cake] is the self;
ghee is cattle.
When he fills it up with ghee,
he enriches him with cattle.
If he were to fill it completely, cattle would attend on him without giving enjoyment,
for he would make him lower than the cattle.
It should be made with visible back;
he makes him higher than the cattle;
cattle attend on him giving enjoyment.
That is not to be heeded:
he should just fill it up,
for cattle indeed do give enjoyment.
He places it on the barhis,
for firm standing.
When the sacrificer totters, the offspring totter after him;
when the sacrificer stands firm, they stand firm after him.
It should be offered standing straight and firm,
for the firm standing of the sacrificer.

He sacrifices to the Vājins;
the Vājins indeed are cattle;
them he sacrifices then.
This is not quite like a well-performed sacrifice;
for when the sacrifice has concluded and the enclosing-sticks have been thrown into the fire, he offers.
Because the Hotṛ sacrifices without drawing breath,
thereby the sacrifice is performed.
He offers an after-sacrifice;
Agni is the finished work, Agni is the firm foundation;
[it is] for the firm foundation of the finished work.
The Vājins indeed are cattle;
if he were to conclude, he would conclude them;
for un-concluded, they are continually propagated.
Pouring along the sacrificial grass, he takes [the whey];
he places semen in the bulls.
Offspring, indeed, are the sacrificial grass; semen is the whey (vājina);
he places semen in the offspring.
Sitting with knees raised, he sacrifices;
for the animal has its knees raised;
he places semen in the animal.
Having invited one another, they consume it;
for this is like a Soma-draught.
All the priests eat of it, [thinking:]
“May the Vājins bear my sacrifice.”
He should eat of it himself;
he places vigor in his own self.

Having created creatures and having sacrificed away distress, he desired: “May I slay Vṛtra.”
Having joined together with these deities—the Maruts as the folk, Agni as the vanguard—he advanced.
Having approached Vṛtra and having seen Vṛtra, he stood paralyzed in the thighs, not daring to attack.
The Maruts charged upon him with wind-chariots of reed;
they went over [him].
Where they reached his vital part, there he writhed;
then indeed they scorched him thoroughly;
therefore [these offerings are] the Sāṃtapanas.
The Sākamedhas are destroying Vṛtra.
With Agni as the vanguard, Indra slew Vṛtra,
precisely for having a vanguard.
This Agni is the commander of the gods' army;
this [offering] is indeed for raising the army.
Indra could not raise the thunderbolt against Vṛtra;
he poured out this portion for the Maruts;
the Maruts scorched him thoroughly for heroic strength;
with that heroic strength he raised the thunderbolt;
then indeed they scorched him thoroughly;
therefore [they are] the Sāṃtapanas.
It is to be poured out at midday,
for then he scorches both ends.
It is a caru,
for he scorches it all around.
The gods did not find Vṛtra’s vital part;
the Maruts penetrated him with their razor-sharp fellies;
then indeed they scorched him thoroughly;
therefore [they are] the Sāṃtapanas.

About to slay Vṛtra tomorrow, they fasted nearby.
They said: “To whom indeed will this [world] belong tomorrow? For whom will there be cooking?”
They cooked this boiled rice.
They looked out for the cattle: “Whichever of these two [sides] they shall favor tomorrow, they shall be the conquerors.”
With this [rice] they went forth towards them;
with this they held them;
this is the path of cattle.
They dwelt [there], victorious on the morrow.
They said: “To whom indeed will this [world] belong tomorrow? For whom will there be cooking?”
They cooked this boiled rice.
They said: “Let us not eat what has not been offered.”
They offered to the Maruts, the Gṛhamedhas;
the Maruts, the Gṛhamedhas, are cattle;
to them indeed they offered then.
They were arrayed for battle.
The Asuras sent hunger against the gods.
The gods, having heard it coming, cooked this boiled rice.
Not having found a place among the gods, it entered the Asuras again;
it burned them up.
Even in the vicinity, one should cook boiled rice;
he sends hunger against his rival;
it burns him up.
What belongs to the Cāturmāsyas, that of theirs is cattle-bringing, like a domestic sacrifice.
The Gṛhamedhīya is indeed a domestic sacrifice.
Neither fore-sacrifices nor after-sacrifices are offered;
he does not recite the kindling verses.
He offers the two butter portions;
thereby the sacrifice is performed.
He cuts off [a portion] together for Agni.
Agni is the finished work;
Agni is the firm foundation;
[it is] for the firm foundation of the finished work.
The Gṛhamedhas are cattle;
the Iḍā is cattle;
therefore they invoke the Iḍā.
The scraped-out residue is set down,
for continuity.
Indra dwelt away from both of them.
The gods deposited this portion for Indra:
“Having his portion set down, may he choose us tomorrow.”
The roar is the vaṣaṭ-call.
Moreover, they then called away the bull of the Asuras:
“May he propagate us.”
He calls upon the bull;
he thereby invokes Indra;
and he also wrenches away the prosperity of his rival.

The cows dwell with their calves
for the sake of Sākamedha-nature.
If the wife were not to eat, they would not be Sākamedhas;
in that the wife eats,
[it is] for the sake of Sākamedha-nature.
Nirṛti indeed seizes that of the sacrifice which a woman eats;
a woman indeed is Nirṛti;
this ladle is the hand of a woman.
In that he offers with the ladle,
he appeases Nirṛti by means of Nirṛti herself.
Nirṛti indeed seizes that part of the sacrifice which does not attain proper consecration;
the ladle is indeed seized by Nirṛti,
for it moves about in what is heated.
In that he offers with the ladle,
he appeases Nirṛti by means of [that which is] seized by Nirṛti.
He advanced to slay Vṛtra tomorrow;
the Maruts came sporting around him;
they dispelled his panic;
they emboldened him;
therefore [they are] the Krīḍins.
They were arrayed for battle;
the gods did not see the far end of the Asuras;
they saw the Maruts, the Krīḍins, sporting;
they knew: “These indeed have minds bent on victory.”
For them they poured out this portion;
thence they conquered;
this [offering] is indeed for victory.
Yonder sun indeed is Indra;
the rays are the Krīḍins;
they perform [the rite] together with the rays
for victory.
The gods indeed did not perceive that Vṛtra was slain;
the Maruts sported over him;
therefore [they are] the Krīḍins.
Then these five oblations
[are] for continuity.
Then [the cake] for Indra and Agni;
Indra and Agni indeed hurled the thunderbolt at him;
also they say: “Indra and Agni held the thunderbolt for him.”
Then [the cake] for Indra;
with this Indra indeed slew Vṛtra;
he took up this choice portion;
it is indeed his choice portion;
it is indeed [his] share.
Then [the cake] for Viśvakarman;
[saying,] “All my deeds are done,”
for having slain Vṛtra he became Viśvakarman.
He pours the libation with: “May Agni relish the hotṛ’s office, may he relish the message,”
for the fulfillment of the oblations, to make [them] thirty.

Having created creatures, having sacrificed away distress, and having slain Vṛtra, they desired immortality;
the heavenly world indeed is immortality.
The year is twelve months, the heavenly world;
in that there are twelve oblations,
he thereby wins immortality.
He indeed has cut short this year;
he indeed has fallen into distress with regard to this year.
In that he brings about six and six [oblations],
six indeed are the seasons;
he thereby restores the seasons;
he follows along as they are restored;
thence he is propagated.
He indeed has surpassed this year;
in that there are twelve oblations,
he turns back towards the year.
He indeed has fallen into distress with regard to this year;
the fathers are the seasons;
he propagates him [the year];
he is propagated in accordance with him as he is being propagated.
This year of his has indeed been obtained, the seasons obtained;
now his fathers are unobtained;
in that this is the sacrifice to the fathers,
his fathers thereby become obtained and pleased.
[The oblation] is to be poured out from the south,
for the south belongs to the fathers.
[Others say] it is to be poured out from both sides,
for both are sacrificed to;
that is not to be heeded;
it is to be poured out only from the south,
for the south belongs to the fathers.
The cake is on six potsherds;
six indeed are the seasons;
the seasons indeed become obtained and pleased by him through this.
Not indeed with parched grains, nor with the mash is it a sacrifice;
in that there is this cake, by that the sacrifice is performed.
Now these parched grains:
these are indeed nectar in yonder world, the boundless nights of the year;
by these, they indeed become obtained and pleased by him.
Not indeed with parched grains, nor with the cake is it a sacrifice to the fathers;
in that there is this mash, by that it is a sacrifice to the fathers.
It is prepared in the milk of a cow that suckles an adopted calf,
for she belongs to the fathers.
He stirs it up closest [to himself] with one [stalk],
for the fathers are in one world;
he stirs it up towards the south,
for the south belongs to the fathers;
he stirs it up without touching [the bottom],
for that reaches the fathers.

[The ground] is not to be dug up towards the east,
for it is a sacrifice to the fathers;
nor towards the south,
for it is a sacrifice.
He digs up intermediate between both directions,
for both are sacrificed to;
he reaches both, the gods and the fathers.
The altar is four-cornered,
for the fathers are in all quarters.
They sacrifice within an enclosure,
for the fathers are concealed from yonder sun;
also, the fathers are concealed from both gods and men.
He cuts the sacrificial grass close to the root;
thereby [it belongs] to the fathers;
in that [he cuts] without the root,
thereby [it belongs] to the gods;
both are sacrificed to.
He goes around, strewing the sacrificial grass all around,
for these seasons are encircled all around.
He who goes around the sacrifice to the fathers indeed departs from this world;
in that he goes around again without strewing,
he returns to this very world.
They sacrifice to the deities in that former [fire];
if he were to sacrifice there, he would mix the oblations;
he would cause strife.
They make [the oblation] cooked on the Gārhapatya;
thereby the sacrifice is performed.
They bring fire from the rice-cooking [fire];
is it indeed brought from anywhere else?
He recites: “Yearning, we call upon thee,”
for the fathers are yearning.
He recites an anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh is the end;
the fathers are the end;
also the anuṣṭubh is all the metres.
He recites one [verse],
for the fathers are in one world;
he recites [it] three times,
for the fathers are in the third world.
In that he recites one,
thereby [it belongs] to the fathers;
in that he recites [it] three times,
thereby [it belongs] to the gods;
both are sacrificed to.
He chooses neither the ārṣeya nor the hotṛ;
he releases them from death.
He offers the fore-sacrifices without the sacrificial grass;
the sacrificial grass is indeed offspring;
he releases the offspring from death.
Intending to act for men, one puts on the sacred thread normally;
for the gods, he wears it over the right shoulder;
now here it should be worn over the right shoulder [as prācīnāvīta],
for distinction.
Having sliced off [the portions] from the south, stepping beyond to the north, turning southwards, he offers,
for the south belongs to the fathers.

He sacrifices to Soma first;
Soma indeed is the deity of the fathers,
for Soma has the fathers as his deity.
In that he sacrifices to Soma accompanied by the fathers,
he thereby sacrifices to the Soma-drinking fathers.
In that [he sacrifices to those] seated on the sacrificial grass, [he sacrifices] to those who sacrificed;
in that [he sacrifices to the] Agniṣvāttas, [he sacrifices] to the householders;
in that [he sacrifices to] Agni, the bearer of the Kavyas—
two indeed are Agni's bodies:
with the one that bears the Havyas he bears the oblation to the gods, with the one that bears the Kavyas to the fathers.
Agni is the Samiṣṭi, Agni is the firm foundation;
[it is] for the Samiṣṭi, for the firm foundation.
He cuts portions five times each;
for five are the seasons.
He utters the vaṣaṭ-call as 'Svadhā, namaḥ';
for svadhā is for the fathers, namaskāra for the gods.
Two indeed are the yājyā and the anuvākyā of the gods;
with the one he holds forth, with the other he causes [it] to reach [them].
Now here three are to be made,
for separation:
two anuvākyās, one yājyā.
Farther indeed than the gods are the fathers;
that which is the third, with it indeed he reaches out.
The human fathers follow in pursuit of the divine fathers;
he has thereby sacrificed to the divine fathers.
In that he places [the portions] on the corners,
he thereby sacrifices to the human fathers.
He places [them] three times;
for we here look towards three men,
[and] he mentions three beyond [them];
three indeed are the father, the son, the grandson:
for continuity.
He places [them] on all the corners;
he causes [them] to reach all the quarters;
for in all the quarters are the fathers.
He should not place [any] on this [corner];
if he were to place [any] on this [corner], he would encompass them with death.
They follow along that very [direction].
In that he wipes [his hand] on it,
thereby indeed he gratifies them.
With, 'Here, fathers, take delight!' they go out;
they approach the Āhavanīya,
with, 'May we [see] you of good appearance.'
They stand until breathless.
Those who approach the sacrifice to the fathers go to the end of their breath;
having made Agni their overseer, they go to the end of their breath.
With, 'Depart, fathers, fond of Soma!' they come back.
The fathers are indeed attached to them;
[this is] for separation.
With, 'The fathers have taken delight,' they enter.
They throw down a tuft of wool or a fringe of cloth,
for the satisfying of whatever they may have met with there.
He sprinkles water around;
he cleanses indeed.
He walks around again without sprinkling around.
Those who approach the sacrifice to the fathers go to yonder world;
only Prajāpati can lead them up from there.
In that he recites this unexpressed [verse] addressed to Prajāpati: 'By this position generating works...',
Prajāpati himself leads them up from there.
The sacrifice has indeed gone to the fathers;
they return with the Paṅkti;
the sacrifice is fivefold (pāṅkta);
they return together with the sacrifice.
His mind indeed goes to the fathers;
with, 'Now we call back our mind,'
he calls it back to himself.

For him the year has now been won through sacrifice; the fathers have been won through sacrifice;
now the Rudras are not yet won through sacrifice for him;
the Tryambakas are the Rudras.
In that these are the Tryambakas,
thereby for him the Rudras become won through sacrifice [and] pleased.
They are one for each person;
he appeases Rudra for each person.
There is one extra;
he thereby appeases Rudra for the embryos.
They are on one potsherd;
one cannot reach a person with potsherds;
he reaches him in a single way.
They ponder: 'Is butter to be poured over [them], or is butter not to be poured over [them]?'
Butter is indeed to be poured over [them],
for an oblation is not without butter poured over it.
If he were to pour butter over [them], he would bring Rudra down upon the cattle;
therefore butter is not to be poured over [them].
They carry a single firebrand;
for a single firebrand belongs to the Rudras.
They carry it while it smokes;
for what smokes belongs to the Rudras.
They carry it facing away;
they carry Rudra away.
They carry it to this quarter;
in this quarter indeed are the dwellings of the Rudras;
he appeases Rudra in his own dwellings.
With, 'O Rudra, I make the rat thy beast,' he strews one [cake] on a molehill;
he thereby appeases Rudra away from the cattle;
therefore Paśupati is dangerous to them.
They sacrifice at a crossroad;
at a crossroad indeed are the dwellings of the Rudras;
he appeases Rudra in his own dwellings.
With, 'This is thy portion, O Rudra, together with thy sister Ambikā; enjoy it, svāhā!'
Autumn indeed is Rudra's sister Ambikā;
he roams along after her;
therefore he slays most in autumn.
Along with her indeed he appeases him.
He offers with a middle leaf [of the palāśa];
for that is unhurt by the Rakṣases.
If he were to offer with [something] domestic, he would bring Rudra down upon the cattle;
therefore it is offered with [something] wild.
With, 'We have appeased Rudra, O mother,'
they have become free from debt.
With, '[Auspicious] medicine for ox, horse, and man,'
having become free from debt, they make medicine.
With, 'We sacrifice to Tryambaka,' they walk around.
She who may be desirous of a husband should also walk around;
they bring about the finding of a husband for her.
Having tossed them up, they catch them;
having become free from debt, they attain good fortune.
They pour them out together for the sacrificer;
they pour out good fortune together for him.
If [a woman] is desirous of a husband, they should pour them out together for her;
they pour out good fortune together for her.
Having placed them in a net-bag, they hang them on a tree with, 'This is thy portion, O Rudra; with this provision go beyond the Mūjavants!'
The mountain indeed is Rudra's womb;
from there he roams down;
by his own portion he causes him to go to his own womb.
Without looking back they return,
so as not to be pursued by Rudra.
With, 'Fuel art thou; may we prosper,'
having appeased Rudra, they approach prosperity.
With, 'A kindling-stick art thou; may we completely prosper,'
he utters a blessing.
With, 'Splendor art thou; place splendor in me,'
he places splendor in himself.
Ambī indeed is a woman whose name is good fortune (bhagā);
therefore [they are called] Tryambakas.
He indeed is without a firm foundation whose oblation has no firm foundation; the Tryambakas have no firm foundation:
no fore-sacrifices are offered, nor after-sacrifices;
he does not recite the kindling-verses.
He pours out a mess of boiled rice for Aditi;
Aditi indeed is this [earth];
this [earth] is the firm foundation;
on it indeed he finds a firm foundation.

There is a cake on eight potsherds for Agni, a caru for Soma, a cake on eight potsherds for Savitṛ, a caru for Bṛhaspati, a cake on eleven potsherds for Agni and Soma, a caru for Sarasvatī, a barley cake on ten potsherds for Varuṇa, [and] a cake on one potsherd for Heaven and Earth.
In that it is for Agni:
for the Brahmin belongs to Agni.
In that it is for Soma:
for the Brahmin belongs to Soma.
In that it is for Savitṛ:
that for Savitṛ is solely for instigation.
In that it is for Bṛhaspati:
Bṛhaspati is the brahman;
this is the Brahmin's lordship of speech.
In that it is for Agni and Soma:
for the Brahmin belongs to Agni and to Soma;
when these two unite, then he becomes vigorous.
In that it is for Sarasvatī:
Sarasvatī is speech;
this is the Brahmin's visible lordship of speech.
In that it is for Varuṇa:
that for Varuṇa is solely for freedom from Varuṇa;
for whoever he may be that is consecrated, he belongs to Varuṇa.
In that it is for Heaven and Earth:
Indra raised his thunderbolt against Vṛtra;
Heaven and Earth did not assent to him;
they assented to him with this share;
this is the approval of the thunderbolt;
[it is done] that he may be consecrated with approved thunderbolt.
In that there are eight oblations:
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
the gāyatrī is the brahman;
[it is done] that he may be consecrated with the brahman.
He is consecrated upon a black antelope skin;
for this is the form of the Ṛc and the Sāman;
in that there is a black antelope skin,
he is consecrated upon the Ṛc and the Sāman.
He purifies the ghee with gold,
solely for brilliance, for luster.
When he is consecrated with ghee,
he thereby attains pre-eminence.

There is a cake on eight potsherds for Agni, a caru for Pūṣan, a cake on eight potsherds for Savitṛ, a caru for the Viśve Devas, a cake on eight potsherds for Tvaṣṭṛ, a barley cake on ten potsherds for Varuṇa, a cake on seven potsherds for the Maruts, [and] a dappled barren cow for the Maruts.
In that it is for Agni:
that for Agni is for success;
for success has Agni at its head.
In that it is for Pūṣan:
Pūṣan is prosperity;
prosperity belongs to the Vaiśya.
In that it is for Savitṛ:
that for Savitṛ is solely for instigation.
In that it is for the Viśve Devas:
for the Vaiśya belongs to the Viśve Devas.
In that it is for Tvaṣṭṛ:
for Tvaṣṭṛ transforms forms.
In that it is for Varuṇa:
that for Varuṇa is solely for freedom from Varuṇa;
for whoever he may be that is consecrated, he belongs to Varuṇa.
In that it is for the Maruts:
for the Vaiśya belongs to the Maruts.
In that there are seven oblations:
for the Maruts are seven.
Then there is this dappled barren cow for the Maruts;
portions from it are cut together for Agni;
then he is consecrated.
The Maruts are the common people;
he is consecrated from the midst of the people;
therefore he is dear to the people;
for he is consecrated from the midst of the people.
He is consecrated upon a bull's hide;
for he is a procreator,
for procreation.
He is consecrated with curds;
curds are soft;
he makes him soft.

"We praise the greatness of Narāśaṃsa among these, of the adorable one, through sacrifices, / the gods of good resolve, pure, mindful, who relish both oblations."
He who sacrifices with Soma has a consecration of the gods;
he who sacrifices with an animal has a consecration of the gods;
he who sacrifices with an iṣṭi has a consecration of men.
The gods assented to this for Pṛthī;
with it they consecrated him;
he was consecrated also over wild animals.
As many creatures as there are that speak speech, over all of them is he consecrated who is consecrated with this.
Having made amends, he is consecrated with this Nārāśaṃsī verse;
for Narāśaṃsa is men.
Whatever in the Rājasūya does not have a high altar, all that is performed here.
Then there is this cake on twenty-one potsherds for the Maruts;
portions from it are cut together for Agni;
then he is consecrated.
Thrice seven are the Maruts;
the Maruts are the common people;
he is consecrated from the midst of the people;
therefore he is dear to the people;
for he is consecrated from the midst of the people.

"Let your milks gather together, let your vigorous powers gather, let your manly strengths gather, O vanquisher of foes! / Swelling for immortality, O Soma, place the highest glories in heaven!"
There is indeed no consecration of Soma with Soma;
for Soma is [already] consecrated;
for they kill Soma;
for what has been killed is not consecrated.
He is consecrated with a Triṣṭubh addressed to Soma,
for that is the consecration of Soma.
He is consecrated upon a barren cow that has calved, belonging to Soma;
for she is an emitter of semen, and Soma is semen.
Whatever else is in the Rājasūya other than the stomas, all that is performed here.
Then there is this cake on twenty-one potsherds for the Maruts;
from it a portion is cut off for Agni.
Then he is consecrated.
Thrice seven are the Maruts;
the Maruts are the people;
he is consecrated from the midst of the people themselves.
Therefore is he dear to the people;
for he is consecrated from the midst of the people.

What is the Rājasūya, that is the consecration of Varuṇa;
what is the Vājapeya, that is the consecration of the universal ruler.
Prajāpati is universal rule, Parameṣṭhin;
sovereignty is the cow itself;
that is this Gosava.
Just like a cow does he become who is consecrated with this.
He is consecrated with freshly milked milk;
for that is sovereignty.
The Bṛhat is the sāman;
for that is sovereignty.
Ten thousand is the sacrificial fee;
for that is sovereignty.
In the Pavamāna [lauds] they introduce the Kaṇvarathaṃtara.
To the south of the Āhavanīya, on [ground] that has not been dug up, he is consecrated.
This [earth] is the cow;
unseparated from her is he consecrated.
The stoma is thirty-six-fold;
this is indeed an animal-stoma;
therefore it is the Gosava.

The stoma is ninefold;
the ninefold is spiritual luster;
with spiritual luster he is consecrated.
The Rathaṃtara is the sāman;
the Rathaṃtara is spiritual luster;
with spiritual luster he is consecrated.
The Agniṣṭoma is the stoma;
the Agniṣṭoma is spiritual luster;
with spiritual luster he is consecrated.
The Pravargya is performed;
the Pravargya is spiritual luster;
with spiritual luster he is consecrated.
The Hotṛ wears a garland;
he is reddish, blinking, [having studied] the Triśukriya;
this is the visible form of spiritual luster;
by its visible form he secures spiritual luster.
This is indeed a single pressing, in that there is one stoma and one meter.
He should give eleven at each pressing,
for the differentiation of the pressings.
They amount to thirty-three;
thirty-three are the deities;
he secures the deities.
He gives a horse as the thirty-fourth;
Prajāpati is the thirty-fourth;
he reaches Prajāpati.
The gods did not have faith in Bṛhaspati;
Prajāpati caused him to sacrifice with this;
then they had faith in him.
That is this Bṛhaspatisava.
One who desires spiritual luster should sacrifice with this;
he becomes possessed of spiritual luster.
For him this is the rule:
all his offspring become Brahmins possessed of spiritual luster.

The stoma is fifteenfold;
the fifteenfold is vigor and virility;
with vigor and virility he is consecrated.
The Bṛhat is the sāman;
the Bṛhat is vigor and virility;
with vigor and virility he is consecrated.
The Ukthya is the stoma;
the Ukthas are vigor and virility;
with vigor and virility he is consecrated.
The stotra and the śastra belong to Indra, and the Pavamānas are in the Triṣṭubh meter;
the Triṣṭubh is vigor and virility;
with vigor and virility he is consecrated.
All adversaries, all destructions, all demons wished to slay Indra;
Prajāpati caused him to sacrifice with this;
he overcame all adversaries, all destructions, all demons.
Indra is the sacrificer;
in that he sacrifices with this,
he overcomes all adversaries, all destructions, all demons.
That is this Indrasava.

May Bodha and Pratībodha protect me from the east;
and may Asvapna and Anavadrāṇa protect me from the south;
and may Gopāyamāna and Rakṣamāṇa protect me from the west;
and may Jāgṛvi and Arundhatī protect me from the north.
Make us free from enemies below, free from enemies above;
O Indra, make us free from enemies behind, free from enemies in front.
May we accompany days free from enemies; O all-gods, free from enemies may dawns and sunsets be for us.
The six broad ones, the five intermediate directions, may they protect us;
O Mitradhā, establish us in a friend;
let there be fearlessness for us.
You who attend the night and who watch over beings,
who guard all cattle, watch over us in ourselves.
O Bodha, Pratībodha, Asvapna, Anavadrāṇa, Gopāyamāna, Rakṣamāṇa, Jāgṛvi, Arundhatī, you gods who are body-protectors, protect my body here.
'O Bodha, Pratībodha':
that sun, indeed, is Bodha;
Agni is Pratībodha.
'O Asvapna, Anavadrāṇa':
the moon, indeed, is Asvapna;
he who purifies himself is Anavadrāṇa.
'O Gopāyamāna, Rakṣamāṇa':
the day, indeed, is Gopāyamāna;
the night is Rakṣamāṇa.
'O Jāgṛvi, Arundhatī':
the sacrifice, indeed, is Jāgṛvi;
the sacrificial fee is Arundhatī.
These gods, indeed, are the realm-sustainers;
them indeed he praises,
for the protection of himself.

To the earth, svāhā!
To the intermediate space, svāhā!
To the sky, svāhā!
To desire, svāhā!
To sleep, svāhā!
To the day, svāhā!
To the night, svāhā!
Satisfied are you;
satisfied am I.
Satisfied is my out-breath; satisfied is my diffused breath; satisfied is my in-breath;
satisfied is my sight; satisfied is my hearing; satisfied is my speech; satisfied is my self.
Sky is my father; Earth is my mother;
Sun is my sight; Wind is my breath.
Unconquered by name am I; as such, I entrust this self of mine to Sky and Earth; upon them two I lean, upon them two in heroic stride.
Carry me across unto protection!
Do not harm me!
Protect [me] from the hater; protect from injury;
protect from ungodly defamation!
You are a stone, body-protecting;
as such, protect my body here.
In you is my vulnerable spot, in Bṛhaspati my breath; as such, protect me from death.
Upward speech, upward insight, upward faculty, upward offspring, upward cattle—may he reach this who threatens me from this direction.
May Indra and Agni protect me from this direction;
may the fathers protect me from this direction;
may Savitṛ protect me from this direction;
may Mitra and Varuṇa protect me from this direction;
may Bṛhaspati protect me from this direction.
Life-making, mistress of life, O one of independent power!
Our herdsmen are you, protectors;
may no one dig us out; may we not perish!

You sit in Ṛta;
you sit in Satya;
you sit in splendor;
you sit in luster;
you sit in glory;
you are praised;
you are a domain;
for the domain [I place] you;
for the domains [I place] you;
sit in the domains!
You are gain;
for gain may I gain you!
You are finding;
for finding may I find you!
You are work;
for work may I perform you!
You are power;
for power may I be able for you!
You are prosperity;
for prosperity may I prosper with you!
You are Gūrda;
for Gūrda [I place] you;
for the Gūrdas [I place] you;
sit in Gūrda!
Protect dominion;
make dominion swell;
quicken dominion;
uphold dominion!
You are dominion;
for dominion [I place] you;
for dominions [I place] you;
sit in dominion!
Among all earthly things I seat you;
among all atmospheric things I seat you;
among all heavenly things I seat you;
among all waters I seat you;
among all plants I seat you;
among all trees I seat you;
in all quarters I seat you;
in all intermediate quarters I seat you.
Sit in heaven!
You are winning the sun;
you are winning the battle-array;
you are winning abundance;
you are victorious;
you are all-winning;
you are winning everything;
you are winning simultaneously;
you are winning wealth;
you are shining;
you are shining out;
you are shining forth;
I seat you as the vajra that slays the rival;
I seat you as the vajra that slays the Rakṣases;
I seat you as the vajra that slays the Piśācas;
I seat you as the vajra that slays the Yātudhānas;
I seat you as the vajra that slays the niggards;
I seat you as the vajra that slays the adversaries;
you are slayer of the foe; I seat you as the Indra-vajra that slays the foe, slays the rival, slays the Asuras!

You are ascending;
you are rising;
you are mounting;
you are stepping forward;
you are stepping upon;
you are conquest;
you are stepping together;
you are crossing over;
you are transition;
you are heavenly;
you are heaven.
Sit in refreshment!
Sit in nourishment!
Sit in good fortune!
Sit in wealth!
Sit in prosperity!
Sit on the sacrificial [spot] of the earth!
Sit in the step of Viṣṇu!
Sit in the step of Iḍā!
Sit in the ghee-holding!
Sit in the overflowing!
Sit in the year (Saṃvatsara)!
Sit in the Parivatsara!
Sit in the Idāvatsara!
Sit in the Anuvatsara!
Sit in the Udvatsara!
Sit in the one!
Sit in the ten!
Sit in the hundred!
Sit in the thousand!
Sit in the ten thousand!
Sit in the hundred thousand!
Sit in the million!
Sit in the hundred million (arbuda)!
Sit in the thousand million (nyarbuda)!
Sit in the badva!
Sit in the ocean (samudra)!
Sit in the middle!
Sit at the end!
Sit in the highest number (parārdha)!
You are overflowing;
for the overflowing one [I place] you;
for the overflowing ones [I place] you;
sit in the overflowing!
You are Ṛta;
for Ṛta [I place] you;
for the Ṛtas [I place] you;
sit in Ṛta!
You are Satya;
for Satya [I place] you;
for the Satyas [I place] you;
sit in Satya!
You are the joint;
for the joint [I place] you;
for the joints [I place] you;
sit in the joints!
You are the union;
for the union [I place] you;
for the unions [I place] you;
sit in the unions!
You are fulfillment;
for fulfillment [I place] you;
for fulfillments [I place] you;
sit in fulfillments!

The eastern quarter;
the Gāyatrī meter;
the Rathantara Sāman;
Agni the deity;
spring the season;
the Trivṛt Stoma with the fifteenfold course;
age of three years the strength;
the Kṛta among the throws of dice;
the east wind the wind;
Sānaga the ṛṣi.
The southern quarter;
the Triṣṭubh meter;
the Bṛhat Sāman;
Indra the deity;
summer the season;
the Pañcadaśa Stoma with the seventeenfold course;
a two-and-a-half-year-old steer the strength;
the Tretā among the throws of dice;
the south wind the wind;
Pratna the ṛṣi.
The western quarter;
the Jagatī meter;
the Vairūpa Sāman;
the Maruts the deity;
the rains the season;
the Saptadaśa Stoma with the twenty-onefold course;
a three-year-old steer the strength;
the Dvāpara among the throws of dice;
the west wind the wind;
Sanātana the ṛṣi.
The northern quarter;
the Anuṣṭubh meter;
the Vairāja Sāman;
the Viśve Devas the deity;
autumn the season;
the Ekaviṃśa Stoma with the twenty-sevenfold course;
a four-year-old steer the strength;
the Āskanda among the throws of dice;
the north wind the wind;
Purāṇa the ṛṣi.
The upward quarter;
the Paṅkti meter;
the Śākvara and Raivata Sāmans;
Mitra and Varuṇa the deity;
winter and cool season the seasons;
the Triṇava Stoma with the thirty-threefold course;
a five-year-old ox the strength;
the Abhibhū among the throws of dice;
the wind from all directions the wind;
Suparṇa the ṛṣi.
Fathers, grandfathers, from higher to lower, may they protect us, may they help us,
in this Brahman, in this dominion, in this blessing, in this priestly office, in this invocation of the gods!
By that Brahman, by that meter, by that deity, like an Aṅgiras, sit firm!

Extend out in breath from life;
extend out through-breath from out-breath;
extend out in-breath from through-breath;
extend out sight from in-breath;
extend out hearing from sight;
extend out speech from hearing;
extend out the self from speech;
extend out the earth from the self;
extend out the intermediate space from the earth;
extend out heaven from the intermediate space;
extend out the sun from heaven.
The earth is the barren cow, the new moon the embryo, the plants the afterbirth, Agni the calf, the Agnihotra the colostrum.
The intermediate space is the barren cow, Dhātṛ the embryo, the Rudras the afterbirth, Vāyu the calf, the Gharma the colostrum.
Heaven is the barren cow, thunder the embryo, the constellations the afterbirth, Sūrya the calf, rain the colostrum.
The Ṛgveda is the barren cow, the Bṛhat and Rathantara the embryo, the summons and Nivids the afterbirth, the sacrifice the calf, the sacrificial fee the colostrum.
The commoners are the barren cow, the warrior is the embryo, the cattle are the afterbirth, the king is the calf, tribute is the colostrum.

You are the maker of increase;
you are the maker of wide space;
you face east;
you face upward;
you are the intermediate space.
Giver of splendor, I set you in splendor;
giver of luster, I set you in luster;
giver of milk, I set you in milk;
giver of fame, I set you in fame;
giver of wealth, I set you in wealth;
I set you in the wealth of the earth;
I set you in the wealth of the intermediate space;
I set you in the wealth of heaven;
I set you in the wealth of the quarters;
giver of wealth, I set you in wealth.
You sit in the waters;
you sit in the birds;
you sit in the falcon;
you sit in the vulture;
you sit in the eagle.
The goddess who you are, O brick, giving life, lying close beside [us],
lie close beside me, just as a wife [lies] ever beside her husband.
The goddess who you are, O brick, giving out-breath, giving through-breath, giving in-breath, giving sight, giving hearing, giving speech, giving the self, giving earth, giving intermediate space, giving heaven, giving the sun, giving maidens, giving wanton girls, giving women with their first head-ornament, giving young women, lying close beside [us],
lie close beside me, just as a wife [lies] ever beside her husband.

This indeed is she who shone forth first; entering into these she moves within.
The bride, the newly-calved mother, has lowed; three greatnesses attend upon her.
Possessing meters, the two dawns, adorned, move together along the common womb;
the two wives of the sun wander knowing, bearing signs, unaging, rich in seed.
Three have arrived following the path of Ṛta; three Gharmas have arrived following seed.
One protects the offspring, one the nourishment, one protects the dominion of the god-seeking.
She who was fourth established the four-stoma sacrifice, becoming the wings of the sacrifice, the ṛṣis;
Gāyatrī, Triṣṭubh, Jagatī, Virāj, yoking the solar chant, brought hither this sun.
When Dhātṛ arranged this [world] with five, he generated sisters for them, five by five;
and of them five go forth in succession, the intentions putting on various forms.
Along the five dawnings are the five milkings, the seasons five along the five-named cow;
the five quarters arranged by the fifteenfold [stoma], having a common summit toward one world.
Luminous, goddess Dawn puts on the cloud, according to the ordinance of Sūrya;
the cattle, being born manifold in form, look out from the lap of this mother.
Thirty sisters approach the appointed rendezvous, putting on a common sign;
the wise extend the seasons, knowing them; luminous, they go around in the midst of the meters.
With a bright bull, with cloud, with light she has come, all-formed, variegated, with the beacon of fire;
doing good work toward a common goal, bringing old age, you, unaging Dawn, have arrived.
The mistress of the seasons, this first one has arrived, leader of days, mother of offspring;
being one, O Dawn, shine forth manifold; unaged yourself, make everything else grow old.
First dawning at the domain of Ṛta, one bears the greatness of the waters;
one moves at the appointed places of Sūrya, one of the Gharma, one does Savitṛ hold fast.
The Ekāṣṭakā, practicing ascetic heat, brought forth the embryo, the greatness, Indra;
by him the gods overcame the Dasyus; by his powers he became the slayer of the Asuras.
‘You have made me younger who had no younger sister,’ speaking truth, follow this [demand];
may I be in his favor as you [are]; let not one of you drive past another!
Now the all-knowing one has been in our favor; he has reached a firm foundation, they have found a ford;
may I be in his favor as you [are]; let not one of you drive past another!
First she shone forth; she became a milch cow with Yama;
rich in milk, may she milk for us year after year!

With Agni, overcoming through Ṛta;
with intention, the lord of power;
with goodwill, heavenly;
with sacrifice, the generous one;
with fury, the slayer of Vṛtra;
with the bull, Tvaṣṭṛ;
with the Vaṣaṭ call, partaking of the half;
of the earth, the grass-blade;
with food, Gaya;
with the Sāman, possessed of a body;
with the Ṛg verses, eating food;
with ascetic heat, unassailable;
with the sun, sovereign ruler;
with the seasons he flourished;
with the year he encompassed all.
Both prevailing and to be prevailed over;
both advancing in strength and of high strength;
both of equal strength and of great strength;
both victorious and more powerful;
both overpowering and mighty.
Unto you, O hero, we shout praises;
for you alone do we invoke.

Hiraṇyagarbha arose in the beginning; born, he was the sole lord of what has come into being.
He sustained this earth and heaven: to which god shall we make offering with the oblation?
Who became king of what breathes and winks, the sole lord of the whole world;
who rules over this two-footed and four-footed: to which god shall we make offering with the oblation?
Who is the giver of spirit, the giver of strength; whose command all, whose command the gods attend;
whose shadow is immortality, whose shadow is death: to which god shall we make offering with the oblation?
By whom the mighty heaven and the earth were made firm, by whom the sun was propped up, by whom the firmament;
who measured out the wide midspace: to which god shall we make offering with the oblation?
Who supported these two, heaven and earth, propped up though trembling;
upon whom the sun, extended, travels: to which god shall we make offering with the oblation?
Whose are all these mountains by his greatness; whose, they say, is the ocean together with the Rasā;
whose regions are the five divine directions: to which god shall we make offering with the oblation?
When the great waters came over all, holding the embryo and generating Agni,
thence arose the one life-spirit of the gods: to which god shall we make offering with the oblation?
May Prajāpati generate offspring for us; may the Creator, benevolent, ordain [it];
and the Year, in harmony with the seasons; may the lord of nourishment bestow nourishment upon me.

With out-breath and in-breath as my yoked pair, I yoke you.
With the two butter-pourings as my yoked pair, I yoke you.
With Indra and Agni as my yoked pair, I yoke you.
With the meters, you;
with the stomas, you;
with the ukthas, you;
for fiery energy, you;
for splendor, you;
for wealth, you;
for nourishment, you; for leadership among my kinsmen, with me as your yoked pair, I yoke you.
O Agni, as a bull generating [you] again young, attentive, I have approached you.
May our household rites not run on one bull; sharpen us together with sharp sacred formula!
Those who attend on you, O Night, as the likeness of the year,
unite their long-lived offspring with wealth and nourishment!

I seat you upon the red clouds.
I seat you upon the ruddy clouds.
I seat you upon the black, upon the dark-blue, upon the dark clouds.
Ambā is your name;
Dulā is your name;
Nitatnī is your name;
Cupuṇīkā is your name;
Abhrayantī is your name;
Meghayantī is your name;
Varṣayantī is your name.
I seat you full of light.
I seat you light-maker.
I seat you light-finding.
I seat you blazing.
I seat you shining.
I seat you glimmering.
I seat you radiant.
I seat you of great light.
I seat you bright.
I seat you waking [others].
I seat you wakeful.
I seat you unceasing.
For impulsion, you;
for support, you;
for side-glance, you;
for the surging wave, you;
for the stream, you;
for the ocean, you;
for the lake, you;
for all-encompassing space, you;
for the supporter, you;
for wealth, you;
for good fortune, you;
for the intermediate space, you!

Father Mātariśvan has set down unbroken foot-tracks;
the unbroken Uśijas, the inspired sages, carved the tracks;
Soma the all-knower, the leader, will lead;
Bṛhaspati will recite the hymns of joy.
When you cried out…
Given by Yama, Trita yoked him; Indra first mounted him;
the Gandharva seized his rein; from the sun the Vasus fashioned the horse.
You are Yama, you are Āditya, O courser; you are Trita by secret ordinance;
you are thoroughly united with Soma; they say you have three bonds in heaven.
They say you have three bonds in heaven, three in the waters, three within the sea;
and to me you seem like Varuṇa, O courser, where they say is your highest birthplace.
These are your groomings, O prize-runner, these the placings of the hooves of Savitṛ;
here I have seized your auspicious testicles, which the guardians of truth watch over.
You are the Saṃvatsara;
you are the Parivatsara;
you are the Idāvatsara;
you are the Anuvatsara;
you are the Udvatsara.
At the impulsion of divine Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I seat you accompanied by Indra.
You are the cloak of the domain;
sit in your own womb, sit in your own womb of earth!
May the seasons be arranged for you!
May the months be arranged for you!
May the half-months be arranged for you!
May the days and nights be arranged for you!
May the limbs be arranged for you!
May the joints be arranged for you!
May the bodies be arranged for you!
Coming and going, bend together and stretch out!
Agni is light, light is Agni;
Indra is light, light is Indra;
the Sun is light, light is the Sun.
Suck this nourishing, swollen breast of the waters, O Agni, in the midst of the watery flood;
enjoy the spring, the honeyed wave; enter the ocean dwelling!

From the ocean the honeyed wave has arisen; together with the Upāṃśu cup it has attained immortality,
which is the secret name of ghee, the tongue of the gods, the navel of immortality.
Let us proclaim the name of ghee; in this sacrifice let us uphold it with homages;
may the Brahman priest listen to it being praised; the four-horned buffalo has bellowed this forth.
Four are his horns, three are his feet, two are his heads, seven are his hands;
bound in three ways, the bull bellows aloud; the great god has entered into mortals.
Placed in three ways, hidden by the Paṇis, the gods found the ghee within the cow;
Indra generated one, the Sun one; from Vena they carved out one through inherent power.
These flow from the ocean of the heart, having a hundred enclosures, not to be looked upon by the enemy;
I behold the streams of ghee; in their midst is a golden reed.
They flow together like rivers, like streams of milk, being purified inwardly with heart and mind;
these waves of ghee rush onward like deer fleeing from an archer.
Like swift ones on the course of a river, breaking wind, the young waters fly;
the streams of ghee swell with waves like a reddish prize-horse bursting through the course.
They stream forward toward Agni like lovely women smiling on their way to a festival;
the streams of ghee reach the kindling-logs; enjoying them, Jātavedas is pleased.
I look upon them as upon maidens anointing themselves with anointment to go to a bridal procession;
where the soma is pressed, where the sacrifice is, thither flow the streams of ghee.
Flow toward the praise-song, toward the contest for cattle; bestow auspicious riches upon us;
lead this sacrifice of ours to the gods; the streams of ghee flow sweet with honey.
Upon your seat the whole world is rested, within the ocean, within the heart, within life;
that honeyed wave of yours which was brought in the assembly, at the battle-line of waters—may we obtain that!
